window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jemas-2022"] = {"id": "jemas-2022", "title": "JEMAS(PG) 2022", "fullTitle": "JEMAS(PG) 2022, West Bengal, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "100 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence:", "options": {"a": "Fluid, Fixed.", "b": "Fixed, Fluid.", "c": "Fluid, Crystalized.", "d": "Crystalized, Fluid."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Cattell (1963) and his student John Horn (1966) split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, such as spotting the rule in a matrix puzzle or working out an unfamiliar gadget. It depends heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience. Because it keeps growing with use, it holds steady or even rises into old age. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) followed adults for decades and found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood, while perceptual speed declines from early adulthood. Timothy Salthouse (1996) traced much of the fluid loss to a general slowing of processing. Example: Riya's grandmother, aged 72, finishes the newspaper crossword before anyone else and knows every rare word (crystallised), but takes far longer than Riya to learn a new phone app (fluid). Option c is right because it puts the declining ability (fluid) in the first blank and the stable one (crystallised) in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: examiners reverse the order (option d) to catch students who remember the pair but not which one falls; think 'fluid flows away, crystals last'. They also invent a 'fixed' intelligence, which is not a type in any theory; it echoes Carol Dweck's (2006) fixed mindset, a belief about ability rather than a kind of ability.", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence in ageing"}, {"q": "Grief refers to:", "options": {"a": "The period of emotional adjustment that follows the death of a loved one.", "b": "The intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend, or relative.", "c": "The period during which a person seems dazed or numb and shows little emotion.", "d": "Demoralization and discouragement."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: grief is the intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend or relative. Three words are often mixed up. Bereavement is the objective state of having lost someone close through death: it describes your situation. Grief is the emotional response to that loss: sorrow, yearning, anger, guilt, anxiety and loneliness, often with bodily signs such as poor sleep and loss of appetite. Mourning is the period of adjustment and the culturally shaped rituals through which people express grief and adapt, such as a funeral, a set time of wearing white, or the thirteen days of rites many Hindu families observe. John Bowlby (1980) saw grief as the response to a broken attachment and described phases: numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, and reorganisation. Colin Murray Parkes (1972), studying London widows, described a similar course. Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) later replaced fixed phases with a dual process model, in which the bereaved move back and forth between facing the loss and rebuilding daily life. Example: when Arjun's father dies, Arjun is bereaved (his state); the heavy sadness and longing he feels is grief; the cremation, the prayers and the year of family observances make up mourning. Option b is right because it names the emotion itself, an intense emotional state, rather than a period, a phase or a general mood. Exam trap: option a sounds almost identical but describes a period of adjustment, which is mourning, not grief; option c describes only the first numb phase within grief.", "topic": "Grief, bereavement and mourning"}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because:", "options": {"a": "Each involves a crisis or dilemma.", "b": "Each signals a new stage of cognitive development.", "c": "Each is an expression of biological programming.", "d": "Their failure to appear is evidence of psychopathology."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson (1950), life stages matter because each one involves a crisis or dilemma. In Childhood and Society, Erikson described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage is defined by a psychosocial crisis: a turning point between two opposite tendencies, set off when a growing person meets new social demands. They run trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). 'Crisis' here means a choice of direction, not a disaster. A favourable resolution, tilted towards the positive pole, leaves the ego with a basic strength or virtue, such as hope from trust or fidelity from identity. An unresolved crisis makes later ones harder but not hopeless, because development continues across the whole lifespan. Erikson called the built-in sequence the epigenetic principle: the stages unfold on a timetable, but society shapes how each one turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician while his parents push medicine; he is working through identity versus role confusion. Option a is right because a crisis or dilemma is the defining feature of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: examiners offer cognitive development, which is the stage logic of Jean Piaget (1936), and biological programming, which fits the libido-driven psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud better (Three Essays 1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923); Erikson's stages are psychosocial, defined by social conflicts.", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial crises"}, {"q": "Elizabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of:", "options": {"a": "Eating disorders.", "b": "Adolescent behavior.", "c": "Coping with stress.", "d": "Death and dying."}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969) is known for her research on death and dying. She was a Swiss-born psychiatrist who interviewed terminally ill patients at a Chicago hospital. In her book On Death and Dying she proposed five stages that people often pass through after learning that their illness is terminal: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live to see my son's wedding'), depression (grieving the losses to come) and acceptance (a calm, often quiet readiness). The mnemonic is DABDA. Her work changed medical care: it encouraged doctors to talk honestly with dying patients and supported the hospice movement, which Cicely Saunders (1967) had begun with St Christopher's Hospice in London. Later research found that people do not move through the stages in a fixed order; they skip, repeat or mix them, so the stages are best read as common reactions rather than a timetable. The model was also stretched, with little evidence, to the grief of the bereaved. Example: when Meera, 58, is told her cancer has spread, she first insists the scans are wrong (denial), later snaps at the nurses (anger), and weeks later promises to give up sweets if she can see her granddaughter born (bargaining). Option d is right because her classic book and five-stage model are about how people face their own death. Exam trap: coping with stress (option c) is the near miss, because dying is a stressor; but stress and coping research belongs to Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), not Kubler-Ross.", "topic": "Kübler-Ross stages of dying"}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called:", "options": {"a": "Proactive memories.", "b": "Reactive memories.", "c": "Explicit memories.", "d": "Implicit memories."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: memories that operate outside conscious awareness are called implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) defined implicit memory as an influence of past experience on behaviour, feeling or thought without conscious recollection. Explicit memory is the opposite: you deliberately and consciously retrieve something, as when you recall a list or recognise a face, so it is tested by recall and recognition. Larry Squire (1992) used the parallel terms declarative (explicit) and nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Implicit memory includes procedural skills (typing, cycling), priming (a word seen earlier is completed faster on a word-fragment test even if you do not remember seeing it), classically conditioned responses and habituation. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) could not consciously remember new events after hippocampal surgery in 1953, yet Brenda Milner (1962) showed that he improved day by day at mirror drawing while insisting he had never done the task. So explicit memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, while implicit memory relies on other systems such as the striatum and cerebellum. Example: Arjun types his email password perfectly every morning without thinking, but if asked to say the characters aloud he has to mime typing to recover them; his fingers hold an implicit memory. Option d is right because working outside conscious awareness is the defining feature of implicit memory. Exam trap: options a and b borrow from interference theory, where proactive interference (old learning disrupts new) and retroactive interference (new disrupts old) cause forgetting; 'proactive memories' and 'reactive memories' are not memory systems at all.", "topic": "Implicit versus explicit memory"}, {"q": "______ is the study of the meaning of words and language.", "options": {"a": "Linguistics.", "b": "Encoding.", "c": "Semantics.", "d": "Syntax."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: semantics is the study of the meaning of words and language. Language can be analysed at several levels. Phonology studies the sound system (phonemes); morphology studies how words are built from morphemes, the smallest units of meaning; syntax studies the rules that combine words into sentences; semantics studies meaning, of words, phrases and whole sentences; and pragmatics studies how language is used in social context, such as knowing that 'Can you pass the salt?' is a request, not a question about ability. Semantics covers word meanings, synonyms and opposites, ambiguity, and how the meaning of a sentence is built from its parts. Noam Chomsky (1957) showed that syntax and meaning can come apart with his sentence 'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously', which is grammatically perfect but meaningless. Charles Hockett (1960) listed semanticity, the link between symbols and the things they stand for, among the design features of human language. The same root appears in memory research: Endel Tulving's (1972) semantic memory is our store of facts and word meanings. Example: Riya reads 'the boat drifted to the bank' and 'she deposited cash at the bank' and picks the right sense of 'bank' each time; that skill is semantic. Option c is right because the stem asks specifically about meaning, and meaning is what semantics studies. Exam trap: linguistics (option a) is the broad scientific study of all aspects of language, of which semantics is only one branch, so it is too wide; syntax (option d) is grammar, the rules of word order, not meaning.", "topic": "Semantics and levels of language"}, {"q": "Basic speech sounds are called:", "options": {"a": "Morphemes.", "b": "Syllables.", "c": "Phonemes.", "d": "Syntax."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the basic speech sounds of a language are called phonemes. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, although it carries no meaning of its own. Swap /p/ for /b/ and 'pat' becomes 'bat'; swap /k/ for /h/ and 'cat' becomes 'hat'. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and each language uses its own set, so two sounds that are separate phonemes in one language may be heard as one sound in another. Hindi, for instance, separates aspirated /kh/ from plain /k/, so 'kaana' (one-eyed) and 'khaana' (food) are different words, while English speakers barely notice the puff of air. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units of meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness) and 'cats' has two (cat and the plural -s). Morphemes build words, words build sentences by the rules of syntax, and the meaning of the whole is studied by semantics. Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984) found that English-learning infants could tell apart Hindi dental and retroflex 't' sounds at 6 to 8 months but had mostly lost this by 10 to 12 months, as their ears tuned to the phonemes of their own language. Example: Meera's cousin from London cannot hear the difference between 'kaana' and 'khaana', while Meera hears two quite separate words. Option c is right because the stem asks for the basic units of speech sound, which is the definition of a phoneme. Exam trap: morpheme (option a) is the classic near miss; the morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning, the phoneme the smallest unit of sound.", "topic": "Phonemes and morphemes"}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "options": {"a": "Trait; Humanistic.", "b": "Psychoanalytic; Behaviorist.", "c": "Psychoanalytic; Humanistic.", "d": "Trait; Behaviorist."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "topic": "Unconscious across personality approaches"}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of:", "options": {"a": "Negative reinforcement.", "b": "Punishment.", "c": "Positive reinforcement.", "d": "Secondary reinforcement."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both forms of punishment. In operant conditioning, Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) experiments showed that consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Two questions classify every consequence. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Here positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour: a scolding, a slap, extra homework. Negative punishment removes something pleasant: time-out from play, a fine (response cost), losing phone time. Both make the behaviour less likely, so both are punishment. By contrast, positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, money), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet, the seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up); both make the behaviour more likely. Skinner argued that punishment suppresses behaviour only temporarily, teaches nothing about what to do instead and can breed fear and aggression, so reinforcing a better behaviour usually works better. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him when he hits his sister (positive punishment) and also takes away his cricket bat for a day (negative punishment); both aim to reduce hitting. Option b is right because adding an aversive stimulus and removing a pleasant one are the two forms of punishment. Exam trap: negative reinforcement (option a) is the classic confusion; it removes something unpleasant and so increases behaviour, the reverse of punishment.", "topic": "Positive and negative punishment"}, {"q": "Homophobia is:", "options": {"a": "Fear of blood.", "b": "Prejudice against and dislike of gays and lesbians.", "c": "A capacity for erotic attraction to members of the same sex.", "d": "None."}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: homophobia is prejudice against, and dislike of, gay men and lesbians. The psychologist George Weinberg (1972), in Society and the Healthy Homosexual, popularised the term for an irrational dread of and hostility towards homosexual people. Despite the suffix, it is not a clinical phobia like a fear of heights; it is an attitude with the three parts every prejudice has: a negative belief (stereotype), a negative feeling (prejudice) and unfair action (discrimination, from jokes and exclusion to violence). Gregory Herek (2000) preferred the term sexual prejudice, and the related word heterosexism names the cultural assumption that heterosexuality is the only normal form. Facing such prejudice causes minority stress (Ilan Meyer, 2003), which helps explain higher rates of depression and anxiety in sexual minorities, while homosexuality itself was removed from the DSM in 1973. In India, the Supreme Court in Navtej Singh Johar v Union of India (2018) decriminalised consensual same-sex relations between adults by reading down Section 377. Example: Arjun's colleague refuses to sit near a gay co-worker and mocks him in meetings; that is homophobia shown as discrimination. Option b is right because homophobia names a prejudice and dislike, not a fear of blood or a sexual orientation. Exam trap: option c defines homosexuality (same-sex attraction), not homophobia, and option a plays on a look-alike Greek root, since fear of blood is haemophobia, from haima (blood), while homo comes from homos (same).", "topic": "Homophobia as sexual prejudice"}, {"q": "Which of the statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "options": {"a": "CBT and sertraline are not significantly different in terms of efficacy.", "b": "CBT improves outcome in around 60% of cases.", "c": "CBT and sertraline combined produce the best outcome.", "d": "All of these."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the general findings on treating childhood anxiety. The landmark trial is the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS) of John Walkup and colleagues (2008), published in the New England Journal of Medicine. It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT, based on Philip Kendall's (1990) Coping Cat programme), the SSRI sertraline, the two combined, or a pill placebo. Rated much or very much improved were 80.7% with the combination, 59.7% with CBT, 54.9% with sertraline and 23.7% with placebo. Three findings follow. CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other (statement a). CBT helped about 60% of children (statement b). The combination beat both single treatments (statement c). Philip Kendall's own randomised trial (1994) had already shown that CBT, which teaches children to recognise anxious feelings and thoughts, use coping plans and then face feared situations through graded exposure, helps roughly two thirds of anxious children. Clinical practice therefore usually offers CBT first and considers adding an SSRI for moderate to severe anxiety or when CBT alone is not enough. Example: Meera, 11, panics before school and cannot sleep away from her mother; a CAMS-style plan might start weekly CBT and add sertraline if she stays stuck. Option d is right because each of the three statements matches the CAMS results. Exam trap: students who know that the combination won may wrongly reject statement a, assuming the two single treatments must also differ; in CAMS they did not.", "topic": "Treating childhood anxiety: CAMS"}, {"q": "Which of the following defines Beck's cognitive triad?", "options": {"a": "Negative beliefs about self, world and future.", "b": "Negative beliefs about self, world and others.", "c": "Negative beliefs about self, others and future.", "d": "Negative beliefs about present, past, and future."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the cognitive triad of Aaron Beck (1967) is a set of negative beliefs about the self, the world and the future. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' dreams and thoughts were full of automatic negative themes about loss and failure rather than hidden anger turned inwards. In his 1967 book on depression and in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues), he proposed that depression rests on three linked negative views. The self: 'I am worthless, unlovable, a failure.' The world, or current experience: 'Everything is against me; no one helps.' The future: 'It will never get better.' These views flow from dysfunctional schemas laid down by early experience and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. Hopelessness, the negative view of the future, is the corner most closely tied to suicide, which Beck measured with his Hopelessness Scale (1974). Cognitive therapy targets the triad through collaborative empiricism: client and therapist treat each belief as a hypothesis, test it with thought records and behavioural experiments, and build more balanced alternatives. Example: after failing one exam, Arjun thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'my teachers are unfair and nobody cares' (world) and 'I will never get a job' (future). Option a is right because it lists all three corners correctly: self, world and future. Exam trap: examiners swap one corner for a near miss, putting 'others' in place of the world or the future, or substituting a time sequence (past, present, future); the mnemonic is self, world, future.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad"}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "options": {"a": "'Eccentric' behaviour marked by odd patterns of thinking and communication.", "b": "An enduring pattern of instability in personal relationships.", "c": "A lack of a well-defined and stable self-image.", "d": "Regular and unpredictable changes in mood."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder features"}, {"q": "Which of these is not an information provided in DSM-IV-TR?", "options": {"a": "Essential features of the disorder.", "b": "Associated features.", "c": "A list of symptoms that must be present for diagnosis.", "d": "Information on treatment options."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: DSM-IV-TR does not give information on treatment options. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Since DSM-III, directed by Robert Spitzer (1980), the manual has been descriptive and atheoretical: it says what a disorder looks like and how to diagnose it reliably, stays neutral about causes and is silent on treatment. DSM-IV-TR (2000) is a text revision of DSM-IV (1994): the criteria stayed almost the same, but the descriptive text was updated. For each disorder the text covers diagnostic features (the essential features), subtypes and specifiers, recording procedures, associated features and disorders (including laboratory findings), specific culture, age and gender features, prevalence, course, familial pattern and differential diagnosis, followed by the diagnostic criteria, the list of symptoms that must be present and for how long. DSM-IV-TR also used five axes: clinical disorders; personality disorders and mental retardation; general medical conditions; psychosocial and environmental problems; and the Global Assessment of Functioning. Treatment guidance lives elsewhere, in the American Psychiatric Association's separate practice guidelines, in national guidelines and in textbooks. Example: a clinical psychologist, Kabir, uses DSM-IV-TR to confirm that his client meets the criteria for a major depressive episode and to rule out bipolar disorder, but turns to a practice guideline to choose between CBT and an antidepressant. Option d is right because the manual is a diagnostic and descriptive tool, not a treatment manual. Exam trap: the size of the book and the word 'Statistical' tempt students to think it covers everything about a disorder; it covers diagnosis and description, never therapy.", "topic": "Contents of DSM-IV-TR"}, {"q": "Lifetime prevalence rate for OCD is:", "options": {"a": "Around 2.5%.", "b": "Around 1.5%.", "c": "Around 4.5%.", "d": "Around 1%."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the lifetime prevalence of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is around 2.5%. OCD is defined by obsessions, recurrent intrusive and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety (fear of germs, doubts about locking the door, taboo images), and/or compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, praying) done to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event. It must be time-consuming (more than an hour a day) or cause marked distress or impairment. The classic figure comes from the Epidemiologic Catchment Area (ECA) study of the 1980s, which interviewed more than 18,000 adults at five US sites with a structured diagnostic interview. Marvin Karno and colleagues (1988) reported a lifetime OCD prevalence of about 2.5%, far higher than clinicians had assumed, since many sufferers hide their rituals. Lifetime prevalence is the proportion of people who have had the disorder at any point in their life; 12-month prevalence counts only recent cases and is therefore lower. OCD usually begins in late adolescence or early adulthood, earlier in males. Example: Arjun, 20, checks the gas knob 30 times before leaving home and is late for college every day; he is one of the 2 to 3 people in every 100 who will have OCD at some point. Option a is right because about 2.5% is the standard lifetime figure. Exam trap: 'around 1%' (option d) is close to the 12-month figure, so it catches students who mix up lifetime and 12-month prevalence. Key note: the later National Comorbidity Survey Replication (Ayelet Meron Ruscio and colleagues, 2010) gives about 2.3%, and DSM-5 gives a 12-month figure of 1.2% (1.1% to 1.8% internationally), so a '1%' option is a 12-month trap; around 2.5% stands.", "topic": "Lifetime prevalence of OCD"}, {"q": "Which of these has been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence?", "options": {"a": "Criminal behaviour leading to a prison sentence.", "b": "Psychosis.", "c": "Antisocial personality disorder.", "d": "All of these."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: criminal behaviour leading to prison, psychosis and antisocial personality disorder have all been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence. Conduct disorder (CD) is a repetitive, persistent pattern of violating others' basic rights or major age-appropriate rules: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule violations such as truancy and running away. DSM requires 3 of 15 criteria in the past 12 months and separates a childhood-onset type (a symptom before 10) from an adolescent-onset type. The links run in three directions. First, antisocial personality disorder (ASPD): DSM requires evidence of conduct disorder before age 15 for an adult ASPD diagnosis, and Lee Robins (1966), in Deviant Children Grown Up, found that adult antisocial personality almost never appeared without childhood conduct problems. Second, crime: Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated a rare life-course-persistent pattern, starting in early childhood and continuing into adult offending and imprisonment, from a common adolescence-limited pattern that fades in early adulthood. Third, psychosis: birth-cohort studies such as the Dunedin study in New Zealand found that children with conduct problems had higher rates of adult psychotic and schizophreniform disorders, so CD is a broad risk marker, not only a route to crime. Example: Kabir, 14, has been setting fires, stealing and bullying since age 9; his risk of adult offending, of ASPD and even of later psychosis is raised. Option d is right because each of the three outcomes has research support. Exam trap: students know the conduct disorder to ASPD link and pick c, overlooking the evidence for imprisonment and psychosis.", "topic": "Outcomes of adolescent conduct disorder"}, {"q": "In early Alzheimer's Disease, which is NOT a common symptom:", "options": {"a": "Anomia.", "b": "Hallucination.", "c": "Social withdrawal.", "d": "Anxiety."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: hallucinations are not a common symptom of early Alzheimer's disease. Alzheimer's disease, described by Alois Alzheimer (1906) in his patient Auguste Deter, is the commonest cause of dementia. It begins insidiously and progresses gradually as amyloid plaques (outside neurons) and tau tangles (inside neurons) spread from the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus to the wider cortex. That spread explains the order of symptoms. Early on, recent episodic memory fails: repeating questions, misplacing things, forgetting recent conversations. Word-finding difficulty (anomia) appears early too, along with mild problems in planning and finding one's way. Mood and behaviour change early as well: apathy, social withdrawal from hobbies and gatherings, depression and anxiety, often because the person senses they are slipping. Hallucinations and delusions (such as believing that someone is stealing misplaced items) usually come in the moderate to late stages. When vivid, well-formed visual hallucinations appear early, together with fluctuating attention and parkinsonism, clinicians suspect dementia with Lewy bodies instead. Example: Meera's grandfather, 74, keeps asking what day it is, struggles to name everyday objects ('the thing you cut with'), stops going to his evening walking group and seems anxious; that is a typical early Alzheimer's picture. Option b is right because hallucinations are a late feature of Alzheimer's disease and an early feature of a different dementia. Exam trap: the stem asks what is NOT common, and anomia sounds too technical to be an early sign, so students pick it; but word-finding difficulty is one of the first language symptoms of Alzheimer's disease.", "topic": "Early signs of Alzheimer's disease"}, {"q": "Which of these has NOT been associated with psychosis?", "options": {"a": "Birth complications.", "b": "Maternal stress.", "c": "Child abuse.", "d": "Large families."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: large family size has not been established as a risk factor for psychosis. Modern thinking about psychosis uses a diathesis-stress, or neurodevelopmental, model: genes and early brain development create a vulnerability, and later stresses push a vulnerable person into illness. Robin Murray and Shon Lewis (1987) argued that an early brain insult can stay silent until adolescent brain maturation unmasks it. Several early and environmental factors are well supported. Obstetric or birth complications, such as oxygen shortage at delivery, low birth weight and pre-eclampsia, modestly raise the risk. Maternal stress, infection (for example influenza) and malnutrition during pregnancy are linked to later schizophrenia in the child. Childhood adversity, especially sexual and physical abuse, bullying and neglect, raises the risk of psychotic experiences and disorder in a dose-response fashion: the more adversity, the higher the risk. Urban birth and upbringing, migration and heavy adolescent cannabis use add risk too. Family size, by contrast, has shown no consistent effect: studies of sibship size and birth order give mixed results, and it is not listed among the established risk factors. Example: Riya's psychiatrist asks about complications at her birth, her mother's health in pregnancy, any childhood trauma and her cannabis use, but not how many siblings she has. Option d is right because it is the only option without a consistent research link to psychosis. Exam trap: because family theories such as Frieda Fromm-Reichmann's (1948) 'schizophrenogenic mother' once blamed the family, students may think family structure matters; those family-blame theories are discredited, and family size has no established role.", "topic": "Risk factors for psychosis"}, {"q": "Which one of these statements is TRUE?", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia makes people violent.", "b": "If you hear voices or hold delusional beliefs you have schizophrenia.", "c": "Siblings of people who are diagnosed with schizophrenia are more likely to suffer mental health problems in their lifetime.", "d": "People with a diagnosis of schizophrenia are more likely to commit murder than their non-schizophrenic peer group."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: siblings of people diagnosed with schizophrenia are more likely to have mental health problems in their lifetime. Schizophrenia runs in families because risk is partly genetic and polygenic, carried by many common gene variants of small effect. Irving Gottesman (1991) pooled about 40 family and twin studies: lifetime risk is about 1% in the general population, about 9% for a sibling, about 13% for a child of one affected parent and about 48% for an identical co-twin. These genes overlap with those for bipolar disorder, depression and autism, and siblings also share family stress, so first-degree relatives show raised rates of several disorders, not only schizophrenia. The other statements are popular myths. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent and are far more often victims of violence than perpetrators. Hearing voices or holding odd beliefs does not by itself mean schizophrenia: Marius Romme and Sandra Escher (1989) showed that many voice-hearers in the general population cope well and never seek help, and hallucinations also occur in bereavement, post-traumatic stress disorder, bipolar disorder, severe depression, drug use and on falling asleep. Example: Arjun's elder brother has schizophrenia; Arjun's own lifetime risk of schizophrenia is several times the average, and his risk of depression is also higher, yet most siblings never develop schizophrenia. Option c is right because family and twin studies consistently show raised risk in relatives. Exam trap: options a and d play on media stereotypes, and b equates a symptom with a diagnosis. Key note: c is the clearly supported statement, though d is not baseless: meta-analyses such as Seena Fazel and colleagues (2009) find a raised relative risk of violent crime, including homicide, in schizophrenia, mostly explained by co-occurring substance misuse. The absolute risk is tiny and textbooks treat the blanket claim as a myth, so c stands.", "topic": "Myths and genetics of schizophrenia"}, {"q": "Most current Alzheimer treatments work by altering levels of which neurotransmitter?", "options": {"a": "Dopamine.", "b": "GABA.", "c": "Noradrenaline.", "d": "Acetylcholine."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: most current Alzheimer treatments work by raising levels of acetylcholine. The cholinergic hypothesis, set out by Raymond Bartus and colleagues (1982), grew from the finding that Alzheimer brains lose cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, at the base of the forebrain, which send acetylcholine to the cortex and hippocampus. Acetylcholine is vital for attention, learning and memory, so its loss fits the memory decline. Normally the enzyme acetylcholinesterase breaks acetylcholine down in the synapse soon after release. Cholinesterase inhibitors block that enzyme, so acetylcholine lingers longer and signals more strongly. Three are in wide use: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. They modestly improve or steady symptoms for a time in mild to moderate disease, but they do not stop the underlying plaques and tangles. The exception is memantine, used in moderate to severe disease, which blocks NMDA glutamate receptors and so protects neurons from excess glutamate stimulation. Newer antibody drugs that clear amyloid act on the disease process itself rather than on any transmitter. Example: Kabir's grandmother is diagnosed with mild Alzheimer's disease and started on donepezil; her neurologist explains that the tablet keeps more acetylcholine available at her synapses. Option d is right because the main drug class for Alzheimer's disease targets acetylcholine. Exam trap: dopamine (option a) is the transmitter targeted in Parkinson's disease (L-dopa) and schizophrenia, so students who mix up the neurodegenerative diseases pick it; GABA loss marks Huntington's disease.", "topic": "Cholinergic treatment of Alzheimer's"}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Binet and Simon.", "b": "Spearman.", "c": "Stanford and Binet.", "d": "Terman and Wechsler."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the first intelligence test was developed by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905). In 1904 the French Ministry of Education asked Alfred Binet to find a way to identify children who could not keep up in ordinary classes and needed special schooling. Earlier attempts, such as Francis Galton's (1884) anthropometric laboratory in London and James McKeen Cattell's (1890) 'mental tests', measured reaction time and sensory sharpness, and they failed to predict school success. Binet and the physician Simon took a different route, measuring higher mental processes: judgement, comprehension, reasoning and memory. Their 1905 Binet-Simon scale had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, giving the concept of mental age, and the 1911 revision extended it towards adults. Binet insisted the score was a practical guide for helping children, not a fixed measure of inborn ability. Later, Lewis Terman (1916) adapted it at Stanford University as the Stanford-Binet, using William Stern's (1912) intelligence quotient. Example: a teacher in Kolkata worried about Meera, aged 9, could use a Binet-style scale to find that she passes the items typical of 7-year-olds, a mental age of 7, signalling that she needs extra support. Option a is right because the 1905 Binet-Simon scale is recognised as the first practical intelligence test. Exam trap: option c sounds right because the Stanford-Binet is the most famous descendant, but 'Stanford' is a university, not a person, and Terman's revision came eleven years later.", "topic": "Binet-Simon: the first intelligence test"}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of:", "options": {"a": "Delinquency as a factor affecting the development of intelligence.", "b": "Mental age versus chronological age.", "c": "A z-score.", "d": "Verbal versus performance scores."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the z-score. A z-score tells how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies above or below the mean of a group: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The older ratio IQ, from William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), was mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It worked for children but failed for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a perfectly ordinary adult's IQ would fall with every birthday. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, replaced it with the deviation IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the mean and SD of his or her own age group in a large norm sample, turned into a z-score, and then rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. So scoring exactly at your age group's mean gives 100 at any age, one SD above (z = +1) gives 115, and one SD below gives 85. Because the deviation IQ is a standard score on the normal curve, it converts directly to a percentile: 115 is about the 84th percentile, 130 about the 98th. Modern Wechsler tests and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition all use it. Example: Arjun, 30, scores 12 points above the mean for his age group on a test whose SD at that age is 6; his z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 30 = 130. Option c is right because the deviation IQ is a z-score rescaled to mean 100 and SD 15. Exam trap: option b describes the ratio IQ that the deviation IQ replaced, so students who remember 'mental age' pick the wrong formula.", "topic": "Deviation IQ as a z-score"}, {"q": "Projective tests originated from:", "options": {"a": "Freud's idea that all behaviour was caused by unconscious motivational effects.", "b": "Jung's theory of psychological types.", "c": "The attempt to develop tests that did not rely on language.", "d": "Accidentally spilling ink on a test booklet."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: projective tests originated from the idea of Sigmund Freud (1896) that behaviour is driven by unconscious motives. In his early papers Freud described projection as a defence in which a person attributes his or her own unacceptable feelings or wishes to others. More broadly, psychoanalysis held that unconscious motives shape everything we do, including how we see ambiguous things. Lawrence Frank (1939) turned this into the projective hypothesis and coined the term 'projective methods': when people meet an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus with minimal instructions, they impose their own needs, conflicts and ways of seeing on it, revealing what they could not or would not report directly. Hence the tests: Hermann Rorschach's (1921) ten inkblots, Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray's (1935) Thematic Apperception Test of ambiguous pictures, sentence completion and drawing tests. Rorschach did not stumble on his blots; he deliberately designed and selected them, perhaps inspired by klecksography, a popular game of making pictures from blots. Projective tests are prized for clinical richness but criticised for weak reliability and validity; John Exner's (1974) Comprehensive System was a major attempt to standardise Rorschach scoring. Example: shown a picture of a boy staring at a violin, Riya tells a story about a child forced to practise by a harsh father; the clinician wonders whether she is projecting her own feelings about pressure at home. Option a is right because the logic of projection and hidden motives comes from Freud's psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung is a tempting near miss because his word association test (from 1904) was an early projective-style method, but option b names his theory of psychological types, which led to the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, an objective self-report inventory.", "topic": "Origins of projective tests"}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of +1?", "options": {"a": "50 Percent.", "b": "16 Percent.", "c": "25 Percent.", "d": "10 Percent."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "topic": "Normal curve areas above z"}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "options": {"a": "Verbal fluency.", "b": "Visual perception.", "c": "Memory.", "d": "Attention."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "topic": "Stroop test and attention"}, {"q": "A nerve cell carrying information from your eyes, ears, fingers, etc. toward the CNS is called a(n):", "options": {"a": "Motor neuron.", "b": "Effector cell.", "c": "Connector neuron.", "d": "Sensory neuron."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: a nerve cell that carries information from the eyes, ears, skin and other receptors towards the central nervous system (CNS) is a sensory neuron. Neurons fall into three functional classes. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry signals inwards, from receptors to the brain and spinal cord. Motor (efferent) neurons carry commands outwards, from the CNS to muscles and glands. Interneurons (also called connector or association neurons) lie wholly inside the CNS and link the two; they are by far the commonest kind. The muscles and glands that finally act are effectors, which are targets, not neurons at all. Charles Sherrington (1906) showed how these pieces join in the reflex arc. Suppose Meera touches a hot pan: receptors in her fingertip fire, sensory neurons carry the message through the dorsal root into the spinal cord, an interneuron relays it, and a motor neuron makes her arm muscles pull the hand away before she even feels the pain. A handy memory aid is SAME: Sensory Afferent, Motor Efferent; afferent arrives, efferent exits. Option d is right because the stem describes the inward direction of travel, from sense organs to the CNS, and that direction defines a sensory neuron. Exam trap: examiners swap the direction, so check whether the signal goes towards the CNS (sensory) or away from it (motor), and remember that 'effector' names the target organ, not a nerve cell.", "topic": "Sensory, motor and interneurons"}, {"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the:", "options": {"a": "Somatic nervous system.", "b": "Central nervous system.", "c": "Autonomic nervous system.", "d": "Cerebral cortex."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event, such as a pounding heart, fast breathing, sweating and a dry mouth, are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the internal organs (heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut and glands) largely without conscious control. It has two branches that work in opposite directions. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for emergencies, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: it raises heart rate and blood pressure, widens the pupils, slows digestion and makes the adrenal medulla release adrenaline. The parasympathetic branch calms the body afterwards (rest and digest), slowing the heart and restarting digestion. Imagine Arjun in a road accident: within seconds his heart races, his palms sweat and his stomach knots, all sympathetic effects, and an hour later his parasympathetic branch brings him back to baseline. The hypothalamus and amygdala in the brain trigger these changes, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. The polygraph (lie detector) works by recording exactly these autonomic changes. Option c is right because the stem asks which system most directly controls the emotional reaction, and the involuntary arousal of fear and stress is autonomic. Exam trap: the somatic nervous system sounds bodily, but it carries sensation and controls the voluntary skeletal muscles; the gut-level, involuntary arousal of emotion belongs to the autonomic system.", "topic": "Autonomic system and emotion"}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "options": {"a": "Heart stoppage or respiratory failure.", "b": "A loss of hearing ability.", "c": "A loss of muscular coordination.", "d": "Reduction in emotional response."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most likely causes a loss of muscular coordination. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the brain, beneath the occipital lobes and behind the pons, as part of the hindbrain. It does not start movements; the motor cortex does that. Instead it compares the movement you intended with the movement actually happening, using feedback from muscles, joints and the balance organs of the inner ear, and smooths out the errors. It also stores well-practised motor skills and timing, such as cycling or typing, and takes part in simple conditioning such as the eyeblink reflex. Damage produces ataxia: an unsteady, wide-based, staggering walk, poor balance, overshooting when reaching for an object (dysmetria), a tremor that grows as the hand nears its target (intention tremor) and slurred speech. Damage on one side affects the same side of the body. Alcohol depresses the cerebellum early, which is why roadside sobriety tests ask a driver to walk a straight line or touch the nose. For example, after a cerebellar stroke Kabir may know exactly what he wants to do and have full strength, yet he sways when he walks and spills tea when he lifts a cup. Option c is right because coordination, balance and the smooth timing of movement are the cerebellum's core jobs. Exam trap: heart and breathing failure tempts students because the cerebellum lies close to the brainstem, but those vital centres sit in the medulla oblongata.", "topic": "Cerebellum and coordination"}, {"q": "An elderly acquaintance of yours has suffered from partial blindness since she had a stroke. Apparently the stroke damaged her:", "options": {"a": "Occipital lobe.", "b": "Parietal lobe.", "c": "Temporal lobes.", "d": "Reticular formation."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: partial blindness after a stroke points to damage in the occipital lobe. The occipital lobe, at the back of the cerebral cortex, holds the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) around the calcarine fissure. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve, partly cross at the optic chiasm, relay in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus and arrive in V1. Because of this crossing, each occipital lobe sees the opposite half of the visual field from both eyes. A stroke in the posterior cerebral artery, which feeds the occipital lobe, therefore often leaves a person blind in the same half of the field in both eyes (contralateral homonymous hemianopia), or with smaller blind islands (scotomas), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Damage to both occipital lobes can produce cortical blindness. Beyond V1, visual information flows along two streams: a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe (Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin, 1982). Suppose Meera's grandmother can read the right half of a newspaper line but keeps missing the words on the left; a right occipital stroke would explain it. Option a is right because partial loss of sight with intact eyes means the visual cortex is damaged, and the visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: parietal damage can make a person ignore the left side of space (hemispatial neglect), which looks like blindness but is a failure of attention, not of seeing.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision"}, {"q": "The technique that uses a magnetic field to develop an image of the brain is called the:", "options": {"a": "Clinical scan.", "b": "Ablation technique.", "c": "MRI scan.", "d": "EEG."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the technique that uses a magnetic field to build an image of the brain is magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). The person lies inside a powerful magnet that lines up the hydrogen nuclei (protons) in the body's water and fat. Pulses of radio waves knock them out of line; as they relax back they give off signals, and because grey matter, white matter and fluid relax at different rates, a computer turns the signals into detailed slices in any plane. Paul Lauterbur (1973) showed how to make such images, and he shared the 2003 Nobel Prize with Peter Mansfield. MRI uses no X-rays or radioactivity and has the best spatial resolution of the common scans, so it is the standard way to see brain structure, such as tumours, multiple sclerosis plaques or a shrunken hippocampus. Functional MRI (fMRI) tracks changes in blood oxygen (the BOLD signal) to show which areas are active during a task. Compare its look-alikes: computed tomography (CT), introduced by Godfrey Hounsfield in the early 1970s, builds slices from X-rays, and positron emission tomography (PET) follows a radioactive tracer. If Riya's neurologist wants a detailed view of a small lesion, she orders an MRI; if Riya has just hit her head and may be bleeding, a quick CT comes first. Option c is right because MRI is the only option that images the brain with a magnetic field. Exam trap: the EEG and the MRI are both 'brain tests', but the EEG records electrical activity through scalp electrodes and makes no picture of structure.", "topic": "Brain imaging: MRI"}, {"q": "Which of these is associated with conduct disorder?", "options": {"a": "Criminal behaviour leading to imprisonment.", "b": "Antisocial personality disorder.", "c": "Psychosis.", "d": "All of these."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: conduct disorder is associated with all three outcomes: criminal behaviour leading to imprisonment, antisocial personality disorder and psychosis. Conduct disorder (CD) is a childhood or adolescent pattern of repeatedly violating the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate rules. DSM-5 groups its 15 signs under four headings (aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious violations of rules such as truancy or running away) and requires at least 3 in the past year. Its long-term outcomes are well documented. First, CD is the gateway to antisocial personality disorder (ASPD): an adult cannot receive an ASPD diagnosis unless there is evidence of conduct disorder with onset before age 15. Lee Robins (1966), following child guidance clinic children into adulthood, found that serious childhood antisocial behaviour was the strongest predictor of adult antisocial personality. Second, Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated common, short-lived adolescence-limited offending from a life-course-persistent pattern that starts early with conduct problems and continues into adult crime, violence and imprisonment. Third, cohort studies such as the Dunedin study in New Zealand found that children with conduct problems were more likely to develop psychotic and schizophreniform disorders as adults. Imagine Arjun, who at 9 bullies classmates, hurts stray dogs and steals; without help, each of these three paths becomes more likely for him. This paper asks this same question twice, as Q16 and Q31, and the answer is the same both times. Option d is right because imprisonment, ASPD and later psychosis are each established outcomes of conduct disorder. Exam trap: students pick antisocial personality disorder alone because it is the best-known link, but the other two are also documented, so 'All of these' is the complete answer.", "topic": "Conduct disorder outcomes"}, {"q": "Where is Broca's area located?", "options": {"a": "Frontal Lobe.", "b": "Occipital Lobe.", "c": "Parietal Lobe.", "d": "Temporal Lobe."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: Broca's area lies in the frontal lobe, in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45) in most people, just in front of the part of the motor cortex that controls the face, tongue and lips. Paul Broca (1861) described a patient, Louis Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan' because it was nearly the only syllable he could say. Leborgne understood speech but could not produce it, and at autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left frontal lobe. This was the first strong evidence that a mental function sits in one region of the cortex and that language depends mainly on the left hemisphere. Damage causes Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech such as 'Walk... dog... park', with comprehension fairly well preserved. If Kabir has a left frontal stroke, he may understand his doctor's questions yet struggle to answer them. Option a is right because Broca's area is a speech production area in the frontal lobe. Exam trap: Wernicke's area (Carl Wernicke, 1874), the comprehension area, lies in the temporal lobe, and examiners swap the two names and their lobes.", "topic": "Broca's area location"}, {"q": "Afferent and efferent nerves are found in which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Sympathetic nervous system.", "b": "Para Sympathetic nervous system.", "c": "Somatic nervous system.", "d": "Central nervous system."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: afferent and efferent nerves are the two parts of the somatic nervous system. The nervous system is first split into the central nervous system (CNS: brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (PNS: all the nerves outside them). The PNS has two divisions. The somatic (voluntary) nervous system deals with the outside world. Its afferent (sensory) nerves carry signals from the skin, eyes, ears and muscles into the CNS, and its efferent (motor) nerves carry commands out to the skeletal muscles. The autonomic nervous system runs the internal organs and is itself split into sympathetic and parasympathetic branches. A simple memory aid: afferent arrives, efferent exits. When Riya feels a mosquito on her arm (an afferent signal in) and slaps it (an efferent command out to her arm muscles), she is using the somatic system. The 31 pairs of spinal nerves are mixed: sensory fibres enter through the dorsal root and motor fibres leave through the ventral root, the Bell-Magendie law (Charles Bell, 1811; Francois Magendie, 1822). Option c is right because the textbook diagram of the nervous system lists afferent and efferent nerves as the two branches of the somatic division. Exam trap: the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems are branches of the autonomic system, offered to tempt students who remember that the autonomic system also has inward and outward fibres. Key note: strictly the whole peripheral nervous system carries both kinds of fibre (the autonomic system has visceral afferents too), while inside the CNS bundles of axons are called tracts, not nerves. The somatic system is the textbook answer and stands.", "topic": "Somatic nervous system"}, {"q": "We usually avoid remembering something that is associated with fear or unpleasantness. This avoidance is termed as:", "options": {"a": "Suppression.", "b": "Repression.", "c": "Retrieval failure.", "d": "Forgetting."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the habit of avoiding memories that are linked with fear or unpleasantness is called repression. Sigmund Freud (1915), in his paper 'Repression', made it the cornerstone of psychoanalysis: the ego unconsciously and automatically blocks threatening memories, wishes and feelings from awareness, so the person is protected from anxiety. Because it happens outside awareness, the person does not know they are doing it; they simply cannot recall. Freud saw repression as the primary defence, with other defences acting as back-ups when it fails, and the loss of access it produces is often called motivated forgetting. The repressed material does not vanish: it can return in disguised form through dreams, slips of the tongue and neurotic symptoms. For example, Meera cannot remember anything about the day her father was rushed to hospital, though she clearly remembers the days before and after; she is not choosing to forget. Later laboratory work has shown that people can push unwanted memories out of mind, though whether true repression of trauma happens remains debated. Option b is right because the stem describes forgetting driven by the unpleasantness of the memory, which is the textbook definition of repression. Exam trap: suppression and repression differ in awareness; suppression is the conscious decision not to think about something, while repression is unconscious. Key note: suppression is the conscious, deliberate pushing away of a thought; the stem's habitual avoidance of remembering painful things is what textbooks call repression (motivated forgetting, Sigmund Freud), so b stands.", "topic": "Repression versus suppression"}, {"q": "______ glands secrete directly into the bloodstream.", "options": {"a": "Exocrine.", "b": "Endocrine.", "c": "Duct.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: endocrine glands secrete their products, called hormones, directly into the bloodstream. 'Endocrine' means 'secreting within'. Endocrine glands have no ducts (they are ductless glands); their hormones pass into the capillaries around them and travel in the blood to target cells anywhere in the body that carry the right receptors. Ernest Starling (1905) coined the word 'hormone', from a Greek word meaning 'to set in motion'. The main endocrine glands are the pituitary (often called the master gland, itself controlled by the hypothalamus), the thyroid, the parathyroids, the adrenals, the pancreas (its islets of Langerhans release insulin and glucagon), the pineal gland (melatonin) and the gonads (testes and ovaries). Exocrine glands, by contrast, release their secretions through ducts onto a surface or into a cavity: sweat, tear, salivary and digestive glands. The pancreas is both: its islets are endocrine and its digestive juice is exocrine. When Kabir is frightened before a viva, his adrenal glands pour adrenaline into his blood and within seconds his heart beats faster; here the endocrine system works alongside the sympathetic nervous system. Hormones act more slowly than nerve signals but their effects last longer. Option b is right because only endocrine glands release their secretions straight into the blood. Exam trap: 'duct glands' sounds like a separate category, but it is simply another name for exocrine glands, which by definition do not secrete into the bloodstream.", "topic": "Endocrine versus exocrine glands"}, {"q": "Which part of the brain is responsible for transferring short term memory to long term memory?", "options": {"a": "Cerebellum.", "b": "Hippocampus.", "c": "Amygdala.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the hippocampus is the brain structure that transfers (consolidates) new information from short-term into long-term memory. The hippocampus is a curved, seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system. It is not the permanent store; instead it binds the pieces of a new experience together and, over days to years, helps the cortex lay down a lasting copy, a process called consolidation. The classic evidence is the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Scoville removed most of both medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampi, to control H.M.'s epilepsy, and William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported the result. H.M. kept his intelligence, his short-term memory span and most of his older memories, but he could no longer form new long-term memories for facts and events (anterograde amnesia). He could still learn new motor skills such as mirror drawing without remembering the practice, showing that procedural memory relies on other structures. The hippocampus also builds maps of space; London taxi drivers who learned the city's streets were found to have a larger posterior hippocampus. If Riya's grandmother has early Alzheimer's disease, which strikes the hippocampus first, she can chat normally but forgets the conversation within minutes. Option b is right because consolidation from short-term to long-term memory depends on the hippocampus. Exam trap: the amygdala sits right next to the hippocampus and also affects memory, but its job is to add emotional strength (especially fear) to memories, not to transfer ordinary facts into long-term storage.", "topic": "Hippocampus and consolidation"}, {"q": "The term used to refer to the first two weeks of life is:", "options": {"a": "Infancy.", "b": "Neonatal stage.", "c": "Early childhood.", "d": "Preoperational stage."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Elizabeth Hurlock's lifespan scheme, the first two weeks of life are called infancy. Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), whose Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach is the standard text in Indian courses, divides life into ten periods: prenatal (conception to birth), infancy (birth to the end of the second week), babyhood (2 weeks to 2 years), early childhood (2 to 6 years), late childhood (6 to 10 or 12), puberty, adolescence, early adulthood, middle age and old age. Her infancy is short because it is the period of adjustment to life outside the womb: the baby must start breathing, feeding, controlling body temperature and eliminating on its own. She splits it into two parts: the partunate period, from birth until the umbilical cord is cut (about 15 to 30 minutes), and the neonate period, from then to the end of the second week. During these days the newborn often loses a little weight and then regains it; once the adjustments are made, babyhood begins. In everyday English 'infancy' means roughly the first year or two, which Hurlock calls babyhood, so the word is easy to misread. If baby Arjun is ten days old, Hurlock would place him in infancy (the neonate part); at three months he is in babyhood. Option a is right because Hurlock's own label for birth to two weeks is infancy. Exam trap: everyday usage makes students think infancy covers the first year, and 'neonatal' sounds like the medical word for newborns, so both lead away from Hurlock's term. Key note: in medicine the neonatal period is the first 28 days, and Hurlock's neonate period sits inside infancy, so b is the near miss; Hurlock's term for the whole first two weeks is infancy, and a stands.", "topic": "Hurlock's infancy period"}, {"q": "The act in which people help someone without any personal interest or motive is known as:", "options": {"a": "Nurturance.", "b": "Social obligation.", "c": "Prosocial behaviour.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: helping someone without any personal interest or motive falls under prosocial behaviour. Prosocial behaviour is any voluntary action that benefits another person, such as sharing, comforting, donating, volunteering or rescuing. Within it, social psychologists pick out altruism: helping done purely for the other person's welfare, with no expectation of reward and often at a cost to oneself. Whether true altruism exists is a long debate. C. Daniel Batson (1981) argued in his empathy-altruism hypothesis that empathy produces a genuinely selfless wish to help: in his studies, students who felt high empathy for a suffering young woman, 'Elaine', offered to take her electric shocks even when they could easily leave. Robert Cialdini (1973) replied with the negative-state relief model, which says we help mainly to ease our own distress. Evolutionary accounts add kin selection (William Hamilton, 1964) and reciprocal altruism (Robert Trivers, 1971). Picture Meera stopping on a busy Kolkata road to help an elderly stranger who has fallen, then leaving without giving her name; she gains nothing and loses time, so her act is prosocial and, more precisely, altruistic. Option c is right because helping with no personal gain is the defining form of prosocial behaviour, and it is the only option that names helping at all. Exam trap: 'social obligation' sounds like helping, but helping because a norm demands it is a motive, which the stem rules out. Key note: strictly, helping with no expected reward is altruism, a subset of prosocial behaviour; since altruism is not offered, the umbrella term prosocial behaviour is the best fit, and c stands.", "topic": "Prosocial behaviour and altruism"}, {"q": "Short term memory holds information for ______ seconds.", "options": {"a": "30 seconds or less.", "b": "50 seconds.", "c": "More than 50 seconds.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: short-term memory (STM) holds information for about 30 seconds or less unless it is rehearsed. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information passes from sensory memory (under a second for visual traces) into STM, and from there into long-term memory (LTM), which is effectively unlimited and can last a lifetime. STM is small as well as brief. George Miller (1956) put its capacity at 'seven, plus or minus two' chunks, and Nelson Cowan (2001) argued it is nearer four when chunking and rehearsal are blocked. Its duration was measured by John Brown (1958) and by Lloyd Peterson and Margaret Peterson (1959) using the Brown-Peterson technique: participants heard a consonant trigram such as KBF, then counted backwards in threes to stop rehearsal. Recall was about 80 per cent after 3 seconds but fell to about 10 per cent by 18 seconds, so textbooks give STM a duration of about 15 to 30 seconds. Geoffrey Keppel and Benton Underwood (1962) later showed that much of this loss comes from interference by earlier trigrams rather than pure decay. Rehearsal can keep an item alive as long as you like: if Kabir repeats an OTP to himself, it lasts until he types it in, but if someone interrupts him he loses it within seconds. Option a is right because, without rehearsal, STM holds information for roughly 15 to 30 seconds, which is 30 seconds or less. Exam trap: 'seven plus or minus two' is the capacity of STM, not its duration in seconds, and examiners mix up the two numbers.", "topic": "Duration of short-term memory"}, {"q": "Delusion is a disorder of:", "options": {"a": "Perception.", "b": "Thought.", "c": "Personality.", "d": "Affect."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a delusion is a disorder of thought, specifically of the content of thought. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, gave the classic marks of a delusion: it is held with extraordinary conviction, it cannot be changed by contrary evidence or argument, and its content is false or impossible. Modern definitions add that it is out of keeping with the person's cultural and educational background, so a belief shared by a whole community, such as faith in a deity, is not a delusion. Psychopathology splits thought disorders into two kinds. Disorders of thought content concern what a person thinks: delusions, overvalued ideas and obsessions. Disorders of thought form concern how thoughts are linked: loosening of associations, flight of ideas, tangentiality, circumstantiality and thought blocking. Common delusional themes are persecutory, grandiose, referential, jealous, nihilistic and somatic. Delusions are a core symptom of schizophrenia and delusional disorder, and they also occur in psychotic depression and mania. For example, Kabir insists that his neighbours are spying on him through cameras hidden in his ceiling fan; when his family opens the fan and shows him it is empty, he says they have been bribed. That fixed false belief is a disorder of thought content. Option b is right because a delusion is a false belief, and beliefs are products of thinking, not of sensing or feeling. Exam trap: hallucinations and delusions are often listed together as psychotic symptoms, but a hallucination (a perception without a stimulus) is a disorder of perception, while a delusion is a disorder of thought.", "topic": "Delusion as thought disorder"}, {"q": "The term free association was coined by:", "options": {"a": "Adler.", "b": "Erickson.", "c": "Freud.", "d": "Jung."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: free association, as a technique and a term in psychotherapy, comes from Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Working on hysteria with Josef Breuer (their Studies on Hysteria appeared in 1895), Freud first used hypnosis, then a 'pressure technique', pressing the patient's forehead and insisting that a memory would come. Many patients could not be hypnotised, so during the 1890s he replaced both with free association: the patient lies on a couch, the analyst sits out of sight, and the patient follows one 'fundamental rule', to say whatever comes to mind, however trivial, embarrassing or illogical, without censoring it. Freud believed in psychic determinism, so no idea was random; each link in the chain led back towards a buried conflict. When the chain stalls, the patient is showing resistance, and the feelings the patient comes to direct at the analyst show transference; both are worked through in therapy. Free association, with dream analysis (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), became the central method of psychoanalysis. For example, Riya begins by talking about missing a train, drifts to her fear of being left behind, and then reaches a forgotten memory of being lost at a mela as a small child. Option c is right because Freud created and named free association as the core method of psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) is tempting because he used the word association test, in which a person answers a stimulus word with the first word that comes to mind, but that is a structured test, not free association. Key note: Francis Galton (1879) first ran word-association experiments on himself, but free association as a therapy method (say whatever comes to mind) was Sigmund Freud's, developed in the 1890s in place of hypnosis; c stands.", "topic": "Free association"}, {"q": "The term IQ was introduced by:", "options": {"a": "Terman.", "b": "Allport.", "c": "William Stern.", "d": "Watson."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the term intelligence quotient (IQ) was introduced by the German psychologist William Stern (1912). Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test in Paris, and their 1908 revision expressed a child's result as a mental age (MA): the age level of the items the child could pass. Mental age alone is hard to compare across ages, because being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. Stern's solution was to divide mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a quotient, the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916) then multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove the decimals in his Stanford-Binet, giving IQ = MA/CA x 100, and made the abbreviation IQ popular. So if Arjun is 8 years old with a mental age of 10, his IQ is 10/8 x 100 = 125. The ratio IQ later ran into trouble with adults, because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), which compares a person with others of the same age. Option c is right because Stern coined both the quotient and its name. Exam trap: Terman is the most common wrong answer because the Stanford-Binet made IQ famous, but he popularised the term rather than introducing it. Key note: William Stern (1912) coined the intelligence quotient (Intelligenzquotient, mental age divided by chronological age); Lewis Terman (1916, Stanford-Binet) multiplied it by 100 and made the abbreviation IQ popular, so Terman is the trap; Stern stands.", "topic": "Origin of the term IQ"}, {"q": "To compute IQ which formula is used?", "options": {"a": "MA/CA X 100.", "b": "CA/MA X 100.", "c": "CA/MA-100.", "d": "MA/CA+100."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the classic formula for IQ is mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100 (MA/CA x 100). This is the ratio IQ. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test, and their 1908 revision expressed results as a mental age (MA): the age level at which a child performs. William Stern (1912) divided MA by chronological age (CA) to form the intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied it by 100 in the Stanford-Binet to clear the decimals. When MA equals CA, the IQ is exactly 100, which is average. A child ahead of her age scores above 100 and a child behind scores below. Worked example: Meera is 10 years old and passes test items up to the 12-year level, so her IQ is 12/10 x 100 = 120. Her classmate Kabir, also 10, has a mental age of 8, so his IQ is 8/10 x 100 = 80. The ratio IQ fails for adults: mental age stops climbing in the mid-teens while chronological age keeps rising, so a 40-year-old would seem to lose intelligence every year. David Wechsler (1939) therefore introduced the deviation IQ, which places a person's score on a normal curve for their own age group, with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Option a is right because it is the only option that divides mental age by chronological age and then multiplies by 100. Exam trap: the inverted ratio, CA/MA x 100, is the usual slip; remember that a mental age below chronological age must give an IQ below 100, so MA goes on top.", "topic": "Ratio IQ formula"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of thinking?", "options": {"a": "Perceptual thinking (concrete thinking).", "b": "Conceptual thinking (abstract thinking).", "c": "Positive thinking.", "d": "Problem solving thinking."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: 'positive thinking' is not one of the types of thinking described in psychology; it is a popular self-help idea. Thinking is the mental manipulation of representations (images, concepts, symbols and language) to understand, solve problems, reason and decide. Standard educational psychology texts, especially those used in India, classify thinking by its level and purpose. Perceptual or concrete thinking works with objects and events present to the senses, like a young child counting real mangoes. Conceptual or abstract thinking uses concepts and symbols that go beyond what is in front of us, such as 'justice' or 'x + 3 = 7'. Problem-solving or reflective thinking is goal-directed: it defines a problem, gathers facts, tries solutions and checks them, in the steps John Dewey (1910) set out in How We Think. Other listed types include creative or divergent thinking (Joy Paul Guilford, 1950), critical thinking and associative or non-directed thinking such as daydreaming. 'Positive thinking' comes instead from Norman Vincent Peale's (1952) bestseller The Power of Positive Thinking; it describes an optimistic attitude, not a cognitive process with its own structure. Psychology studies optimism under other names, such as the learned optimism of Martin Seligman (1990). For example, when Arjun works out why his scooter will not start by checking the fuel and then the spark plug, he is using problem-solving thinking; telling himself 'it will all be fine' is an attitude, not a type of thought. Option c is right because positive thinking is a self-help outlook rather than a recognised category of thinking. Exam trap: the word 'thinking' appears in all four options, so students assume each is a textbook category; look for the one that describes a mood or outlook rather than a mental process.", "topic": "Types of thinking"}, {"q": "According to Thorndike all of the following are law of learning except:", "options": {"a": "Law of readiness.", "b": "Law of exercise or practice.", "c": "Law of effect.", "d": "Law of memory."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: there is no 'law of memory' among Edward Thorndike's laws of learning. Edward Thorndike (1898) put hungry cats in puzzle boxes with food outside. At first a cat scratched and pushed at random; by accident it worked the latch that opened the door, and over many trials its escape time fell gradually, not suddenly. Thorndike called this trial-and-error learning and explained it through connectionism: learning is the forming of bonds between a stimulus and a response. He proposed three primary laws. The law of readiness says that when a person is ready to act, acting is satisfying and being prevented is annoying. The law of exercise says that bonds are strengthened by use (law of use) and weakened by disuse (law of disuse), so practice matters. The law of effect, set out in Animal Intelligence (1911), says that responses followed by satisfaction are stamped in and those followed by discomfort are stamped out; it became the root of the operant conditioning of Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1938). Thorndike also listed subsidiary laws such as multiple response, attitude (set), associative shifting and response by analogy. Around 1930 he weakened the law of exercise and the punishment half of the law of effect, after finding that practice without feedback, and punishment, did little. For example, Riya learns sums faster when she is eager to start (readiness), practises daily (exercise) and is praised for correct answers (effect). Option d is right because readiness, exercise and effect are Thorndike's three laws, and memory is not among them. Exam trap: the law of disuse sounds like forgetting, which tempts students to accept a 'law of memory', but disuse is part of the law of exercise.", "topic": "Thorndike's laws of learning"}, {"q": "The MMPI is used to measure:", "options": {"a": "Unconscious drives.", "b": "Big Five traits.", "c": "Personality and psychological disorders.", "d": "Leadership potential."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) measures personality and psychological disorders (psychopathology). Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built it at the University of Minnesota Hospitals to aid psychiatric diagnosis. They used empirical criterion keying: hundreds of true or false statements were given to patient groups with known diagnoses and to about 700 'Minnesota normals', and an item was kept for a scale only if it separated the groups, whatever its content seemed to be about. The result was 10 clinical scales: hypochondriasis, depression, hysteria, psychopathic deviate, masculinity-femininity, paranoia, psychasthenia, schizophrenia, hypomania and social introversion. Validity scales guard against distorted answering: L (lie, faking good), F (infrequency, faking bad or random answering) and K (defensiveness), plus the 'cannot say' count. Scores are read as a profile, often by the two highest scales (two-point codes). The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 items; shorter versions, the MMPI-2-RF (2008) and the MMPI-3 (2020), followed, and the MMPI-A serves adolescents. It is the most widely used and researched objective personality test in clinical work. For example, if Kabir's MMPI-2 shows high depression and psychasthenia scores with acceptable L, F and K, the clinician may suspect depression with anxiety and plan a fuller interview. Option c is right because the MMPI was designed to assess both personality characteristics and clinical disorders. Exam trap: the MMPI is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test, so 'unconscious drives' is wrong; and it was not built on the Big Five, which belongs to the NEO inventories.", "topic": "MMPI purpose"}, {"q": "According to Eysenck extraverts seek to ________ their arousal while introverts seek to _________ their arousal.", "options": {"a": "Decrease, increase.", "b": "Hide, reveal.", "c": "Increase, decrease.", "d": "Reveal, hide."}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to Hans Eysenck, extraverts seek to increase their arousal while introverts seek to decrease it. In The Biological Basis of Personality, Hans Eysenck (1967) linked his extraversion-introversion dimension to the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS), the brainstem network that sets how alert and aroused the cerebral cortex is. He proposed that introverts have a higher resting level of cortical arousal. People feel and perform best at a moderate, optimal level of arousal, the inverted U of the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908). Introverts already sit near or above that level, so extra stimulation overloads them and they avoid noise, crowds and risk. Extraverts start below it, so they are chronically under-aroused and seek stimulation, such as parties, music and novelty, to bring their arousal up. The evidence fits: when a few drops of lemon juice are put on the tongue, introverts salivate more than extraverts, and introverts are more sensitive to faint stimuli, tolerate pain less and condition faster. Later research supported these differences more firmly than the exact brain mechanism. Picture two hostel roommates: Kabir studies with loud music and friends chatting around him, while Meera goes to the quiet library corner. On Eysenck's theory Kabir is raising his low arousal and Meera is protecting her already high arousal. Option c is right because it matches the direction of the theory: under-aroused extraverts increase stimulation and over-aroused introverts reduce it. Exam trap: option a reverses the theory, and the reversal is tempting because introverts look calm; remember that their quiet behaviour is a way of lowering arousal that is already high.", "topic": "Eysenck's arousal theory"}, {"q": "Projective tests claim to reveal information about:", "options": {"a": "Career aptitude.", "b": "Intellectual attainment.", "c": "Unconscious processes.", "d": "Parenting style."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: projective tests claim to reveal unconscious processes: hidden needs, conflicts, fears and wishes that a person cannot or will not report directly. They rest on the projective hypothesis named by Lawrence Frank (1939): when people respond to an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they must impose their own meaning on it, and in doing so they project their inner world onto it. The idea grows from the concept of projection of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), the defence of attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others, and from the psychoanalytic belief that much of personality is unconscious. The best-known projective tests are the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921; ten cards), the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), in which people tell stories about ambiguous pictures, sentence completion tests and drawing tests such as Draw-A-Person. Because the task is open-ended and its purpose disguised, faking is harder than on a questionnaire, though scoring is less reliable and validity is often criticised. For example, shown the TAT card of a boy looking at a violin, Arjun tells a story about a child forced to practise by a strict father who keeps failing; a clinician might take this as a clue to Arjun's own feelings about parental pressure. Option c is right because uncovering unconscious material through ambiguous stimuli is the stated aim of projective testing. Exam trap: projective tests are about personality and the unconscious, not abilities; career aptitude and intellectual attainment are measured by structured aptitude and achievement tests with right and wrong answers.", "topic": "Projective tests and the unconscious"}, {"q": "The relative apparent motion of objects in the visual field as the viewer moves the head is called:", "options": {"a": "Image retina system.", "b": "Accommodation.", "c": "Motion parallax.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the relative apparent motion of objects in the visual field as the viewer moves the head is called motion parallax. When you move, objects at different distances seem to move at different speeds and in different directions across your view. Near objects sweep past quickly in the direction opposite to your movement, while far objects move slowly and seem to travel with you. The brain reads these speed differences as distance: the faster something slides across the retina, the nearer it is. James J. Gibson (1950), in The Perception of the Visual World, widened the idea into optic flow, the whole pattern of motion that streams across the eyes as we move, which guides walking, driving and landing an aircraft. Motion parallax is a monocular depth cue, since it works with one eye, and a kinetic cue, since it needs movement. It sits beside the other monocular cues (linear perspective, relative size, interposition, texture gradient, aerial perspective and accommodation) and the two binocular cues (retinal disparity and convergence). The classic example: Arjun looks out of a train window and sees the electric poles beside the track flash backwards, while distant hills and the moon seem to glide along with the train. Option c is right because motion parallax is the name for the depth information carried by the different apparent motions of near and far objects when the head moves. Exam trap: accommodation is also a monocular depth cue, so it sounds plausible, but it comes from the lens changing shape to focus, not from movement.", "topic": "Motion parallax depth cue"}, {"q": "Clinical psychology's major contribution to World War 1 was:", "options": {"a": "Creation of tests to measure the intelligence of recruits.", "b": "Treatment for veterans with shell shock.", "c": "Development of effective interrogation technique.", "d": "Coaching of newly trained fighter pilots."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: clinical psychology's major contribution to World War I was the creation of tests to measure the intelligence of recruits. When the United States entered the war in 1917, the army needed to sort well over a million men quickly. Robert Yerkes (1917), then president of the American Psychological Association, chaired a committee of psychologists that built two group intelligence tests. The Army Alpha was a written test for literate, English-speaking recruits, with items on arithmetic, analogies, number series and general information. The Army Beta was a non-verbal, pictorial test for men who could not read or did not speak English, using mazes, picture completion and cube counting. About 1.75 million recruits were tested, and the scores were used to screen out men judged unfit and to pick candidates for officer training. In the same year Robert Woodworth (1917) wrote the Personal Data Sheet to screen soldiers likely to break down under fire, the forerunner of personality inventories. The war showed that tests could be given to huge groups at once, which set off a boom in group testing in schools and industry in the 1920s, and for decades testing remained the clinical psychologist's main job. Imagine Arjun, a farm worker who never went to school: he would have taken the Beta, not the Alpha. Option a is right because the army intelligence tests were psychology's signature contribution to the First World War. Exam trap: shell shock sounds like the obvious clinical task, but during the war it was treated mainly by army doctors and psychiatrists. Key note: shell shock treatment was mostly psychiatry's work; clinical psychology's big move into therapy came after World War II, so a stands.", "topic": "Army Alpha and Beta tests"}, {"q": "Behaviour modification is an example of which of the following treatment methods?", "options": {"a": "Enable the individual to unlearn maladaptive behaviours and emotions.", "b": "Enable the individual to explore their childhood behaviours.", "c": "Enable the individual to analyse the behaviours of others.", "d": "Enable the individual to modify the behaviours of others."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: behaviour modification is a treatment that helps the individual unlearn maladaptive behaviours and emotions and learn adaptive ones in their place. Behaviour modification (used almost interchangeably with behaviour therapy) applies experimentally established principles of learning to clinical problems. Its core assumption is that problem behaviour is learned by the same laws as normal behaviour, so it can be unlearned. The behaviour itself is the target, not a hidden conflict behind it, and what keeps the problem going now matters more than how it began. Hans Eysenck (1952) argued that a treatment built on learning theory should replace talk therapy, and Joseph Wolpe (1958) and Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1953) supplied the classical and operant tools. The name behaviour therapy first appeared in a 1953 report by Ogden Lindsley, Skinner and Harry Solomon. Classical methods (systematic desensitisation, flooding) extinguish or countercondition learned fears; operant methods (token economies, shaping, extinction, time-out) change behaviour through its consequences. Example: Kabir panics in lifts after getting stuck once. His therapist does not ask about his childhood; she teaches him relaxation and builds a graded hierarchy of lift rides, so the conditioned fear is unlearned. Option a is right because unlearning maladaptive behaviour and emotion is the defining aim of the behavioural approach. Exam trap: b describes psychoanalysis, which digs into childhood. d sounds behavioural because therapists and trained parents do change other people's behaviour, but the stem asks what the treatment does for the individual in treatment.", "topic": "Aim of behaviour modification"}, {"q": "The stage of psycho-social developments in which basic trust vs. mistrust develops:", "options": {"a": "1-3 years.", "b": "0-1 year.", "c": "3-6 years.", "d": "6-11 years."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Erik Erikson's first psychosocial crisis, basic trust versus mistrust, belongs to the first year of life (birth to about 1 year). Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages across the whole lifespan. Each stage is a crisis between two poles, and a good resolution leaves a virtue. In stage 1, infancy (Sigmund Freud's oral stage), the helpless baby learns whether the world is dependable. If feeding, comfort and contact come reliably and warmly, the infant develops basic trust and the virtue of hope; if care is cold, rejecting or unpredictable, mistrust sets in. The key relationship is the maternal person. Erikson held that a little mistrust is healthy, but trust must outweigh it. Example: baby Meera cries and her mother comes, feeds and soothes her, day after day. Meera learns that people can be counted on, a template for her later relationships. The next stages are autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3 years, will), initiative versus guilt (3 to 6, purpose) and industry versus inferiority (6 to 11 or puberty, competence). Option b is right because trust versus mistrust is resolved in the first year, before the toddler's push for autonomy. Exam trap: some texts stretch stage 1 to 18 months, which tempts students towards 1 to 3 years; but 1 to 3 is autonomy versus shame and doubt, the toilet-training stage. Every option here is a real Erikson age band, so match the crisis to its band.", "topic": "Erikson's trust versus mistrust"}, {"q": "At which stage the child begins to utter words to ask for something rather than just reaching out to get it:", "options": {"a": "Pre-operation stage.", "b": "Concrete operation stage.", "c": "Formal operation stage.", "d": "Sensory motor stage."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: using words to ask for something, instead of reaching out to grab it, marks Jean Piaget's preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years). Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) described four stages of cognitive development. In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) the infant knows the world through action: looking, grasping, sucking and reaching. Near its end the child gains mental representation, and the preoperational stage opens with the symbolic (semiotic) function: the ability to let one thing stand for another. Words, mental images, deferred imitation and pretend play all use it. The child no longer has to act on an object to deal with it; a word can now do the work. Thought is still preoperational, though: it is egocentric, centred on one feature at a time and not reversible, so conservation fails. Example: at 10 months Arjun stretches and whines towards the biscuit tin; at two and a half he says 'Biscuit, please' from across the room. That shift from action to symbol is the change of stage. Option a is right because the stem contrasts symbolic requesting with physical reaching, the hallmark of preoperational thought. Exam trap: first words arrive around the first birthday, so 'sensory motor' looks right, but the stem describes words replacing actions, not the first word itself. Key note: first words appear around 12 months, inside Jean Piaget's sensorimotor stage, so d is the near miss; but using words as symbols in place of acting on objects (the symbolic or semiotic function) is the hallmark of the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years), which is what the stem describes, so a stands.", "topic": "Preoperational symbolic function"}, {"q": "According to Piaget the basic unit with which the cognitive structure is built up is:", "options": {"a": "Schema.", "b": "Accommodation.", "c": "Equilibrium.", "d": "Assimilation."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: for Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), the schema (or scheme) is the basic unit from which cognitive structure is built. A schema is an organised pattern of action or thought for dealing with a class of experiences. A newborn's sucking and grasping are schemas; so are a ten-year-old's adding and classifying. Cognitive structure is simply the whole set of a child's schemas and the way they are organised. Schemas change through two functional invariants. Organisation combines simple schemas into larger systems, as looking and grasping merge into reaching for what one sees. Adaptation fits schemas to the world through two processes: assimilation takes new experience into an existing schema, and accommodation changes the schema when the experience will not fit. When schemas handle events smoothly the child is in equilibrium; a misfit causes disequilibrium, and the self-regulating drive back to balance, equilibration, moves the child from stage to stage. Example: two-year-old Riya's 'doggie' schema means any four-legged animal, so she calls a cow 'doggie' (assimilation). When it moos and her father says 'cow', she splits the schema in two (accommodation). Option a is right because assimilation, accommodation and equilibrium all act on schemas; the schema is the unit they work with. Exam trap: examiners list the processes beside the unit. Assimilation and accommodation are things done to schemas, and equilibrium is a state of balance among them, so none of them is the building block itself.", "topic": "Piaget's schema"}, {"q": "One of the following part of the brain controls impulsive behaviour:", "options": {"a": "Cerebrum.", "b": "Prefrontal cortex.", "c": "Cerebellum.", "d": "Left hemisphere."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the prefrontal cortex, the front part of the frontal lobe, is the brain region that controls impulsive behaviour. The prefrontal cortex runs the executive functions: planning, judgement, working memory, set-shifting and, crucially, response inhibition, stopping an urge before it becomes an act. The classic evidence is Phineas Gage. In 1848 a tamping iron passed through his frontal lobe in a railway blast; his speech, memory and intelligence survived, but the reliable foreman became impulsive, profane and poor in judgement. His physician John Martyn Harlow (1868) reported friends saying he was 'no longer Gage', and Hanna Damasio and colleagues (1994) placed the damage in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex. Orbitofrontal and ventromedial damage gives a disinhibited, socially inappropriate picture; dorsolateral damage gives a dysexecutive syndrome with perseveration. The prefrontal cortex is also the last region to mature, into the mid-20s, which helps explain adolescent risk taking, and weak prefrontal control features in ADHD and antisocial behaviour. Example: Arjun, 17, wants to overtake on a blind bend; his prefrontal 'brake' weighs the danger against the thrill and stops him. Option b is right because impulse control is a prefrontal executive function, shown by lesion, imaging and developmental evidence. Exam trap: 'cerebrum' is tempting because the prefrontal cortex sits inside it, but the cerebrum is the whole forebrain mass with sensory, motor and language areas too; the specific region is the answer.", "topic": "Prefrontal cortex and impulse control"}, {"q": "The theoretical position that says the whole is more than the sum of the parts is:", "options": {"a": "Cognitive psychology.", "b": "Gestalt psychology.", "c": "Behaviourism.", "d": "Psychoanalysis."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Gestalt psychology holds that the whole is more than (strictly, different from) the sum of its parts. Gestalt is German for an organised whole or form. The school began when Max Wertheimer (1912) reported the phi phenomenon: two lines flashed about 60 milliseconds apart look like one line moving. The motion lies in neither stimulus, so no adding up of sensations can produce it. With Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) and Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941), Wertheimer attacked structuralism, which broke consciousness into elements, and behaviourism, which broke behaviour into reflexes. Gestalt psychologists studied wholes and relations instead: figure and ground, the laws of grouping (proximity, similarity, closure, continuity, Prägnanz), Kohler's insight in chimpanzees and the field theory of Kurt Lewin (1890 to 1947). Koffka preferred 'different from' to 'more than', because the whole has its own properties, not just extra ones. Example: Meera hears a song played in a different key. Every note has changed, yet she recognises the melody at once, because the tune lives in the relations between notes, not in the notes themselves. Option b is right because the whole-over-parts slogan is the founding claim of Gestalt psychology. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt's (1832 to 1920) creative synthesis and John Stuart Mill's (1806 to 1873) mental chemistry also say a whole gains new properties, so setters sometimes bait with structuralism or associationism; but the slogan and the theoretical position belong to Gestalt.", "topic": "Gestalt: whole and parts"}, {"q": "______ is a negative emotional response or dislike towards members of a group.", "options": {"a": "Prejudice.", "b": "Stereotype.", "c": "Discrimination.", "d": "Differential attitude."}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: prejudice is a negative emotional response, an attitude of dislike, towards people because they belong to a particular group. Social psychologists split intergroup bias into three parts that match the ABC model of attitudes. Prejudice is the affective part (A): a feeling of dislike or hostility. Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, defined it as \"an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalization\". A stereotype is the cognitive part (C): a belief about the traits typical of a group, which the journalist Walter Lippmann (1922) called \"pictures in our heads\". Discrimination is the behavioural part (B): unfair negative action towards a group's members, such as refusing them a flat or a job. The three often travel together but can separate: a person can hold a stereotype without hostility, or discriminate because of a rule without personal dislike. Allport also ranked prejudice in action on a five-step scale, from antilocution (hostile talk) through avoidance, discrimination and physical attack to extermination. Example: Kabir believes people from another state are lazy (stereotype), feels irritated whenever one joins his office (prejudice), and never shortlists their applications (discrimination). Option a is right because the stem asks for an emotional response, and emotion is the affective component, which is prejudice. Exam trap: setters swap the three terms. Remember: stereotype is what you think, prejudice is what you feel, discrimination is what you do.", "topic": "Prejudice, stereotype, discrimination"}, {"q": "______ technique involves presenting target people with small requests and then following up with larger requests, the one that is desired all along.", "options": {"a": "Low ball.", "b": "Foot in the door.", "c": "Door in the face.", "d": "That's not all."}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: making a small request first and then following it with the larger request wanted all along is the foot-in-the-door technique. (The printed paper garbles this option as 'Put in the door'; it means foot in the door.) Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966) showed it with Californian homeowners. Asked cold to put a large, ugly 'Drive Carefully' sign on their lawns, about 17% agreed. But about 76% agreed if, two weeks earlier, they had accepted a small 'Be a safe driver' sign. Two explanations are offered: consistency (having said yes once, refusing now feels inconsistent) and self-perception (watching yourself agree, you infer that you are the kind of person who supports such causes). The first request must be small enough that nearly everyone accepts it. Its relatives work differently. Door-in-the-face (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) reverses the order. Low-ball (Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins agreement to a good deal and then worsens the terms. That's-not-all (Jerry Burger, 1986) sweetens the offer before the person has answered. Example: a volunteer asks Riya to sign a petition for a cleaner lake; a week later he asks her to give up a Sunday to clear rubbish from it, and she agrees. Option b is right because small-then-large is the defining sequence of the foot-in-the-door. Exam trap: door-in-the-face is the mirror image (large, refused, then small), and examiners swap the two; fix the order of the requests before you answer.", "topic": "Foot-in-the-door technique"}, {"q": "The Father of Experimental Psychology is:", "options": {"a": "William James.", "b": "Gustav Theodor Fechner.", "c": "Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt.", "d": "Sigmund Freud."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt (1832 to 1920) is called the father of experimental psychology. Wundt, a physician who had worked as assistant to Hermann von Helmholtz (1821 to 1894), announced psychology as a new, independent science in Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), where 'physiological' meant using the experimental methods of physiology. In 1879 his students began original research in his laboratory at the University of Leipzig, the date textbooks give for psychology's birth as a science. He added a journal, Philosophische Studien (1881), and trained a generation of students who carried the laboratory method to Europe and America. His method was controlled introspection: trained observers gave short reports on carefully timed and repeated stimuli. He studied reaction times, sensations, feelings and attention, and called his system voluntarism because it stressed will and attention. Example: in Wundt's laboratory a student such as Arjun would press a key when a light flashed, and the difference between simple and choice reaction times would be measured in milliseconds. Option c is right because Wundt gave experimental psychology its first formal research laboratory, journal and students. Exam trap: Gustav Theodor Fechner (1801 to 1887) and William James (1842 to 1910) are each 'first' at something nearby, Fechner for psychophysics and James for a small Harvard demonstration laboratory (1875) and American psychology. Key note: some historians (for example Edwin Boring, 1886 to 1968) credit Gustav Fechner's Elements of Psychophysics (1860) with founding experimental psychology, but Wundt is the standard answer and stands.", "topic": "Wundt, father of experimental psychology"}, {"q": "Hermann Ebbinghaus, had done the pioneering experiments on:", "options": {"a": "Perception.", "b": "Psychophysics.", "c": "Serial Position Effect.", "d": "Memory."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1850 to 1909) did the pioneering experiments on memory, reported in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory, 1885). Ebbinghaus showed that a higher mental process could be measured experimentally, something Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) had doubted. Acting as his own sole participant, he invented the nonsense syllable (consonant-vowel-consonant strings such as DAX or BUP) to avoid the meanings and associations of real words. He learned lists until he could recite them twice without error and then relearned them after delays. Relearning was quicker even when nothing could be recalled, and he measured this with the savings method: original trials minus relearning trials, divided by original trials, times 100. His forgetting curve drops steeply, losing more than half within an hour and about two thirds within a day, then levels off without reaching zero. He also found the spacing effect (spread-out practice beats massed practice) and noticed that items at the start and end of a list are learned more easily. Example: Meera learns a list of nonsense syllables in 20 readings; a week later she recalls none, but relearns the list in 12 readings, a 40% saving. Option d is right because nonsense syllables, savings and the forgetting curve are all memory research, and the serial position finding is one result within it. Exam trap: the serial position effect is a real Ebbinghaus observation, so it baits the half-informed; but it is one part of his memory work, and memory is the full answer.", "topic": "Ebbinghaus and memory"}, {"q": "Among the following pioneering psychologists who is not associated with Behaviourism?", "options": {"a": "A.T Beck.", "b": "E.R Guthrie.", "c": "B.F Skinner.", "d": "C.L Hull."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021) is not associated with behaviourism; he founded cognitive therapy. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), held that psychology should study only observable behaviour and its environmental causes, leaving out mind and consciousness. The neobehaviourists who followed refined it. Edwin Ray Guthrie (1886 to 1959) explained learning by contiguity alone: a stimulus that accompanied a movement will tend to be followed by that movement again, at full strength after one trial. Clark Leonard Hull (1884 to 1952) built a hypothetico-deductive system of drive reduction in which reaction potential equals habit strength times drive (Principles of Behavior, 1943). Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist, studied operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement. Beck, a psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, moved in a different direction. In the 1960s he argued that depression grows from negative automatic thoughts and schemas, summed up in the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), and he built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). His therapy targets thoughts, which strict behaviourists refused to treat as causes. Example: Riya fails a test and thinks 'I am useless and always will be'. A Beck therapist tests that thought against the evidence, while a Skinnerian would look at how studying is reinforced. Option a is right because Beck's theory is cognitive, from the school that rose against behaviourism. Exam trap: cognitive behaviour therapy uses behavioural techniques, so Beck's name sits near behaviour therapy in textbooks, but he is a cognitive pioneer, not a behaviourist.", "topic": "Behaviourists versus Beck"}, {"q": "Who is the most valuable contributor in the theory of insight learning?", "options": {"a": "E.L Thorndike.", "b": "E.C Tolman.", "c": "K. Koffka.", "d": "W. Kohler."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) is the main contributor to the theory of insight learning. Kohler, one of the three founders of Gestalt psychology, was stranded at the anthropoid research station on Tenerife from 1913 to 1920 and studied chimpanzees there (The Mentality of Apes, 1917 in German, 1925 in English). His best-known chimpanzee, Sultan, could not reach a banana outside the cage with either of two short sticks. After a pause he fitted one stick into the other and at once raked in the fruit. Other apes stacked boxes to reach fruit hung from the roof. Kohler called this insight: a sudden reorganisation of the perceptual field in which the animal sees how the parts of the problem relate to the goal. Insight learning is abrupt rather than gradual, smooth once found, well remembered and readily transferred to new problems, and the moment of understanding is the 'aha' experience. Example: Arjun stares at a puzzle for ten minutes, then suddenly sees that turning one piece sideways solves it, and next time he repeats the solution at once. Option d is right because Kohler's ape studies are the founding evidence and theory of insight learning. Exam trap: Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941) was Kohler's Gestalt colleague, so the shared school makes c tempting, and Edward Thorndike (1898) is offered because his cats in puzzle boxes are the classic contrast: their learning was gradual trial and error, the opposite of insight.", "topic": "Köhler's insight learning"}, {"q": "Transference Focused Therapy is a structured psychodynamic treatment designed by:", "options": {"a": "M. Linehan.", "b": "O. F Kernberg.", "c": "H. Kohut.", "d": "F. Perls."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: transference-focused psychotherapy (TFP) was designed by Otto F. Kernberg (born 1928) for borderline personality disorder. Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, described borderline personality organisation: identity diffusion, primitive defences (especially splitting and projective identification) and largely intact reality testing. Because good and bad images of self and others never merged in early development, the person sees others as all good or all bad and swings between idealising and devaluing them. TFP, set out in a manual by John Clarkin, Frank Yeomans and Kernberg (1999), uses this pattern as its material. It begins with a treatment contract that sets limits on self-harm and acting out. Sessions are usually twice a week, and the therapist watches how the split images of self and others appear in the relationship with the therapist (the transference), then clarifies, confronts and interprets them so the patient can join them into a whole picture. A randomised trial by Clarkin and colleagues (2007) found improvement in suicidality, anger and impulsivity. Example: Meera praises her therapist as perfect one week and calls him cruel the next when he moves a session; in TFP the therapist explores that switch as it happens in the room. Option b is right because TFP is Kernberg's object-relations treatment built on his model of borderline organisation. Exam trap: Marsha Linehan (1993) also designed a structured treatment for borderline personality disorder, dialectical behaviour therapy, so a is the near miss; but DBT is cognitive-behavioural, not psychodynamic.", "topic": "Kernberg's transference-focused psychotherapy"}, {"q": "The concept of Transitional Object was introduced by:", "options": {"a": "D. W. Winnicott.", "b": "J Bowlby.", "c": "HS Sullivan.", "d": "R Fairbairn."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: the transitional object was introduced by Donald W. Winnicott (1896 to 1971), the British paediatrician and psychoanalyst, in his paper 'Transitional Objects and Transitional Phenomena' (read 1951, published 1953). A transitional object is the infant's first 'not-me' possession: a blanket, soft toy or corner of cloth that the baby clings to, especially at bedtime or when the mother is away. Winnicott said it is neither wholly inside (part of the baby) nor wholly outside (part of the world); it lives in an intermediate area of experience between fantasy and reality. It helps the child move from fusion with the mother to awareness that she is separate, soothing anxiety while the child learns to bear her absence. Parents sense its value and do not wash or replace it, because changing it breaks the continuity it offers; in time it simply loses importance. Winnicott linked this intermediate space to adult play, art, culture and religion. The idea sits inside his wider object-relations theory: the good-enough mother, the holding environment, and the true self versus the false self. Example: three-year-old Kabir cannot sleep without his worn blue blanket when his mother travels; it stands in for her comfort until he can hold her in mind. Option a is right because both the term and the theory are Winnicott's. Exam trap: John Bowlby (1907 to 1990) also wrote about separation and comfort, so attachment theory tempts, but Bowlby's concepts are the attachment bond, the secure base and internal working models.", "topic": "Winnicott's transitional object"}, {"q": "\"Dereflection\" is a component of:", "options": {"a": "Existential Therapy.", "b": "Person Centered Therapy.", "c": "Logotherapy.", "d": "Gestalt Therapy."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: dereflection is a technique of logotherapy, the meaning-centred therapy of Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997). Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi concentration camps, described his approach in Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German). Logotherapy (from logos, meaning) holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning, and that a frustrated search for meaning produces an existential vacuum and noogenic neurosis. Frankl gave it two signature techniques. Dereflection counters hyper-reflection, excessive attention to oneself or one's symptom, which often makes the symptom worse, as in insomnia or sexual dysfunction. The client is helped to turn attention away from the self and towards a meaningful task or other people, so the problem fades for lack of feeding. Paradoxical intention, the second technique, asks the client to wish for or do the very thing he fears, which breaks anticipatory anxiety. Socratic dialogue then helps the client find meaning in work, love or suffering. Example: Arjun lies awake checking whether sleep is coming. His therapist uses dereflection, asking him to spend that time planning the music class he teaches, and sleep arrives once he stops watching for it. Option c is right because dereflection is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: logotherapy is one of the existential therapies, so a is the near miss; but when the specific school is offered, a named technique goes to its own school, not to the broad family.", "topic": "Dereflection in logotherapy"}, {"q": "The concept of \"birth trauma\" was first mentioned by:", "options": {"a": "O. Fenichel.", "b": "S. Freud.", "c": "C.G Jung.", "d": "O. Rank."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the concept of birth trauma belongs to Otto Rank (1884 to 1939), who set it out in The Trauma of Birth (1924). Rank was one of the closest early followers of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) and for years secretary of the Vienna Psychoanalytic Society. He argued that birth is the first and model anxiety of life: the baby is torn from the blissful unity of the womb and separated from the mother. All later anxiety, he said, repeats this primal separation, and much of life is a struggle between the wish to return to that union and the fear of losing oneself in it. Neurosis was an unresolved birth trauma, and therapy should help the patient relive and master the separation. Rank later developed will therapy, a short, time-limited approach that stressed the patient's creative will; it became the basis of the functional school of social case work. Example: on Rank's view, Riya's panic every time she leaves home for a new hostel would echo the first separation of all, at birth. Option d is right because the named concept, and the book that made it famous, are Rank's. Exam trap: Freud had already called birth the prototype of anxiety, so b looks safe, but the stem names 'birth trauma', which is Rank's theory. Key note: Sigmund Freud had already called birth the first experience and prototype of anxiety (a 1909 footnote to The Interpretation of Dreams) and later rejected Rank's theory (Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety, 1926), so b is arguable for 'first mentioned'; the concept 'birth trauma' is Rank's, and d stands.", "topic": "Rank's birth trauma"}, {"q": "The best known work of Erich Fromm is:", "options": {"a": "Freud and Beyond.", "b": "Escape from Freedom.", "c": "Freud along the Ganges.", "d": "Madness and Civilization."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the best-known work of Erich Fromm (1900 to 1980) is Escape from Freedom (1941), published in Britain as The Fear of Freedom. Fromm, a German psychoanalyst also trained in sociology, called his approach humanistic psychoanalysis and studied how whole societies shape character. Writing as fascism spread across Europe, he argued that modern people have won freedom from the old ties of church, clan and guild, but at the price of isolation and anxiety. Many flee this burden through three mechanisms of escape: authoritarianism (submitting to a strong leader or dominating others), destructiveness (attacking the world that threatens them) and automaton conformity (becoming exactly what society expects). The healthy answer is positive freedom, reached through spontaneous love and productive work. His later books include Man for Himself (1947), The Sane Society (1955), which set out the existential needs of relatedness, transcendence, rootedness, identity and a frame of orientation, and The Art of Loving (1956). Example: Kabir, newly living alone in a big city and anxious about his choices, joins a group whose leader tells him exactly what to think and how to live; Fromm would call this escape through authoritarianism. Option b is right because Escape from Freedom is Fromm's landmark book. Exam trap: all four titles are real books about Freud, psychoanalysis or madness, so you must match title to author; none of the other three is by Fromm.", "topic": "Fromm's Escape from Freedom"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a part of Gerstmann syndrome?", "options": {"a": "Prosopagnosia.", "b": "Left right disorientation.", "c": "Agraphia.", "d": "Dyscalculia."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: prosopagnosia is not part of Gerstmann syndrome. Josef Gerstmann (1924) first reported finger agnosia and over the next few years linked three more signs to it, giving a tetrad that follows damage to the dominant (usually left) parietal lobe, especially the angular gyrus. The four signs are finger agnosia (inability to recognise, name or point to one's own or the examiner's fingers), agraphia (acquired loss of writing), acalculia or dyscalculia (acquired loss of calculation) and left-right disorientation. Gerstmann suggested a common disorder of the body schema behind them. The angular gyrus sits where the parietal, temporal and occipital lobes meet and supports reading, writing, number and spatial processing, so damage there disturbs all four. The pure tetrad is rare and often comes with mild aphasia. Prosopagnosia, face blindness, comes instead from damage to the fusiform face area on the underside of the temporal and occipital lobes, usually on the right or on both sides. Example: after a left parietal stroke, Meera cannot write her name, cannot work out change at the shop, muddles 'show me your left hand' and cannot say which finger the doctor is touching. Yet she recognises her son's face instantly. Option a is right because face recognition is a ventral temporo-occipital function and is not one of Gerstmann's four signs. Exam trap: dyscalculia and agraphia sound like separate learning disorders, but here they are acquired signs; remember the tetrad as fingers, writing, sums and left-right.", "topic": "Gerstmann syndrome tetrad"}, {"q": "Value Scales are associated with the personality psychologist:", "options": {"a": "Allport.", "b": "Cattell.", "c": "Eysenck.", "d": "Erikson."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: value scales are associated with Gordon Allport (1897 to 1967), through the Allport-Vernon-Lindzey Study of Values. Gordon Allport and Philip Vernon (1931) built the Study of Values; Gardner Lindzey joined for the revisions of 1951 and 1960. It measures the relative strength of six basic values taken from Types of Men by Eduard Spranger (1914 in German; 1928 in English): theoretical (the discovery of truth), economic (the useful and practical), aesthetic (form and harmony), social (love of people), political (power) and religious (unity with the whole of experience). Items are forced choices, so the scores are ipsative: a high score on one value must be balanced by lower scores on others. The profile therefore shows what matters most to the person compared with her own other values, not compared with other people. It was widely used in counselling, vocational guidance and research. Allport, the founder of trait psychology in America, held that a mature personality has a unifying philosophy of life, and values reveal it. Example: on the Study of Values, Riya scores highest on theoretical and lowest on economic, which fits her choice of research over a better-paid corporate job. Option a is right because the classic value scale carries Allport's name. Exam trap: the 16PF of Raymond Cattell (1949) and the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire of Hans Eysenck (1975) are trait inventories, not value scales, and Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994) built no test.", "topic": "Allport's Study of Values"}, {"q": "What is the resolution point of Erik Erikson's fourth stage of psychosocial development?", "options": {"a": "Fidelity.", "b": "Competence.", "c": "Will.", "d": "Purpose."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the fourth stage of Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994), industry versus inferiority, resolves in the virtue of competence. In Childhood and Society (1950) Erikson gave each of his eight psychosocial stages a crisis between two poles, and in Insight and Responsibility (1964) he named the virtue, or basic strength, that a good resolution leaves. Stage 4 covers the school years, about 6 to 11 or puberty, matching the latency period of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). School brings tools, tasks and classmates to compare oneself with. A child who finishes tasks, learns skills and earns recognition develops industry, a sense of being able to make things work; repeated failure, harsh comparison or being made to feel behind brings inferiority. Competence is the free use of skill and intelligence in completing tasks, unimpaired by a sense of inferiority. Teachers and peers become key relationships as the child's world widens beyond the family. The virtues to memorise, in order: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care, wisdom. Example: nine-year-old Kabir struggles with long division, but his teacher breaks it into steps and praises each success; by the end of term he volunteers to solve problems at the board, the sense of competence Erikson described. Option b is right because competence is the virtue of the fourth stage. Exam trap: the other three options are also Erikson virtues, from stage 2 (will), stage 3 (purpose) and stage 5 (fidelity), so count the stages carefully.", "topic": "Erikson's industry stage virtue"}, {"q": "\"the range of psychological functions between, on the one hand, those functions which the child is able to master without assistance, and on the other hand, those functions which the child can manage if given assistance\", is the concept of _____________ in cognitive development.", "options": {"a": "Zone of proximal development.", "b": "Central neoformation.", "c": "Conservation.", "d": "Central line of development."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: the range between what a child can master alone and what the child can manage with help is the zone of proximal development (ZPD) of Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934). Vygotsky, the Soviet psychologist, developed the idea in the early 1930s; it reached most English readers in the collection Mind in Society (1978). He described two children who both solve tasks set for eight-year-olds when working alone. Given hints, one solves tasks set for twelve-year-olds, the other only those for nine-year-olds. Their actual developmental level is the same, but their zones differ, so a single test score misses what each is ready to learn. The ZPD holds functions that are still ripening, 'buds' rather than 'fruits' of development. Good teaching aims inside it: what a child does with an adult or a more capable peer today, she can do alone tomorrow. Support fitted to the learner and withdrawn as skill grows was later named scaffolding by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976). Example: Meera cannot finish a 20-piece jigsaw alone, but succeeds when her father suggests starting with the edge pieces; that jigsaw lies in her ZPD. Option a is right because the stem quotes the classic definition of the ZPD. Exam trap: central neoformation and central line of development are genuine Vygotsky terms from his theory of age periods, so the author fits all three; but they name the main new structure of each age and the processes tied to it, not the gap between solo and assisted performance.", "topic": "Vygotsky's zone of proximal development"}, {"q": "A salesperson knocks on your door and requests you to buy the latest technology refrigerator of his \"internationally famed\" organization. When you turn him down, he requests you to buy a portable ice box. You find yourself obliged to comply. He has used, to near success:", "options": {"a": "Foot-in-the-door technique.", "b": "Door-in-the-face technique.", "c": "Low ball technique.", "d": "Reciprocity."}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: asking first for an expensive refrigerator, which you refuse, and then for a cheap ice box, which you feel obliged to buy, is the door-in-the-face technique. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1975) demonstrated it with students. Asked to counsel juvenile delinquents two hours a week for two years, every student refused. Asked next to take the delinquents on a single two-hour trip to the zoo, about 50% agreed, against under 17% of students asked only the small favour. The large request is meant to be refused; it makes the second, real request look like a concession. Because of the reciprocity norm (we repay favours and concessions), the target feels pressure to concede in turn, and by contrast the second request also seems smaller than it is. Guilt about the first refusal may add to the pull. The technique works best when the same person makes both requests, one soon after the other, and the first is not absurdly large. In the stem, the salesman's real goal was always the ice box. By retreating from the refrigerator he seemed to give something up, and you felt you owed him a yes. Option b is right because the order is a large refused request followed by the smaller target request. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door uses the reverse order (small then large), so check which request came first. Key note: the door-in-the-face works through the reciprocity norm (a concession is returned with a concession), so d names the mechanism, not the technique; b stands.", "topic": "Door-in-the-face technique"}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "options": {"a": "Algorithm.", "b": "Analogies.", "c": "Meta cognition.", "d": "Constructive process."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to monitor and correct learning, is metacognition, literally thinking about thinking. John Flavell (1979) made the term central to developmental psychology. Metacognition has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what you know about your own mind, about tasks and about strategies: for instance, that a long list needs more study than a short one. Metacognitive regulation is the active control of thinking: planning how to tackle a task, monitoring whether you understand as you go, and evaluating and correcting when you do not. Its memory branch is metamemory (Flavell, 1971), which includes the feeling of knowing and judgements of how well something has been learned. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts school success, so study-skills training, self-questioning and reciprocal teaching all aim to strengthen it. Example: while reading a chapter on neurons, Meera notices she cannot explain the last paragraph in her own words, so she rereads it and draws a diagram. That check-and-fix loop is metacognition. Option c is right because the stem describes awareness of one's own thinking that makes learning possible through self-correction. Exam trap: algorithms and analogies are problem-solving tools, things you think with, while metacognition is thinking about how you are thinking; 'constructive process' sounds learning-related but describes how memory and knowledge are built, not awareness of that building.", "topic": "Metacognition"}, {"q": "How many subjects would be required in a 3 × 3 × 2 factorial design with ten subjects per cell?", "options": {"a": "10.", "b": "18.", "c": "90.", "d": "180."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a 3 × 3 × 2 factorial design has 18 cells, and ten subjects per cell gives 180 subjects. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at once, combining every level of each factor with every level of the others. The notation tells you everything. The number of numerals is the number of factors, and each numeral is that factor's number of levels, so 3 × 3 × 2 means three factors: two with three levels and one with two. Each combination of levels is a cell (a treatment condition), and the number of cells is the product: 3 × 3 × 2 = 18. In a between-subjects design each participant serves in only one cell, so the total sample is the number of cells times the number of participants per cell: 18 × 10 = 180. A three-factor design yields three main effects, three two-way interactions and one three-way interaction, tested with factorial analysis of variance. Example: Kabir tests therapy type (CBT, interpersonal therapy, waiting list), dose (low, medium, high) and sex (male, female) on depression scores. With ten clients in each of the 18 combinations, he needs 180 clients. Option d is right because subjects equal cells multiplied by subjects per cell. Exam trap: each distractor stops a step early. 10 is one cell's sample, 18 is the number of cells, and 90 multiplies only two factors (3 × 3 = 9, times 10). Also do not add the numerals (3 + 3 + 2 = 8) or count the factors (3) instead of multiplying the levels.", "topic": "Factorial design sample size"}, {"q": "Which of the following clinical procedure/s is/are not based on classical conditioning? 1. Flooding, 2. Transference, 3. Client centered therapy, 4. Systematic desensitization", "options": {"a": "1 only.", "b": "2 and 3 only.", "c": "1, 2 and 3 only.", "d": "1, 3 and 4 only."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: transference (2) and client-centred therapy (3) are not based on classical conditioning, while flooding (1) and systematic desensitisation (4) are. Classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), explains how a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned one comes to trigger a conditioned response such as fear. If fears are learned this way, the same laws can unlearn them. Systematic desensitisation, from Joseph Wolpe (Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, 1958), uses counterconditioning: the relaxed client works up an anxiety hierarchy so that relaxation, a response incompatible with anxiety, replaces fear at each step (reciprocal inhibition). Flooding, and its imaginal form implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967), uses extinction: the client stays with the feared stimulus at full intensity, with escape blocked, until the conditioned fear dies away because no harm follows. Transference, by contrast, is a psychoanalytic concept from Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939): the patient redirects feelings about early figures onto the analyst, and interpreting this brings insight. Client-centred therapy, from Carl Rogers (1951), relies on empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence to free the client's actualising tendency. Example: Riya's fear of dogs could be treated by relaxed, graded steps towards a dog (desensitisation) or by staying beside a calm dog until her panic subsides (flooding); both are conditioning methods. Option b is right because only items 2 and 3 lack a conditioning basis. Exam trap: in 'not based on' items students pick the conditioning therapies by reflex; read the negative and choose the non-behavioural pair.", "topic": "Therapies and classical conditioning"}, {"q": "The aim of the ______ was to regulate training policies and programs in the field of disability rehabilitation and standardize training courses for professionals working with people with disabilities.", "options": {"a": "RPwD Act 2016.", "b": "RCI Act 1992.", "c": "PwD Act 1995.", "d": "MHCA 2017."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) Act 1992 was passed to regulate and standardise the training of professionals who work with people with disabilities. The Rehabilitation Council of India began in 1986 as a registered society, and the RCI Act 1992 made it a statutory body (in force from 22 June 1993); the Act was amended in 2000. Its jobs are to regulate and monitor training policies and programmes in disability rehabilitation, to set minimum standards and standardise syllabi, to recognise institutions and qualifications, and to keep the Central Rehabilitation Register of qualified professionals. Practising without registration is an offence under the Act. The RCI sits under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, not the Ministry of Health. Clinical psychologists, rehabilitation psychologists, special educators, audiologists and speech therapists all come under it, which is why an Indian clinical psychologist must hold an RCI-recognised qualification and be on the RCI register. For example, when Meera finishes her clinical psychology training and wants to practise, she applies for RCI registration; the Mental Healthcare Act then counts her as a mental health professional because of that qualification. Option b is right because the stem lists exactly the RCI Act's purposes: regulating training policies and programmes and standardising courses for rehabilitation professionals. Exam trap: the other three Acts are rights or care laws, not training laws. Examiners rely on the shared word 'disability' in the PwD and RPwD Acts to pull candidates away from the RCI Act, which is about the professionals, not the people they serve.", "topic": "Rehabilitation Council of India Act"}, {"q": "The first Disability Act came into effect in India in the year:", "options": {"a": "1968.", "b": "1990.", "c": "1995.", "d": "2016."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: India's first disability Act, the Persons with Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act, dates from 1995. Before it, India had no comprehensive law on the rights of disabled people; services were left to welfare schemes and charities. The 1995 Act recognised 7 disabilities: blindness, low vision, leprosy-cured, hearing impairment, locomotor disability, mental retardation and mental illness. It promised free education up to 18 years, a 3 percent reservation in identified government posts, barrier-free access and protection from discrimination, and it set up Chief Commissioners and State Commissioners to enforce these rights. It was replaced by the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016 (in force 2017), which widened the list to 21 disabilities, including autism spectrum disorder and specific learning disabilities, and raised the job reservation to 4 percent. A useful timeline: RCI Act 1992 (professionals), PwD Act 1995 (rights), National Trust Act 1999 (autism, cerebral palsy, intellectual and multiple disabilities), RPwD Act 2016 (rights, widened). Suppose Kabir, who has low vision, applied for a reserved government post in 2005; his claim would have rested on the 1995 Act. Option c is right because 1995 is the year of India's first comprehensive disability law. Exam trap: 2016 is the famous recent Act, so candidates who know only the RPwD Act pick d; it is the replacement, not the first. Key note: the 1995 Act was passed in December 1995 and came into force on 7 February 1996; the RCI Act 1992 came earlier but regulates professionals, not disability rights, and is not an option; 1995 stands.", "topic": "India's first disability Act"}, {"q": "\"Constructive alternativism\" is a core assumption of whose personality theory?", "options": {"a": "Skinner.", "b": "Kelly.", "c": "Goldstein.", "d": "Rogers."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: constructive alternativism is the core philosophical assumption of George Kelly's psychology of personal constructs. George Kelly (1955), in The Psychology of Personal Constructs, pictured every person as a scientist who builds theories about the world, tests them through prediction and revises them when they fail. The theories are personal constructs: bipolar dimensions such as friendly versus hostile, or clever versus dull, through which a person construes events. Constructive alternativism is the assumption that any event can always be construed in more than one way, so no one is a prisoner of their past or of a single interpretation; people can always re-construe their lives. Kelly built the rest of the theory on this base: a fundamental postulate ('a person's processes are psychologically channelised by the ways in which he anticipates events') and eleven corollaries. He measured constructs with the Role Construct Repertory Test (the Rep Test) and treated clients with fixed-role therapy, in which a client lives out a new character sketch for a few weeks to try a different way of construing. Take Arjun, who construes his failed exam as proof that he is stupid. Kelly would say this is only one construction; Arjun could re-construe it as evidence that he studied the wrong way, and that alternative opens new action. Option b is right because constructive alternativism is Kelly's own term and the foundation of his cognitive theory of personality. Exam trap: 'constructive' and 'alternative' sound like the growth language of humanistic psychology, so candidates drift to d; but Carl Rogers (1951) speaks of the actualising tendency and conditions of worth, not of constructs.", "topic": "Kelly's constructive alternativism"}, {"q": "'Moses and Monotheism' is authored by:", "options": {"a": "Heinz Hartman.", "b": "Sigmund Freud.", "c": "Carl G Jung.", "d": "Victor Frankl."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Moses and Monotheism (1939) is Sigmund Freud's last book, published in the year he died. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) began it in Vienna and finished it in London after fleeing the Nazis in 1938. In it he argued that Moses was an Egyptian nobleman who passed on the one-god religion of the Pharaoh Akhenaten to the Hebrews, and that the Hebrews later killed him. The guilt over this killing was repressed and returned, like a neurotic symptom, as the religion's lasting sense of guilt. The book applies psychoanalysis to religion and history, and it belongs to a group of Freud's cultural works: Totem and Taboo (1913, the primal horde and the killing of the father), The Future of an Illusion (1927, religion as wish fulfilment) and Civilization and Its Discontents (1930, instinct against civilisation). A handy chain: Totem and Taboo starts the father-killing story, and Moses and Monotheism ends it. If Riya is asked which Freud book calls religion an illusion, the answer is The Future of an Illusion, not Moses and Monotheism, even though both deal with religion. Option b is right because Freud is the author, and the book's theme, repressed guilt returning in religious form, is classic Freudian reasoning. Exam trap: Carl Jung (1938) also wrote widely about religion, so a religion-themed title pulls candidates towards c, and Viktor Frankl's (1946) spiritual themes make d sound plausible; neither wrote this book.", "topic": "Freud's cultural works"}, {"q": "The contention that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience, attests to which of the following hypotheses:", "options": {"a": "Universality hypothesis of facial expressions.", "b": "Emotional specificity hypothesis of ANS activity.", "c": "Emotional generality hypothesis of ANS activity.", "d": "Facial feedback hypothesis."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the idea that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience is the facial feedback hypothesis. The hypothesis says that the face does not only show an emotion; signals from the facial muscles and skin feed back to the brain and shape, or even start, the feeling. Charles Darwin (1872) suggested that freely expressing an emotion intensifies it, William James (1884) held that feelings follow bodily changes, and Silvan Tomkins (1962) made the face the seat of affect, which is the modern root of the hypothesis. The classic test is the pen study of Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper (1988): people who held a pen in their teeth (forcing a smile without knowing it) rated cartoons funnier than people who held it in their lips (which blocks a smile). A large multi-lab replication in 2016 did not find the effect, and later meta-analyses suggest it is real but small. So if Kabir forces a broad smile before an interview, the hypothesis predicts that he will feel a little calmer and happier. Option d is right because the stem describes influence running from face to feeling, which is the defining claim of facial feedback. Exam trap: the universality hypothesis also concerns faces, so it is the near miss; but universality is about whether expressions are produced and recognised the same way across cultures, not about the face changing the feeling. Options b and c are about the autonomic nervous system, not the face.", "topic": "Facial feedback hypothesis"}, {"q": "Replacing the fragmented self with the cohesive self is the primary developmental and therapeutic goal of:", "options": {"a": "Kernberg.", "b": "Kohut.", "c": "Malan.", "d": "Horowitz."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: replacing a fragmented self with a cohesive self is the central developmental and therapeutic goal of Heinz Kohut's self psychology. Heinz Kohut (1971), in The Analysis of the Self and later The Restoration of the Self (1977), argued that narcissism has its own healthy line of development, not only a pathological one. The child's self grows through selfobjects: people experienced as part of the self because they perform functions the child cannot yet perform alone. Three selfobject needs must be met: mirroring (the parent's delighted response that confirms the child's 'Look at me!'), idealising (merging with a calm, strong parent) and twinship (feeling essentially alike with others). When parents respond well but imperfectly, each bearable failure lets the child take over the soothing function, which Kohut called transmuting internalisation, and this builds a cohesive self. When mirroring and idealising fail chronically, the self stays fragmented and enfeebled, and narcissistic problems follow. In therapy the analyst offers empathic attunement; the client forms selfobject transferences (mirror, idealising, twinship), and the therapist's small, understood empathic failures repeat the healthy process, so the self becomes cohesive. Imagine Kabir, who feels he falls apart whenever his boss ignores him; a Kohutian therapist would read this as a fragile, fragmenting self and work through empathy to strengthen its cohesion. Option b is right because 'fragmented self' and 'cohesive self' are Kohut's signature terms. Exam trap: Otto Kernberg (1975) also wrote on narcissism and borderline states, so a is the near miss; but Kernberg's goal is integrating split good and bad images of self and others, not building self cohesion through selfobjects.", "topic": "Kohut's self psychology"}, {"q": "Which of the following names is not associated with Existential Therapy?", "options": {"a": "Rollo May.", "b": "Sandor Ferenczi.", "c": "Carl Jaspers.", "d": "R. D. Laing."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Sandor Ferenczi was a psychoanalyst, not an existential therapist. Sandor Ferenczi (1873 to 1933) was a Hungarian member of Sigmund Freud's (1856 to 1939) inner circle who experimented with the technique of analysis: an active technique, a warmer relaxation technique, even mutual analysis, and a 1932 paper on the 'confusion of tongues' between adults and children that took childhood sexual trauma seriously. He is a forerunner of relational psychoanalysis, not of existentialism. Existential therapy starts from philosophy, above all Martin Heidegger's Being and Time (1927), and treats a person as being-in-the-world facing the givens that Irvin Yalom (1980) named as death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Its names include Karl Jaspers (1913), the German psychiatrist and philosopher whose General Psychopathology brought phenomenology into psychiatry; Ludwig Binswanger (1881 to 1966) and Medard Boss (1963), who built Daseinsanalysis; Rollo May (1958), who brought existential ideas to America; Viktor Frankl (1946), the founder of logotherapy; and R. D. Laing (1960) in Britain. Suppose Meera is anxious after a friend's death; an existential therapist would help her face mortality and choose how to live, not interpret her childhood drives. Option b is right because Ferenczi's work sits wholly inside psychoanalysis, while the other three are standard names in the existential tradition. Exam trap: Ferenczi stressed warmth and the real relationship, which sounds humanistic, and Laing is often filed only under anti-psychiatry; but Laing's The Divided Self was openly existential and phenomenological, so he belongs here, and Ferenczi does not.", "topic": "Names in existential therapy"}, {"q": "A more preferred activity can be used to reinforce a less preferred activity. This is referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Trace conditioning.", "b": "Premack's principle.", "c": "Principle of stimulus generalization.", "d": "Differential Reinforcement of other behaviour."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: using a more preferred activity to reinforce a less preferred one is the Premack principle. David Premack (1959) argued that reinforcement is relative: any behaviour that an individual does more often when free to choose (a high-probability behaviour) can reinforce one done less often (a low-probability behaviour), if access to the first depends on doing the second. So no activity is a reinforcer in itself; it depends on which is more probable for that individual at that time. In his rat work, for a thirsty rat drinking reinforced running, but for a rat deprived of exercise running reinforced drinking. Parents know the rule as 'Grandma's rule': first eat your vegetables, then you get dessert. Behaviour therapists use it in classrooms, wards and token systems, where a favourite activity is earned by completing a disliked task. For example, Riya's mother tells her she can watch her favourite show only after finishing her mathematics homework; the preferred show strengthens the less preferred homework. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly Premack's rule of high-probability behaviour reinforcing low-probability behaviour. Exam trap: the direction matters, since a less preferred activity cannot reinforce a more preferred one. DRO also uses reinforcement, so candidates who see the word 'reinforce' pick d, but DRO rewards the absence of a problem behaviour, not access to a preferred activity.", "topic": "Premack principle"}, {"q": "\"Moving towards people, moving away from people, moving against people\" are constituents of whose classification of pattern of needs:", "options": {"a": "Dollard-Miller.", "b": "Klein.", "c": "Horney.", "d": "Fromm."}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: moving towards, against and away from people are Karen Horney's three neurotic trends. Karen Horney (1937), in The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, argued that personality is shaped mainly by social relationships, not sexual instincts. A child whose parents show indifference (no genuine warmth, broken promises, favouritism, ridicule) feels basic hostility, represses it out of helplessness or fear, and is left with basic anxiety: a pervasive feeling of being lonely and helpless in a hostile world. To cope, the child adopts strategies that harden into ten neurotic needs, such as needs for affection, a dominant partner, power, prestige, self-sufficiency and perfection. In Our Inner Conflicts (1945) she grouped these needs into three trends. The compliant type moves towards people, seeking love and approval; the aggressive type moves against people, seeking power and admiration; the detached type moves away from people, seeking independence and distance. A healthy person uses all three flexibly; a neurotic person is locked into one, and the clash among them is the core of neurosis. For example, Arjun agrees with everyone and panics if a friend seems annoyed (towards), while his brother Kabir turns every conversation into a contest (against). Option c is right because the stem quotes Horney's own triad. Exam trap: Erich Fromm (1941) also listed social strategies (mechanisms of escape and character orientations), and both were neo-Freudians of the cultural school, so d is the near miss; but the towards, against and away wording is Horney's alone.", "topic": "Horney's neurotic trends"}, {"q": "In as much as the first three stages of Kohlberg's theory of moral development represent a shift from unquestioning obedience to a relativistic outlook and a concern towards good motives, they echo whose theory?", "options": {"a": "Vygotsky.", "b": "Piaget.", "c": "Harlow.", "d": "Bowlby."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the first three of Kohlberg's stages echo Jean Piaget's theory of moral development. Jean Piaget (1932), in The Moral Judgment of the Child, watched Swiss children play marbles and told them paired stories (John breaks fifteen cups by accident; Henry breaks one cup while sneaking jam). He described heteronomous morality, or moral realism, from about 5 to 10 years: rules come from adults and cannot change, acts are judged by their consequences, and punishment is expected to follow wrongdoing automatically (immanent justice). Then comes autonomous morality: rules are agreements that can be changed, and acts are judged by intentions. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), working from Piaget, used dilemmas such as Heinz stealing a drug to save his wife and found three levels with six stages. Stage 1 (punishment and obedience) is unquestioning obedience to avoid punishment, close to heteronomy. Stage 2 (instrumental exchange) is a relativistic outlook: right is whatever serves each person's interest, as in 'you scratch my back and I'll scratch yours'. Stage 3 (good boy, good girl) judges by good motives and approval, close to Piaget's autonomous concern with intentions. So the march from obedience to relativism to good motives is a finer-grained version of Piaget's shift. For example, at 6 Meera says John is naughtier because more cups broke; at 10 she says Henry is naughtier because he meant to misbehave. Option b is right because Kohlberg openly built his stages on Piaget's two moralities. Exam trap: Lev Vygotsky (1934) is the other big developmental name and sits beside Piaget in every syllabus, but he wrote no stage theory of moral reasoning.", "topic": "Piaget's roots of Kohlberg"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not considered a part of the reticular activating system?", "options": {"a": "Locus coeruleus.", "b": "Intralaminar nuclei of the thalamus.", "c": "Lateral geniculate nuclei of thalamus.", "d": "Mesencephalic reticular nuclei."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus is not part of the reticular activating system; it is a specific sensory relay for vision. The reticular formation is a net-like core of about 100 small nuclei running through the brainstem from the medulla to the midbrain. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), keeps the cortex awake and alert. Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949) showed this by electrically stimulating the reticular formation of sleeping cats: the cats woke, and their EEG switched from slow synchronised waves to the fast desynchronised pattern of alertness. Damage to the system causes coma. The RAS reaches the cortex by two routes: a dorsal route through the intralaminar (non-specific) thalamic nuclei, which project diffusely to wide areas of cortex, and a ventral route through the hypothalamus and basal forebrain. Its components include the mesencephalic (midbrain) reticular nuclei, the locus coeruleus (noradrenaline), the raphe nuclei (serotonin) and cholinergic nuclei at the junction of pons and midbrain. The lateral geniculate nucleus is different in kind: a specific thalamic nucleus carrying a point-to-point map of the retina to the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe. If Arjun dozes in a lecture and a loud bang wakes him, his RAS raises arousal; his lateral geniculate nucleus only passes on what his eyes see. Option c is right because the lateral geniculate is a specific visual relay, not a diffuse arousal nucleus. Exam trap: two thalamic options appear, and candidates reason that the thalamus is only a relay; but the intralaminar nuclei are the non-specific nuclei that belong to the RAS, while the geniculate nuclei are specific sensory relays.", "topic": "Reticular activating system parts"}, {"q": "A Census would entail which sampling technique?", "options": {"a": "Survey.", "b": "Quota Sampling.", "c": "Simple Random Sampling.", "d": "None of the above."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a census involves no sampling technique at all, because every member of the population is counted. A population is the whole group a researcher wants to describe (all households in India, all students in a college). A census is complete enumeration: data are collected from every element of that population. A sample is a smaller subset chosen by some rule, and a sampling technique is that rule. Probability methods give each element a known chance of selection: simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling. Non-probability methods do not: convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling. Because a census studies everyone, it has no sampling error; its figures are population parameters, not estimates. It can still suffer non-sampling error, such as households missed by enumerators (coverage error), people who refuse (non-response) and badly worded questions (measurement error). Censuses are slow and costly, which is why India's national Census is held only once every ten years while sample surveys fill the gaps. If Kabir, a college counsellor, wants the stress level of all 300 students, he can give the questionnaire to all 300 (a census) or pick 60 by lottery (simple random sampling). Option d is right because counting every unit leaves nothing to sample. Exam trap: quota and stratified samples are often matched to census proportions, so candidates link the census to b or c. Key note: a census is complete enumeration, every member of the population is counted, so no sampling technique is involved; a census is a kind of survey, which is why a is tempting, but a survey is a method of data collection, not a sampling technique, so d stands.", "topic": "Census versus sampling"}, {"q": "As per the NIMH classification of Intelligence quotient, a person with an IQ of 48 would fall in the category of:", "options": {"a": "Borderline IQ.", "b": "Moderate IQ.", "c": "Severe IQ.", "d": "Mild IQ."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: an IQ of 48 falls in the moderate range of intellectual disability on the NIMH classification. The National Institute for the Mentally Handicapped (NIMH, Secunderabad), now the National Institute for the Empowerment of Persons with Intellectual Disabilities (NIEPID), uses the World Health Organization's ICD-10 bands, which Indian disability certification also followed. On a test with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, the bands are: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34 and profound below 20. Borderline intellectual functioning, 70 to 84 (some sources 71 to 84), is below average but is not intellectual disability. DSM-IV-TR used overlapping bands (mild from 50 or 55 to about 70, moderate from 35 or 40 to 50 or 55), and DSM-5 (2013) now grades severity by adaptive functioning, not IQ, because support needs depend on daily skills. The old educational labels follow the same bands: mild was 'educable' (academic skills to about sixth grade), moderate 'trainable' (self-care and simple supervised work, academics to about second grade), and severe and profound needed extensive support. So if Meera, aged 14, scores 48 on a standardised test and has matching adaptive deficits, she is in the moderate band: she can learn self-care and simple routines but needs supervised support. Option b is right because 48 lies inside 35 to 49. Exam trap: 48 is only two points below 50, so candidates who misremember the bands, or who use the DSM-IV-TR overlap, choose mild; on the ICD-10 and NIMH bands mild starts at 50.", "topic": "Intellectual disability IQ bands"}, {"q": "Who among the following is credited with the development of Logotherapy?", "options": {"a": "Fritz Perls.", "b": "Eric Berne.", "c": "Viktor Frankl.", "d": "Rollo May."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: logotherapy was developed by Viktor Frankl. Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997) was a Viennese psychiatrist and neurologist whose logotherapy (from the Greek logos, 'meaning') is often called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy, after Sigmund Freud's (1900) psychoanalysis with its will to pleasure and Alfred Adler's (1912) individual psychology with its will to power. Frankl held that the primary human motive is the will to meaning. When it is frustrated, a person falls into an existential vacuum (boredom, emptiness) or a noogenic neurosis, a neurosis arising from the spiritual dimension rather than from conflict between drives. His Man's Search for Meaning (1946) drew on his survival of Nazi concentration camps, where he saw that prisoners who kept a purpose were more likely to endure. Meaning can be found in three ways: by creating a work or doing a deed, by experiencing something or loving someone, and by the attitude taken to unavoidable suffering. Two logotherapy techniques are well known: paradoxical intention (deliberately wishing for the feared symptom, such as trying to stay awake to beat insomnia) and dereflection (turning attention away from oneself towards a task or another person). If Arjun, a retired teacher, feels his life is pointless, a logotherapist would help him find meaning in mentoring others. Option c is right because Frankl coined the term and founded logotherapy. Exam trap: Rollo May (1950) is also an existential therapist, so d is the near miss; but May wrote on anxiety, will and love and founded no school called logotherapy.", "topic": "Frankl's logotherapy"}, {"q": "Who founded the first psychology laboratory?", "options": {"a": "Ivan Pavlov.", "b": "William James.", "c": "Sigmund Freud.", "d": "Carl Jung."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: among the options given, William James is credited with the first psychology laboratory, the small demonstration laboratory he set up at Harvard in 1875. William James (1842 to 1910) was the leading American pioneer of psychology and the father of functionalism; his Principles of Psychology (1890) defined the field in the United States. In 1875 he was given a room at Harvard with apparatus for showing students experiments on sensation and physiology. It was used for teaching, not for a programme of original research. The usual answer to 'who founded the first psychology laboratory' is Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who opened the first formal research laboratory at the University of Leipzig, trained students such as Edward Titchener (1898) and James McKeen Cattell (1890), and studied sensation and reaction time with controlled introspection. In America, G. Stanley Hall (1883) opened the first research laboratory at Johns Hopkins University. So the sequence to remember is: James's Harvard demonstration room (1875), Wundt's Leipzig research laboratory (1879), Hall's Johns Hopkins laboratory (1883) and, in India, Narendra Nath Sengupta's laboratory at Calcutta University (1915). If Riya sees Wundt among the options she should choose him; here he is missing, so James wins as the only laboratory founder offered. Option b is right because James's 1875 room is the earliest psychology laboratory among the four names. Exam trap: Ivan Pavlov (1897) ran a famous laboratory, so a tempts; but it was a physiology laboratory studying digestion and conditioning, and it came later. Key note: the standard answer, Wilhelm Wundt (Leipzig, 1879, the first formal research laboratory), is not among the options. William James set up a small demonstration laboratory at Harvard in 1875, which is why he is the answer here; b stands.", "topic": "First psychology laboratory"}, {"q": "The structuralist primarily used the method of research called:", "options": {"a": "Free association.", "b": "Survey.", "c": "Introspection.", "d": "Observation."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the structuralists relied mainly on introspection, the trained, systematic observation of one's own conscious experience. Structuralism was the school of Edward Titchener (1898, when he named it), an Englishman trained by Wilhelm Wundt (1879) at Leipzig who taught at Cornell University. Its aim was to break consciousness into its basic elements, as a chemist breaks a compound into elements: sensations, images and feelings, each described by attributes such as quality, intensity, duration and clearness. The method was introspection by highly trained observers, who reported the raw elements of an experience under controlled conditions. They had to avoid the stimulus error: naming the object (an apple) instead of describing the sensations (red, round, smooth). Wundt had used a stricter, more limited experimental self-observation of immediate experience, and his own system is better called voluntarism; Titchener stretched introspection much further. Introspection declined because trained observers in different laboratories could not agree and no one could check their reports, as the imageless thought controversy with the Würzburg school showed. The functionalists, William James (1890) and John Dewey (1896), asked what consciousness is for, and John B. Watson (1913) rejected introspection altogether. For example, a trained observer like Meera, tasting lemonade, would report 'sour, cold, moderately intense, fading' rather than 'it is lemonade'. Option c is right because introspection was the defining method of structuralism. Exam trap: free association also looks inward, so a tempts; but free association is Sigmund Freud's clinical method of the 1890s for reaching the unconscious, while introspection describes conscious experience.", "topic": "Structuralism and introspection"}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses, caused by long-term abuse of alcohol, often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease.", "b": "Parkinson's disease.", "c": "Huntington's disease.", "d": "Korsakoff's disease."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: Korsakoff's syndrome, a result of long-term alcohol misuse, often brings profound retrograde as well as anterograde amnesia. Sergei Korsakoff (1887), a Russian neuropsychiatrist, described patients, mostly heavy drinkers, who could not lay down new memories and had lost large stretches of their past. The cause is deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1): alcohol supplies calories without vitamins and impairs thiamine absorption, and neurons need thiamine to use glucose. It usually follows Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke (1881), an acute emergency with confusion, ataxia and ophthalmoplegia (paralysed eye movements); untreated survivors settle into Korsakoff's amnesia, and the continuum is called Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial (mediodorsal) thalamus, so it is a diencephalic amnesia. The picture is severe anterograde amnesia, temporally graded retrograde amnesia (older memories are better kept than recent ones, and the loss may reach back decades), apathy, poor insight and confabulation: plausible but false stories that fill memory gaps, told sincerely. Short-term memory span and procedural learning are relatively spared, and early thiamine injections can halt the progression. For example, Arjun, aged 55 with twenty years of heavy drinking, cannot recall this morning's visitors, believes it is still years earlier and confidently describes a trip he never took. Option d is right because Korsakoff's syndrome is the alcohol-linked amnesic disorder with profound retrograde loss. Exam trap: Alzheimer's disease also causes severe amnesia, so a tempts; but it is not caused by alcohol, and it begins with loss of recent episodic memory before spreading to language and other cognition.", "topic": "Korsakoff's syndrome"}, {"q": "Who introduced Behaviourism?", "options": {"a": "John B Watson.", "b": "William Wundt.", "c": "Lev Vygotsky.", "d": "Alfred Binet."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: John B. Watson introduced behaviourism. John B. Watson (1913), in his paper 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', often called the behaviourist manifesto, declared that psychology should be a purely objective, experimental branch of natural science whose goal is the prediction and control of behaviour. He rejected introspection and consciousness as unscientific, because no one else can check them, and studied only observable stimuli and responses. With Rosalie Rayner (1920) he conditioned fear in an 11-month-old boy, Little Albert, by pairing a white rat with a loud noise, and the fear generalised to other furry objects. He also boasted that, given a dozen healthy infants, he could train any one of them to become any type of specialist regardless of talent or ancestry, a statement of extreme environmentalism. Behaviourism built on the conditioning work of Ivan Pavlov (1897) and Edward Thorndike (1898), and it was carried forward by the neobehaviourists Clark Hull (1943), Edward Tolman (1932) and B. F. Skinner (1938), whose radical behaviourism studied operant conditioning. For example, if Riya develops a fear of dogs after one bit her, a Watsonian explanation is classical conditioning, with no reference to her inner thoughts. Option a is right because Watson founded and named behaviourism. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (1879) is offered because he founded experimental psychology, and candidates equate 'founder of the laboratory' with 'founder of every school'; but behaviourism began as a revolt against the introspective psychology of Wundt and Edward Titchener (1898).", "topic": "Watson and behaviourism"}, {"q": "Which of the following cells produce myelin in the CNS?", "options": {"a": "Schwann cells.", "b": "Astrocytes.", "c": "Microglia.", "d": "Oligodendrocytes."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: oligodendrocytes produce myelin in the central nervous system (CNS). Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped around axons in many layers. It is interrupted at gaps called nodes of Ranvier, so the action potential jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction), which makes conduction much faster and saves energy. Myelin is made not by neurons but by glial cells. In the CNS (brain and spinal cord), each oligodendrocyte sends out several arms and wraps segments of many different axons. In the peripheral nervous system (PNS), each Schwann cell wraps a single segment of one axon, and Schwann cells also guide the regrowth of cut peripheral nerves, one reason PNS damage can recover while CNS damage usually does not. The other glia have different jobs: astrocytes (the largest, star-shaped) help form the blood-brain barrier, buffer potassium and recycle glutamate; microglia (the smallest) are the brain's immune cells, clearing debris and pruning synapses; ependymal cells line the ventricles and help make cerebrospinal fluid. Disease follows the same split: multiple sclerosis destroys CNS myelin made by oligodendrocytes, while Guillain-Barre syndrome attacks PNS myelin made by Schwann cells. So when Meera's neurologist explains her multiple sclerosis, it is oligodendrocyte myelin that is being lost. Option d is right because oligodendrocytes are the myelin-forming cells of the CNS. Exam trap: Schwann cells also make myelin, so a is the near miss; the CNS versus PNS split is exactly what the stem tests, and swapping the two is the classic error.", "topic": "Myelin-forming glial cells"}, {"q": "As per the Temperament and Character Inventory an individual who scores high on Novelty seeking but low on both Harm avoidance and Reward dependence is likely to have:", "options": {"a": "Histrionic personality.", "b": "Obsessional personality.", "c": "Anti-social personality.", "d": "Passive aggressive personality."}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: high novelty seeking with low harm avoidance and low reward dependence is the profile C. Robert Cloninger linked to antisocial personality. C. Robert Cloninger (1987) proposed a tridimensional model in which three heritable temperament dimensions each depend mainly on one transmitter system: novelty seeking (exploratory excitement, impulsiveness, quick temper; dopamine), harm avoidance (worry, fear of uncertainty, shyness, fatigability; serotonin) and reward dependence (sentimentality, attachment, dependence on approval; noradrenaline). He later added persistence and three character dimensions (self-directedness, cooperativeness, self-transcendence) in the Temperament and Character Inventory (1993). Crossing high and low on the three temperaments gives eight combinations, which he matched to personality types. Antisocial: high novelty seeking, low harm avoidance, low reward dependence. Histrionic: high novelty seeking, low harm avoidance, high reward dependence. Passive-aggressive: high on all three. Obsessional: low novelty seeking, high harm avoidance, low reward dependence. The logic is easy to see: the antisocial person chases thrills (high novelty seeking), feels little fear of punishment (low harm avoidance) and does not care about others' approval (low reward dependence). So if Kabir constantly seeks excitement, shows no anxiety about consequences and is indifferent to praise or disapproval, the model predicts antisocial traits. Option c is right because the stem gives the antisocial combination exactly. Exam trap: histrionic people are also impulsive thrill-seekers with little anxiety, so a is the near miss; the deciding dimension is reward dependence, which is high in histrionic personality (craving attention and approval) and low in antisocial personality.", "topic": "Cloninger's temperament profiles"}, {"q": "An oil painting itself can include all the following cues to depth perception except:", "options": {"a": "Linear perspective.", "b": "Retinal disparity.", "c": "Texture gradient.", "d": "Relative image size."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: an oil painting can contain every pictorial depth cue, but it cannot contain retinal disparity, which needs two eyes looking at a real three-dimensional scene. The brain builds depth from cues, and only two are binocular, needing both eyes. In retinal (binocular) disparity, the eyes, about 6 centimetres apart, receive slightly different images of a near object, and the nearer the object, the bigger the difference; the brain fuses the two images into the solid depth called stereopsis. In convergence, the eyes turn inward to fixate near objects. All the other cues are monocular, and most are pictorial cues, the tools painters have used since the Renaissance: linear perspective (parallel lines such as rail tracks converge towards the horizon), texture gradient (pebbles look smaller and denser with distance), relative size (of two similar objects, the smaller image seems farther), interposition (a partly hidden object is behind), shading, height in the visual field and aerial perspective (distant hills look hazy and bluish). A flat canvas sends both eyes essentially the same image, so it carries no disparity; that is why a painting looks flatter than a real scene, and why the stereoscope of Charles Wheatstone (1838) shows each eye a slightly different picture to create disparity artificially. If Riya paints a road shrinking towards a mountain, she can use perspective, texture and size, but her painting cannot give her viewer's two eyes different views. Option b is right because retinal disparity comes from the viewer's two eyes, not from anything that can be painted. Exam trap: linear perspective, texture gradient and relative size sound technical, but they are exactly the pictorial cues a painter draws; the one binocular cue is the exception.", "topic": "Binocular versus pictorial depth cues"}, {"q": "Which of the following sequence is correct?", "options": {"a": "Oral phase, anal phase, latency, phallic, genital.", "b": "Anal, oral, phallic, latency, genital.", "c": "Oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital.", "d": "Oral, phallic, anal, genital, latency."}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Sigmund Freud's psychosexual stages run oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud (1905), in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, argued that the libido, the energy of the sexual instinct, centres on a different erogenous zone at each stage of childhood, and that too much or too little gratification at a stage leaves a fixation that shapes adult personality. The oral stage (birth to about 1 year or 18 months) centres on the mouth, sucking and biting; fixation is linked to dependence, smoking or sarcasm. The anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) centres on bowel control and toilet training; fixation gives the anal-retentive (orderly, stingy, stubborn) or anal-expulsive (messy, careless) character. The phallic stage (about 3 to 5 or 6 years) centres on the genitals and the Oedipus complex, resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego. The latency stage (about 6 to puberty) is a quiet time when sexual energy is diverted into school, friends and skills; Freud called it a period rather than a true stage. The genital stage (puberty onward) brings mature sexual interest in others. A memory aid: 'Old Age Pensioners Love Grapes'. For example, a 4-year-old like Kabir who announces he will marry his mother is in the phallic stage; at 9, absorbed in cricket and friends, he is in latency. Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps Freud's order. Exam trap: option a puts latency before phallic, because candidates remember latency as early and quiet; but latency follows the Oedipal crisis of the phallic stage.", "topic": "Freud's psychosexual stages"}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is Wernicke area located in?", "options": {"a": "Frontal Lobe.", "b": "Parietal Lobe.", "c": "Temporal Lobe.", "d": "Occipital Lobe."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: Wernicke's area lies in the temporal lobe, in the posterior part of the left superior temporal gyrus, next to the primary auditory cortex. Carl Wernicke (1874), a German neurologist, described patients with damage here who spoke fluently, with normal rhythm and grammar, but whose speech made little sense (word salad and made-up words called neologisms) and who could not understand what was said to them. This is Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia. Its partner is Broca's area, in the left inferior frontal gyrus of the frontal lobe, described by Paul Broca (1861) from his patient 'Tan': damage there gives Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia, with slow, effortful, telegraphic speech but fairly good comprehension. The two areas are joined by the arcuate fasciculus, whose damage causes conduction aphasia (poor repetition), a core idea of Norman Geschwind's (1965) Wernicke-Geschwind model. So if Meera, after a stroke, chatters fluently but cannot follow instructions, suspect her left temporal lobe. Option c is right because Wernicke's area is temporal cortex. Exam trap: the area borders the parietal lobe, and the nearby angular gyrus (reading and writing) is parietal, so b tempts; and Broca's frontal location is often swapped in for a.", "topic": "Wernicke's area location"}, {"q": "What is the full form of EEG?", "options": {"a": "Electroencephalography.", "b": "Electroengiography.", "c": "Electroergography.", "d": "Electroencephalogram."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: EEG stands for electroencephalography, the method of recording the brain's electrical activity through electrodes on the scalp. The word breaks into electro (electrical), encephalo (brain, from the Greek for 'in the head') and graphy (recording). Hans Berger (1929), a German psychiatrist, published the first human recordings, which he had begun in 1924, and described the alpha rhythm of relaxed wakefulness. The EEG picks up the summed activity of many cortical neurons firing together. It has excellent temporal resolution (milliseconds) but poor spatial resolution, and it is the standard test for epilepsy and for sleep staging, where beta, alpha, theta and delta waves mark different states. Look-alike terms follow the same pattern: ECG (heart), EMG (muscles) and EOG (eye movements). Option a is right because electroencephalography is the full form of EEG as a technique. Exam trap: b and c are invented look-alikes that echo angiography (imaging blood vessels) and ergography (recording muscular work), and the real near miss is d. Key note: EEG is used for both electroencephalography (the method of recording the brain's electrical activity through scalp electrodes, Hans Berger, 1924/1929) and electroencephalogram (the recording itself), so d is also a correct expansion; as the name of the technique, a stands.", "topic": "EEG full form"}, {"q": "According to Piaget, children are able to grasp the concept of object permanence in the:", "options": {"a": "Formal operational stage.", "b": "Pre-operational stage.", "c": "Sensorimotor stage.", "d": "Concrete operational stage."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, object permanence is achieved during the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years). Object permanence is the understanding that objects go on existing when they can no longer be seen, heard or touched. Jean Piaget (1937), in The Construction of Reality in the Child, drew on observations of his own three children and traced the concept across the six sensorimotor substages. Before about 4 months a toy covered by a cloth seems to cease to exist, and the infant looks away. From about 4 to 8 months the infant retrieves a partly hidden toy. From about 8 to 12 months the infant searches for a fully hidden toy but makes the A-not-B error: after finding it several times under cloth A, the infant keeps searching at A even after watching it hidden under cloth B. From 12 to 18 months the infant follows visible moves but fails invisible ones, and by 18 to 24 months, with mental representation, object permanence is complete. Later research suggests Piaget underestimated infants: Renee Baillargeon (1987), using the violation-of-expectation (drawbridge) method, found that infants of 3.5 to 4.5 months look longer at events in which a hidden box seems to vanish. For example, when 9-month-old Arjun watches his rattle go under a pillow, he lifts the pillow to find it, something a 3-month-old would not do. Option c is right because object permanence is the landmark achievement of the sensorimotor stage. Exam trap: the preoperational stage follows next and is defined by symbols, so b tempts candidates who link mental representation with preoperational thought; but permanence is complete before the preoperational stage begins.", "topic": "Object permanence in Piaget"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jemas-2022": [{"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence:", "concept": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Cattell (1963) and his student John Horn (1966) split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, such as spotting the rule in a matrix puzzle or working out an unfamiliar gadget. It depends heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience. Because it keeps growing with use, it holds steady or even rises into old age. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) followed adults for decades and found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood, while perceptual speed declines from early adulthood. Timothy Salthouse (1996) traced much of the fluid loss to a general slowing of processing. Example: Riya's grandmother, aged 72, finishes the newspaper crossword before anyone else and knows every rare word (crystallised), but takes far longer than Riya to learn a new phone app (fluid). Option c is right because it puts the declining ability (fluid) in the first blank and the stable one (crystallised) in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: examiners reverse the order (option d) to catch students who remember the pair but not which one falls; think 'fluid flows away, crystals last'. They also invent a 'fixed' intelligence, which is not a type in any theory; it echoes Carol Dweck's (2006) fixed mindset, a belief about ability rather than a kind of ability.", "traps": {"a": "Fluid is correct for the first blank, but 'fixed' intelligence is not a type in Raymond Cattell and John Horn's theory or any other; the word borrows from Carol Dweck's fixed mindset, which is a belief about ability.", "b": "This invents a 'fixed' intelligence and also places fluid intelligence in the stable slot, when fluid reasoning is precisely the ability that declines with age.", "d": "This reverses the finding of Raymond Cattell and John Horn: crystallised knowledge holds or grows into old age, while fluid reasoning is what declines."}}, {"q": "Grief refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: grief is the intense emotional state that follows the death of a lover, friend or relative. Three words are often mixed up. Bereavement is the objective state of having lost someone close through death: it describes your situation. Grief is the emotional response to that loss: sorrow, yearning, anger, guilt, anxiety and loneliness, often with bodily signs such as poor sleep and loss of appetite. Mourning is the period of adjustment and the culturally shaped rituals through which people express grief and adapt, such as a funeral, a set time of wearing white, or the thirteen days of rites many Hindu families observe. John Bowlby (1980) saw grief as the response to a broken attachment and described phases: numbness, yearning and searching, disorganisation and despair, and reorganisation. Colin Murray Parkes (1972), studying London widows, described a similar course. Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut (1999) later replaced fixed phases with a dual process model, in which the bereaved move back and forth between facing the loss and rebuilding daily life. Example: when Arjun's father dies, Arjun is bereaved (his state); the heavy sadness and longing he feels is grief; the cremation, the prayers and the year of family observances make up mourning. Option b is right because it names the emotion itself, an intense emotional state, rather than a period, a phase or a general mood. Exam trap: option a sounds almost identical but describes a period of adjustment, which is mourning, not grief; option c describes only the first numb phase within grief.", "traps": {"a": "This is mourning: the period of emotional adjustment, shaped by cultural customs and rituals, that follows a death. It describes a process over time, not the emotional state itself.", "c": "This is the shock or numbness phase that opens grief in John Bowlby's and Colin Murray Parkes's accounts; it is one early phase, not grief as a whole.", "d": "Demoralisation is a general state of discouragement and helplessness (Jerome Frank's term for what brings people to therapy) that can occur without any loss, so it is not specific to death."}}, {"q": "From the perspective of Erik Erikson, life stages are important because:", "concept": "The answer is a: for Erik Erikson (1950), life stages matter because each one involves a crisis or dilemma. In Childhood and Society, Erikson described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage is defined by a psychosocial crisis: a turning point between two opposite tendencies, set off when a growing person meets new social demands. They run trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). 'Crisis' here means a choice of direction, not a disaster. A favourable resolution, tilted towards the positive pole, leaves the ego with a basic strength or virtue, such as hope from trust or fidelity from identity. An unresolved crisis makes later ones harder but not hopeless, because development continues across the whole lifespan. Erikson called the built-in sequence the epigenetic principle: the stages unfold on a timetable, but society shapes how each one turns out. Example: Kabir, 16, keeps switching between wanting to be a cricketer, an engineer and a musician while his parents push medicine; he is working through identity versus role confusion. Option a is right because a crisis or dilemma is the defining feature of every Eriksonian stage. Exam trap: examiners offer cognitive development, which is the stage logic of Jean Piaget (1936), and biological programming, which fits the libido-driven psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud better (Three Essays 1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923); Erikson's stages are psychosocial, defined by social conflicts.", "traps": {"b": "Stages that mark new levels of cognitive development are Jean Piaget's (sensorimotor to formal operational); Erik Erikson's stages concern social and emotional conflicts, not reasoning ability.", "c": "Erik Erikson's epigenetic principle does assume a built-in ground plan, but each stage is defined by its social crisis; stages as pure biological programming fit Arnold Gesell's maturation theory or Sigmund Freud's libido-driven stages better.", "d": "Erik Erikson's stages are not symptoms that should 'appear'; everyone passes through all eight, and difficulty comes from a poorly resolved crisis, not from a stage failing to show up."}}, {"q": "Elizabeth Kübler-Ross is known for her research in the area of:", "concept": "The answer is d: Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969) is known for her research on death and dying. She was a Swiss-born psychiatrist who interviewed terminally ill patients at a Chicago hospital. In her book On Death and Dying she proposed five stages that people often pass through after learning that their illness is terminal: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining ('let me live to see my son's wedding'), depression (grieving the losses to come) and acceptance (a calm, often quiet readiness). The mnemonic is DABDA. Her work changed medical care: it encouraged doctors to talk honestly with dying patients and supported the hospice movement, which Cicely Saunders (1967) had begun with St Christopher's Hospice in London. Later research found that people do not move through the stages in a fixed order; they skip, repeat or mix them, so the stages are best read as common reactions rather than a timetable. The model was also stretched, with little evidence, to the grief of the bereaved. Example: when Meera, 58, is told her cancer has spread, she first insists the scans are wrong (denial), later snaps at the nurses (anger), and weeks later promises to give up sweets if she can see her granddaughter born (bargaining). Option d is right because her classic book and five-stage model are about how people face their own death. Exam trap: coping with stress (option c) is the near miss, because dying is a stressor; but stress and coping research belongs to Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), not Kubler-Ross.", "traps": {"a": "Eating disorder research is linked with names such as Hilde Bruch (anorexia nervosa) and Gerald Russell (who named bulimia nervosa in 1979), not Elisabeth Kubler-Ross.", "b": "Adolescent behaviour research is linked with G. Stanley Hall (storm and stress, 1904) and Erik Erikson (identity), not Elisabeth Kubler-Ross.", "c": "Coping with stress is Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman's transactional model (appraisal, problem-focused and emotion-focused coping); facing death is a stressor, but Elisabeth Kubler-Ross studied the specific reactions of the dying."}}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called:", "concept": "The answer is d: memories that operate outside conscious awareness are called implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) defined implicit memory as an influence of past experience on behaviour, feeling or thought without conscious recollection. Explicit memory is the opposite: you deliberately and consciously retrieve something, as when you recall a list or recognise a face, so it is tested by recall and recognition. Larry Squire (1992) used the parallel terms declarative (explicit) and nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Implicit memory includes procedural skills (typing, cycling), priming (a word seen earlier is completed faster on a word-fragment test even if you do not remember seeing it), classically conditioned responses and habituation. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) could not consciously remember new events after hippocampal surgery in 1953, yet Brenda Milner (1962) showed that he improved day by day at mirror drawing while insisting he had never done the task. So explicit memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, while implicit memory relies on other systems such as the striatum and cerebellum. Example: Arjun types his email password perfectly every morning without thinking, but if asked to say the characters aloud he has to mime typing to recover them; his fingers hold an implicit memory. Option d is right because working outside conscious awareness is the defining feature of implicit memory. Exam trap: options a and b borrow from interference theory, where proactive interference (old learning disrupts new) and retroactive interference (new disrupts old) cause forgetting; 'proactive memories' and 'reactive memories' are not memory systems at all.", "traps": {"a": "'Proactive' belongs to proactive interference, where older learning disrupts recall of newer material (shown by Benton Underwood in 1957); it is a cause of forgetting, not a kind of unconscious memory.", "b": "There is no 'reactive memory'; the term echoes retroactive interference, where new learning disrupts recall of older material, a cause of forgetting rather than a memory system.", "c": "Explicit memory is the opposite: conscious, deliberate recollection of facts and events (Larry Squire's declarative memory), tested by recall and recognition."}}, {"q": "______ is the study of the meaning of words and language.", "concept": "The answer is c: semantics is the study of the meaning of words and language. Language can be analysed at several levels. Phonology studies the sound system (phonemes); morphology studies how words are built from morphemes, the smallest units of meaning; syntax studies the rules that combine words into sentences; semantics studies meaning, of words, phrases and whole sentences; and pragmatics studies how language is used in social context, such as knowing that 'Can you pass the salt?' is a request, not a question about ability. Semantics covers word meanings, synonyms and opposites, ambiguity, and how the meaning of a sentence is built from its parts. Noam Chomsky (1957) showed that syntax and meaning can come apart with his sentence 'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously', which is grammatically perfect but meaningless. Charles Hockett (1960) listed semanticity, the link between symbols and the things they stand for, among the design features of human language. The same root appears in memory research: Endel Tulving's (1972) semantic memory is our store of facts and word meanings. Example: Riya reads 'the boat drifted to the bank' and 'she deposited cash at the bank' and picks the right sense of 'bank' each time; that skill is semantic. Option c is right because the stem asks specifically about meaning, and meaning is what semantics studies. Exam trap: linguistics (option a) is the broad scientific study of all aspects of language, of which semantics is only one branch, so it is too wide; syntax (option d) is grammar, the rules of word order, not meaning.", "traps": {"a": "Linguistics is the whole scientific study of language (sounds, structure, meaning and use); semantics is just one of its branches, so it is too broad for a stem about meaning alone.", "b": "Encoding is a memory term: turning information into a form that can be stored (acoustic, visual or semantic encoding); it is a process, not the field that studies meaning.", "d": "Syntax is the set of rules for combining words into grammatical sentences (word order, Noam Chomsky's transformational grammar); a sentence can be syntactically correct yet meaningless."}}, {"q": "Basic speech sounds are called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the basic speech sounds of a language are called phonemes. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change the meaning of a word, although it carries no meaning of its own. Swap /p/ for /b/ and 'pat' becomes 'bat'; swap /k/ for /h/ and 'cat' becomes 'hat'. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and each language uses its own set, so two sounds that are separate phonemes in one language may be heard as one sound in another. Hindi, for instance, separates aspirated /kh/ from plain /k/, so 'kaana' (one-eyed) and 'khaana' (food) are different words, while English speakers barely notice the puff of air. Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units of meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness) and 'cats' has two (cat and the plural -s). Morphemes build words, words build sentences by the rules of syntax, and the meaning of the whole is studied by semantics. Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984) found that English-learning infants could tell apart Hindi dental and retroflex 't' sounds at 6 to 8 months but had mostly lost this by 10 to 12 months, as their ears tuned to the phonemes of their own language. Example: Meera's cousin from London cannot hear the difference between 'kaana' and 'khaana', while Meera hears two quite separate words. Option c is right because the stem asks for the basic units of speech sound, which is the definition of a phoneme. Exam trap: morpheme (option a) is the classic near miss; the morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning, the phoneme the smallest unit of sound.", "traps": {"a": "A morpheme is the smallest unit of meaning (free like 'cat' or bound like the plural -s); it is usually built from several phonemes, so it is a unit of meaning, not of sound.", "b": "A syllable is a unit of pronunciation organised around a vowel ('wa-ter' has two); it usually contains several phonemes, so it is not the basic sound unit.", "d": "Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into sentences; it is grammar, not sound, and works several levels above phonemes."}}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "traps": {"a": "Trait theorists such as Gordon Allport and Raymond Cattell describe stable, measurable dispositions and say little about the unconscious, so trait theory cannot be the approach that maximised it.", "c": "Psychoanalytic is right for the first blank, but humanistic theory (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) only played down the unconscious; Rogers still accepted that experiences can be denied to awareness, whereas behaviourism set the unconscious aside entirely.", "d": "Behaviourist fits the second blank, but trait theory never maximised the unconscious; Gordon Allport even stressed that healthy adults are guided by conscious, present motives."}}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of:", "concept": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both forms of punishment. In operant conditioning, Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) experiments showed that consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Two questions classify every consequence. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Here positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour: a scolding, a slap, extra homework. Negative punishment removes something pleasant: time-out from play, a fine (response cost), losing phone time. Both make the behaviour less likely, so both are punishment. By contrast, positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, money), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet, the seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up); both make the behaviour more likely. Skinner argued that punishment suppresses behaviour only temporarily, teaches nothing about what to do instead and can breed fear and aggression, so reinforcing a better behaviour usually works better. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him when he hits his sister (positive punishment) and also takes away his cricket bat for a day (negative punishment); both aim to reduce hitting. Option b is right because adding an aversive stimulus and removing a pleasant one are the two forms of punishment. Exam trap: negative reinforcement (option a) is the classic confusion; it removes something unpleasant and so increases behaviour, the reverse of punishment.", "traps": {"a": "Negative reinforcement removes an unpleasant stimulus when the behaviour occurs (switching off a shock, ending a nagging), so the behaviour increases; punishment does the opposite and decreases it.", "c": "Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus (praise, a sweet) after a behaviour to make it more likely; neither half of the stem adds anything pleasant.", "d": "Secondary (conditioned) reinforcement is a stimulus that gains its power by being paired with a primary reinforcer, such as money or tokens in a token economy; it strengthens behaviour rather than weakening it."}}, {"q": "Homophobia is:", "concept": "The answer is b: homophobia is prejudice against, and dislike of, gay men and lesbians. The psychologist George Weinberg (1972), in Society and the Healthy Homosexual, popularised the term for an irrational dread of and hostility towards homosexual people. Despite the suffix, it is not a clinical phobia like a fear of heights; it is an attitude with the three parts every prejudice has: a negative belief (stereotype), a negative feeling (prejudice) and unfair action (discrimination, from jokes and exclusion to violence). Gregory Herek (2000) preferred the term sexual prejudice, and the related word heterosexism names the cultural assumption that heterosexuality is the only normal form. Facing such prejudice causes minority stress (Ilan Meyer, 2003), which helps explain higher rates of depression and anxiety in sexual minorities, while homosexuality itself was removed from the DSM in 1973. In India, the Supreme Court in Navtej Singh Johar v Union of India (2018) decriminalised consensual same-sex relations between adults by reading down Section 377. Example: Arjun's colleague refuses to sit near a gay co-worker and mocks him in meetings; that is homophobia shown as discrimination. Option b is right because homophobia names a prejudice and dislike, not a fear of blood or a sexual orientation. Exam trap: option c defines homosexuality (same-sex attraction), not homophobia, and option a plays on a look-alike Greek root, since fear of blood is haemophobia, from haima (blood), while homo comes from homos (same).", "traps": {"a": "Fear of blood is haemophobia (hemophobia), from the Greek haima, blood; it is a blood-injection-injury specific phobia, known for its fainting response, and has nothing to do with sexual orientation.", "c": "A capacity for erotic attraction to people of the same sex defines homosexuality, a sexual orientation; homophobia is the prejudice against people with that orientation.", "d": "'None' fails because option b gives the standard textbook definition of homophobia."}}, {"q": "Which of the statements correctly describes the general findings of studies examining treatment of childhood anxiety?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the general findings on treating childhood anxiety. The landmark trial is the Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS) of John Walkup and colleagues (2008), published in the New England Journal of Medicine. It randomly assigned 488 children and adolescents aged 7 to 17, with separation anxiety disorder, generalised anxiety disorder or social phobia, to four arms for 12 weeks: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT, based on Philip Kendall's (1990) Coping Cat programme), the SSRI sertraline, the two combined, or a pill placebo. Rated much or very much improved were 80.7% with the combination, 59.7% with CBT, 54.9% with sertraline and 23.7% with placebo. Three findings follow. CBT alone and sertraline alone did not differ significantly from each other (statement a). CBT helped about 60% of children (statement b). The combination beat both single treatments (statement c). Philip Kendall's own randomised trial (1994) had already shown that CBT, which teaches children to recognise anxious feelings and thoughts, use coping plans and then face feared situations through graded exposure, helps roughly two thirds of anxious children. Clinical practice therefore usually offers CBT first and considers adding an SSRI for moderate to severe anxiety or when CBT alone is not enough. Example: Meera, 11, panics before school and cannot sleep away from her mother; a CAMS-style plan might start weekly CBT and add sertraline if she stays stuck. Option d is right because each of the three statements matches the CAMS results. Exam trap: students who know that the combination won may wrongly reject statement a, assuming the two single treatments must also differ; in CAMS they did not.", "traps": {"a": "True on its own: in CAMS, CBT alone (59.7% improved) and sertraline alone (54.9%) did not differ significantly; but because b and c are also true, choosing a alone is incomplete.", "b": "True on its own: CBT alone produced a good response in about 60% (59.7%) of children in CAMS; but a and c are true as well, so this single option undersells the findings.", "c": "True on its own: combination treatment (80.7%) beat both CBT and sertraline alone; but a and b are also supported, so 'All of these' is the complete answer."}}, {"q": "Which of the following defines Beck's cognitive triad?", "concept": "The answer is a: the cognitive triad of Aaron Beck (1967) is a set of negative beliefs about the self, the world and the future. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients' dreams and thoughts were full of automatic negative themes about loss and failure rather than hidden anger turned inwards. In his 1967 book on depression and in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues), he proposed that depression rests on three linked negative views. The self: 'I am worthless, unlovable, a failure.' The world, or current experience: 'Everything is against me; no one helps.' The future: 'It will never get better.' These views flow from dysfunctional schemas laid down by early experience and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. Hopelessness, the negative view of the future, is the corner most closely tied to suicide, which Beck measured with his Hopelessness Scale (1974). Cognitive therapy targets the triad through collaborative empiricism: client and therapist treat each belief as a hypothesis, test it with thought records and behavioural experiments, and build more balanced alternatives. Example: after failing one exam, Arjun thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'my teachers are unfair and nobody cares' (world) and 'I will never get a job' (future). Option a is right because it lists all three corners correctly: self, world and future. Exam trap: examiners swap one corner for a near miss, putting 'others' in place of the world or the future, or substituting a time sequence (past, present, future); the mnemonic is self, world, future.", "traps": {"b": "Self and world are right, but 'others' replaces the future; Aaron Beck's third corner is the future, and hopelessness about it is the corner most tied to suicide risk.", "c": "Self and future are right, but 'others' replaces the world (current experience); interpersonal themes do feature in depression, but 'others' is not one of Aaron Beck's three corners.", "d": "Past, present and future is a time sequence, not Aaron Beck's triad; the triad is about what the person believes about themselves, their world and what lies ahead."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "concept": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "traps": {"b": "An enduring pattern of unstable, intense relationships, swinging between idealisation and devaluation, is a core DSM criterion for BPD, so it is a cardinal feature, not the exception.", "c": "A lack of a stable self-image is the DSM identity disturbance criterion and matches Otto Kernberg's identity diffusion, so it is a cardinal BPD feature.", "d": "Frequent, unpredictable mood swings describe affective instability (marked reactivity of mood lasting hours, rarely days), a core BPD criterion."}}, {"q": "Which of these is not an information provided in DSM-IV-TR?", "concept": "The answer is d: DSM-IV-TR does not give information on treatment options. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. Since DSM-III, directed by Robert Spitzer (1980), the manual has been descriptive and atheoretical: it says what a disorder looks like and how to diagnose it reliably, stays neutral about causes and is silent on treatment. DSM-IV-TR (2000) is a text revision of DSM-IV (1994): the criteria stayed almost the same, but the descriptive text was updated. For each disorder the text covers diagnostic features (the essential features), subtypes and specifiers, recording procedures, associated features and disorders (including laboratory findings), specific culture, age and gender features, prevalence, course, familial pattern and differential diagnosis, followed by the diagnostic criteria, the list of symptoms that must be present and for how long. DSM-IV-TR also used five axes: clinical disorders; personality disorders and mental retardation; general medical conditions; psychosocial and environmental problems; and the Global Assessment of Functioning. Treatment guidance lives elsewhere, in the American Psychiatric Association's separate practice guidelines, in national guidelines and in textbooks. Example: a clinical psychologist, Kabir, uses DSM-IV-TR to confirm that his client meets the criteria for a major depressive episode and to rule out bipolar disorder, but turns to a practice guideline to choose between CBT and an antidepressant. Option d is right because the manual is a diagnostic and descriptive tool, not a treatment manual. Exam trap: the size of the book and the word 'Statistical' tempt students to think it covers everything about a disorder; it covers diagnosis and description, never therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Essential (diagnostic) features are described for every disorder in the DSM-IV-TR text, so this is information the manual does provide.", "b": "Associated features and disorders, such as typical laboratory findings and common comorbid conditions, form a standard subsection of each DSM-IV-TR entry.", "c": "The list of symptoms that must be present, the diagnostic criteria, has been the heart of the manual since Robert Spitzer's DSM-III (1980)."}}, {"q": "Lifetime prevalence rate for OCD is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the lifetime prevalence of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is around 2.5%. OCD is defined by obsessions, recurrent intrusive and unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety (fear of germs, doubts about locking the door, taboo images), and/or compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, praying) done to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event. It must be time-consuming (more than an hour a day) or cause marked distress or impairment. The classic figure comes from the Epidemiologic Catchment Area (ECA) study of the 1980s, which interviewed more than 18,000 adults at five US sites with a structured diagnostic interview. Marvin Karno and colleagues (1988) reported a lifetime OCD prevalence of about 2.5%, far higher than clinicians had assumed, since many sufferers hide their rituals. Lifetime prevalence is the proportion of people who have had the disorder at any point in their life; 12-month prevalence counts only recent cases and is therefore lower. OCD usually begins in late adolescence or early adulthood, earlier in males. Example: Arjun, 20, checks the gas knob 30 times before leaving home and is late for college every day; he is one of the 2 to 3 people in every 100 who will have OCD at some point. Option a is right because about 2.5% is the standard lifetime figure. Exam trap: 'around 1%' (option d) is close to the 12-month figure, so it catches students who mix up lifetime and 12-month prevalence. Key note: the later National Comorbidity Survey Replication (Ayelet Meron Ruscio and colleagues, 2010) gives about 2.3%, and DSM-5 gives a 12-month figure of 1.2% (1.1% to 1.8% internationally), so a '1%' option is a 12-month trap; around 2.5% stands.", "traps": {"b": "1.5% matches neither the lifetime figure (about 2.5% in the ECA, 2.3% in the NCS-R) nor the DSM-5 12-month figure (1.2%); it is a distractor that sits between the two.", "c": "4.5% roughly doubles the survey figures; no major epidemiological study puts lifetime OCD that high, so it overstates the disorder.", "d": "About 1% matches the DSM-5 12-month prevalence (1.2% in the United States), which counts only recent cases; it is a trap for students who mix up 12-month and lifetime prevalence."}}, {"q": "Which of these has been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence?", "concept": "The answer is d: criminal behaviour leading to prison, psychosis and antisocial personality disorder have all been associated with conduct disorder in adolescence. Conduct disorder (CD) is a repetitive, persistent pattern of violating others' basic rights or major age-appropriate rules: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule violations such as truancy and running away. DSM requires 3 of 15 criteria in the past 12 months and separates a childhood-onset type (a symptom before 10) from an adolescent-onset type. The links run in three directions. First, antisocial personality disorder (ASPD): DSM requires evidence of conduct disorder before age 15 for an adult ASPD diagnosis, and Lee Robins (1966), in Deviant Children Grown Up, found that adult antisocial personality almost never appeared without childhood conduct problems. Second, crime: Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated a rare life-course-persistent pattern, starting in early childhood and continuing into adult offending and imprisonment, from a common adolescence-limited pattern that fades in early adulthood. Third, psychosis: birth-cohort studies such as the Dunedin study in New Zealand found that children with conduct problems had higher rates of adult psychotic and schizophreniform disorders, so CD is a broad risk marker, not only a route to crime. Example: Kabir, 14, has been setting fires, stealing and bullying since age 9; his risk of adult offending, of ASPD and even of later psychosis is raised. Option d is right because each of the three outcomes has research support. Exam trap: students know the conduct disorder to ASPD link and pick c, overlooking the evidence for imprisonment and psychosis.", "traps": {"a": "Adolescent conduct disorder does predict adult offending and imprisonment, especially in Terrie Moffitt's life-course-persistent group, but choosing this alone ignores the equally supported links to psychosis and ASPD.", "b": "Cohort studies such as Dunedin found more adult psychotic and schizophreniform disorder after childhood conduct problems, so this is a real association, but only one of three.", "c": "Antisocial personality disorder requires conduct disorder before age 15, the best-known link; it is true but incomplete, because crime and psychosis are associated too."}}, {"q": "In early Alzheimer's Disease, which is NOT a common symptom:", "concept": "The answer is b: hallucinations are not a common symptom of early Alzheimer's disease. Alzheimer's disease, described by Alois Alzheimer (1906) in his patient Auguste Deter, is the commonest cause of dementia. It begins insidiously and progresses gradually as amyloid plaques (outside neurons) and tau tangles (inside neurons) spread from the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus to the wider cortex. That spread explains the order of symptoms. Early on, recent episodic memory fails: repeating questions, misplacing things, forgetting recent conversations. Word-finding difficulty (anomia) appears early too, along with mild problems in planning and finding one's way. Mood and behaviour change early as well: apathy, social withdrawal from hobbies and gatherings, depression and anxiety, often because the person senses they are slipping. Hallucinations and delusions (such as believing that someone is stealing misplaced items) usually come in the moderate to late stages. When vivid, well-formed visual hallucinations appear early, together with fluctuating attention and parkinsonism, clinicians suspect dementia with Lewy bodies instead. Example: Meera's grandfather, 74, keeps asking what day it is, struggles to name everyday objects ('the thing you cut with'), stops going to his evening walking group and seems anxious; that is a typical early Alzheimer's picture. Option b is right because hallucinations are a late feature of Alzheimer's disease and an early feature of a different dementia. Exam trap: the stem asks what is NOT common, and anomia sounds too technical to be an early sign, so students pick it; but word-finding difficulty is one of the first language symptoms of Alzheimer's disease.", "traps": {"a": "Anomia, difficulty finding or naming words, is one of the earliest language signs of Alzheimer's disease as damage reaches temporal language areas, so it is a common early symptom.", "c": "Social withdrawal, giving up hobbies and gatherings, often appears early as apathy sets in and the person avoids situations that expose memory lapses.", "d": "Anxiety, like depression and apathy, is common in early Alzheimer's disease, partly as a reaction to noticing one's own decline."}}, {"q": "Which of these has NOT been associated with psychosis?", "concept": "The answer is d: large family size has not been established as a risk factor for psychosis. Modern thinking about psychosis uses a diathesis-stress, or neurodevelopmental, model: genes and early brain development create a vulnerability, and later stresses push a vulnerable person into illness. Robin Murray and Shon Lewis (1987) argued that an early brain insult can stay silent until adolescent brain maturation unmasks it. Several early and environmental factors are well supported. Obstetric or birth complications, such as oxygen shortage at delivery, low birth weight and pre-eclampsia, modestly raise the risk. Maternal stress, infection (for example influenza) and malnutrition during pregnancy are linked to later schizophrenia in the child. Childhood adversity, especially sexual and physical abuse, bullying and neglect, raises the risk of psychotic experiences and disorder in a dose-response fashion: the more adversity, the higher the risk. Urban birth and upbringing, migration and heavy adolescent cannabis use add risk too. Family size, by contrast, has shown no consistent effect: studies of sibship size and birth order give mixed results, and it is not listed among the established risk factors. Example: Riya's psychiatrist asks about complications at her birth, her mother's health in pregnancy, any childhood trauma and her cannabis use, but not how many siblings she has. Option d is right because it is the only option without a consistent research link to psychosis. Exam trap: because family theories such as Frieda Fromm-Reichmann's (1948) 'schizophrenogenic mother' once blamed the family, students may think family structure matters; those family-blame theories are discredited, and family size has no established role.", "traps": {"a": "Birth (obstetric) complications such as oxygen shortage at delivery and low birth weight are among the best-replicated early risk factors and are central to the neurodevelopmental model of schizophrenia.", "b": "Maternal stress, infection and malnutrition during pregnancy have all been linked to later psychosis in the child, so this has been associated.", "c": "Childhood abuse raises the risk of psychotic experiences and psychotic disorder in a dose-response way, so it is an established association."}}, {"q": "Which one of these statements is TRUE?", "concept": "The answer is c: siblings of people diagnosed with schizophrenia are more likely to have mental health problems in their lifetime. Schizophrenia runs in families because risk is partly genetic and polygenic, carried by many common gene variants of small effect. Irving Gottesman (1991) pooled about 40 family and twin studies: lifetime risk is about 1% in the general population, about 9% for a sibling, about 13% for a child of one affected parent and about 48% for an identical co-twin. These genes overlap with those for bipolar disorder, depression and autism, and siblings also share family stress, so first-degree relatives show raised rates of several disorders, not only schizophrenia. The other statements are popular myths. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent and are far more often victims of violence than perpetrators. Hearing voices or holding odd beliefs does not by itself mean schizophrenia: Marius Romme and Sandra Escher (1989) showed that many voice-hearers in the general population cope well and never seek help, and hallucinations also occur in bereavement, post-traumatic stress disorder, bipolar disorder, severe depression, drug use and on falling asleep. Example: Arjun's elder brother has schizophrenia; Arjun's own lifetime risk of schizophrenia is several times the average, and his risk of depression is also higher, yet most siblings never develop schizophrenia. Option c is right because family and twin studies consistently show raised risk in relatives. Exam trap: options a and d play on media stereotypes, and b equates a symptom with a diagnosis. Key note: c is the clearly supported statement, though d is not baseless: meta-analyses such as Seena Fazel and colleagues (2009) find a raised relative risk of violent crime, including homicide, in schizophrenia, mostly explained by co-occurring substance misuse. The absolute risk is tiny and textbooks treat the blanket claim as a myth, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is the media stereotype: most people with schizophrenia are never violent, and any added risk is concentrated in those with untreated illness and substance misuse, so the illness does not 'make people violent'.", "b": "Voices and unusual beliefs occur in many people without schizophrenia (Marius Romme and Sandra Escher's voice-hearers, bereavement, PTSD, bipolar disorder), and diagnosis needs a cluster of symptoms with duration and impairment, not one experience.", "d": "Studies do find a raised relative risk of homicide, concentrated in people with substance misuse, but the absolute risk is very small and most people with schizophrenia never harm anyone; textbooks treat this blanket claim as a myth."}}, {"q": "Most current Alzheimer treatments work by altering levels of which neurotransmitter?", "concept": "The answer is d: most current Alzheimer treatments work by raising levels of acetylcholine. The cholinergic hypothesis, set out by Raymond Bartus and colleagues (1982), grew from the finding that Alzheimer brains lose cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, at the base of the forebrain, which send acetylcholine to the cortex and hippocampus. Acetylcholine is vital for attention, learning and memory, so its loss fits the memory decline. Normally the enzyme acetylcholinesterase breaks acetylcholine down in the synapse soon after release. Cholinesterase inhibitors block that enzyme, so acetylcholine lingers longer and signals more strongly. Three are in wide use: donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine. They modestly improve or steady symptoms for a time in mild to moderate disease, but they do not stop the underlying plaques and tangles. The exception is memantine, used in moderate to severe disease, which blocks NMDA glutamate receptors and so protects neurons from excess glutamate stimulation. Newer antibody drugs that clear amyloid act on the disease process itself rather than on any transmitter. Example: Kabir's grandmother is diagnosed with mild Alzheimer's disease and started on donepezil; her neurologist explains that the tablet keeps more acetylcholine available at her synapses. Option d is right because the main drug class for Alzheimer's disease targets acetylcholine. Exam trap: dopamine (option a) is the transmitter targeted in Parkinson's disease (L-dopa) and schizophrenia, so students who mix up the neurodegenerative diseases pick it; GABA loss marks Huntington's disease.", "traps": {"a": "Dopamine is the target in Parkinson's disease (L-dopa replaces lost nigrostriatal dopamine) and in schizophrenia (antipsychotics block D2 receptors), not in standard Alzheimer treatment.", "b": "GABA is the brain's main inhibitory transmitter, boosted by benzodiazepines for anxiety; loss of GABA neurons in the striatum marks Huntington's disease, not Alzheimer's disease.", "c": "Noradrenaline, from the locus coeruleus, is linked to arousal and mood and is targeted by antidepressants such as SNRIs; Alzheimer drugs do not work mainly on it."}}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the first intelligence test was developed by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905). In 1904 the French Ministry of Education asked Alfred Binet to find a way to identify children who could not keep up in ordinary classes and needed special schooling. Earlier attempts, such as Francis Galton's (1884) anthropometric laboratory in London and James McKeen Cattell's (1890) 'mental tests', measured reaction time and sensory sharpness, and they failed to predict school success. Binet and the physician Simon took a different route, measuring higher mental processes: judgement, comprehension, reasoning and memory. Their 1905 Binet-Simon scale had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, giving the concept of mental age, and the 1911 revision extended it towards adults. Binet insisted the score was a practical guide for helping children, not a fixed measure of inborn ability. Later, Lewis Terman (1916) adapted it at Stanford University as the Stanford-Binet, using William Stern's (1912) intelligence quotient. Example: a teacher in Kolkata worried about Meera, aged 9, could use a Binet-style scale to find that she passes the items typical of 7-year-olds, a mental age of 7, signalling that she needs extra support. Option a is right because the 1905 Binet-Simon scale is recognised as the first practical intelligence test. Exam trap: option c sounds right because the Stanford-Binet is the most famous descendant, but 'Stanford' is a university, not a person, and Terman's revision came eleven years later.", "traps": {"b": "Charles Spearman (1904) proposed the two-factor theory and general intelligence (g) using factor analysis; he theorised about intelligence but did not build the first test.", "c": "'Stanford' is Stanford University, where Lewis Terman revised the Binet-Simon scale as the Stanford-Binet (1916); it is a later adaptation, and no person named Stanford co-wrote it.", "d": "Lewis Terman (Stanford-Binet, 1916) and David Wechsler (Wechsler-Bellevue, 1939) built later, influential tests, never together, and both came after Binet and Simon's 1905 scale."}}, {"q": "The Deviation IQ is based on the notion of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the deviation IQ is based on the z-score. A z-score tells how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies above or below the mean of a group: z = (score minus mean) ÷ SD. The older ratio IQ, from William Stern (1912) and Lewis Terman (1916), was mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It worked for children but failed for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so a perfectly ordinary adult's IQ would fall with every birthday. David Wechsler (1939), with the Wechsler-Bellevue scale, replaced it with the deviation IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the mean and SD of his or her own age group in a large norm sample, turned into a z-score, and then rescaled to a mean of 100 and an SD of 15: IQ = 100 + 15z. So scoring exactly at your age group's mean gives 100 at any age, one SD above (z = +1) gives 115, and one SD below gives 85. Because the deviation IQ is a standard score on the normal curve, it converts directly to a percentile: 115 is about the 84th percentile, 130 about the 98th. Modern Wechsler tests and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition all use it. Example: Arjun, 30, scores 12 points above the mean for his age group on a test whose SD at that age is 6; his z is +2, so his deviation IQ is 100 + 30 = 130. Option c is right because the deviation IQ is a z-score rescaled to mean 100 and SD 15. Exam trap: option b describes the ratio IQ that the deviation IQ replaced, so students who remember 'mental age' pick the wrong formula.", "traps": {"a": "Delinquency has no role in IQ scoring; this distractor plays on the word 'deviation', which in statistics means distance from the mean, not deviant behaviour.", "b": "Mental age versus chronological age is the ratio IQ of William Stern and Lewis Terman ((MA ÷ CA) × 100), the method the deviation IQ replaced.", "d": "Verbal versus performance scores were the two halves of David Wechsler's older scales (dropped in the WAIS-IV); comparing them is profile analysis, not the basis of the deviation IQ."}}, {"q": "Projective tests originated from:", "concept": "The answer is a: projective tests originated from the idea of Sigmund Freud (1896) that behaviour is driven by unconscious motives. In his early papers Freud described projection as a defence in which a person attributes his or her own unacceptable feelings or wishes to others. More broadly, psychoanalysis held that unconscious motives shape everything we do, including how we see ambiguous things. Lawrence Frank (1939) turned this into the projective hypothesis and coined the term 'projective methods': when people meet an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus with minimal instructions, they impose their own needs, conflicts and ways of seeing on it, revealing what they could not or would not report directly. Hence the tests: Hermann Rorschach's (1921) ten inkblots, Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray's (1935) Thematic Apperception Test of ambiguous pictures, sentence completion and drawing tests. Rorschach did not stumble on his blots; he deliberately designed and selected them, perhaps inspired by klecksography, a popular game of making pictures from blots. Projective tests are prized for clinical richness but criticised for weak reliability and validity; John Exner's (1974) Comprehensive System was a major attempt to standardise Rorschach scoring. Example: shown a picture of a boy staring at a violin, Riya tells a story about a child forced to practise by a harsh father; the clinician wonders whether she is projecting her own feelings about pressure at home. Option a is right because the logic of projection and hidden motives comes from Freud's psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung is a tempting near miss because his word association test (from 1904) was an early projective-style method, but option b names his theory of psychological types, which led to the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, an objective self-report inventory.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Jung's theory of psychological types (1921: introversion, extraversion and four functions) gave rise to the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, a self-report inventory; his word association test was projective in spirit, but the types theory was not the origin of projective tests.", "c": "Tests that avoid language are non-verbal or culture-fair tests, such as the Army Beta (1917) and John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938); they are ability tests, not projective personality tests.", "d": "This is a myth: Hermann Rorschach deliberately created and selected his inkblots (published 1921), possibly inspired by klecksography, the game of making pictures from blots, not by an accidental spill on a test booklet."}}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of +1?", "concept": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "traps": {"a": "50% is the area above the mean (z = 0), the whole upper half of the curve; by z = +1 another 34% has been passed, leaving about 16%.", "c": "25% is the area above the third quartile, which lies at about z = +0.67, not at z = +1.", "d": "10% is the area above about z = +1.28 (the 90th percentile), so it is too small for z = +1."}}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "traps": {"a": "Verbal fluency tests, such as the FAS (Controlled Oral Word Association) test, ask the person to generate as many words as possible from a letter or category in a minute; the Stroop asks for no word generation.", "b": "Visual perception tests, such as Arthur Benton's Judgment of Line Orientation, assess how a person sees shape and space; the Stroop assumes normal colour vision and measures the control of attention instead.", "c": "Memory is tested by tools such as the Wechsler Memory Scale or the Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test; the Stroop stimuli stay in front of the person throughout, so nothing has to be remembered."}}, {"q": "A nerve cell carrying information from your eyes, ears, fingers, etc. toward the CNS is called a(n):", "concept": "The answer is d: a nerve cell that carries information from the eyes, ears, skin and other receptors towards the central nervous system (CNS) is a sensory neuron. Neurons fall into three functional classes. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry signals inwards, from receptors to the brain and spinal cord. Motor (efferent) neurons carry commands outwards, from the CNS to muscles and glands. Interneurons (also called connector or association neurons) lie wholly inside the CNS and link the two; they are by far the commonest kind. The muscles and glands that finally act are effectors, which are targets, not neurons at all. Charles Sherrington (1906) showed how these pieces join in the reflex arc. Suppose Meera touches a hot pan: receptors in her fingertip fire, sensory neurons carry the message through the dorsal root into the spinal cord, an interneuron relays it, and a motor neuron makes her arm muscles pull the hand away before she even feels the pain. A handy memory aid is SAME: Sensory Afferent, Motor Efferent; afferent arrives, efferent exits. Option d is right because the stem describes the inward direction of travel, from sense organs to the CNS, and that direction defines a sensory neuron. Exam trap: examiners swap the direction, so check whether the signal goes towards the CNS (sensory) or away from it (motor), and remember that 'effector' names the target organ, not a nerve cell.", "traps": {"a": "A motor (efferent) neuron carries commands away from the CNS to muscles and glands, the opposite direction to the one the stem describes.", "b": "An effector is the muscle or gland that carries out the response at the end of a reflex arc; it receives signals from motor neurons and is not a neuron that carries information anywhere.", "c": "A connector neuron (interneuron or association neuron) lies entirely within the brain and spinal cord and links sensory to motor neurons; it does not bring information in from the sense organs."}}, {"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the:", "concept": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event, such as a pounding heart, fast breathing, sweating and a dry mouth, are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the internal organs (heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut and glands) largely without conscious control. It has two branches that work in opposite directions. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for emergencies, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: it raises heart rate and blood pressure, widens the pupils, slows digestion and makes the adrenal medulla release adrenaline. The parasympathetic branch calms the body afterwards (rest and digest), slowing the heart and restarting digestion. Imagine Arjun in a road accident: within seconds his heart races, his palms sweat and his stomach knots, all sympathetic effects, and an hour later his parasympathetic branch brings him back to baseline. The hypothalamus and amygdala in the brain trigger these changes, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. The polygraph (lie detector) works by recording exactly these autonomic changes. Option c is right because the stem asks which system most directly controls the emotional reaction, and the involuntary arousal of fear and stress is autonomic. Exam trap: the somatic nervous system sounds bodily, but it carries sensation and controls the voluntary skeletal muscles; the gut-level, involuntary arousal of emotion belongs to the autonomic system.", "traps": {"a": "The somatic nervous system carries sensory input and commands to the voluntary skeletal muscles; it lets you run from danger but does not produce the involuntary racing heart and sweating of emotion.", "b": "The central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) contains the hypothalamus and amygdala that start an emotional reaction, but it acts on the body through the autonomic system, which is the more direct controller the stem asks for.", "d": "The cerebral cortex appraises and labels an emotion and can partly regulate it, but it does not directly drive heart rate, breathing or sweating."}}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "concept": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most likely causes a loss of muscular coordination. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the brain, beneath the occipital lobes and behind the pons, as part of the hindbrain. It does not start movements; the motor cortex does that. Instead it compares the movement you intended with the movement actually happening, using feedback from muscles, joints and the balance organs of the inner ear, and smooths out the errors. It also stores well-practised motor skills and timing, such as cycling or typing, and takes part in simple conditioning such as the eyeblink reflex. Damage produces ataxia: an unsteady, wide-based, staggering walk, poor balance, overshooting when reaching for an object (dysmetria), a tremor that grows as the hand nears its target (intention tremor) and slurred speech. Damage on one side affects the same side of the body. Alcohol depresses the cerebellum early, which is why roadside sobriety tests ask a driver to walk a straight line or touch the nose. For example, after a cerebellar stroke Kabir may know exactly what he wants to do and have full strength, yet he sways when he walks and spills tea when he lifts a cup. Option c is right because coordination, balance and the smooth timing of movement are the cerebellum's core jobs. Exam trap: heart and breathing failure tempts students because the cerebellum lies close to the brainstem, but those vital centres sit in the medulla oblongata.", "traps": {"a": "Heart stoppage or respiratory failure follows damage to the medulla oblongata, the brainstem structure that holds the cardiac and respiratory centres, not the cerebellum.", "b": "Loss of hearing follows damage to the ear, the auditory nerve or the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), not the cerebellum.", "d": "Reduced emotional response points to the limbic system, especially the amygdala, or to the prefrontal cortex; the cerebellum is a motor coordination structure."}}, {"q": "An elderly acquaintance of yours has suffered from partial blindness since she had a stroke. Apparently the stroke damaged her:", "concept": "The answer is a: partial blindness after a stroke points to damage in the occipital lobe. The occipital lobe, at the back of the cerebral cortex, holds the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) around the calcarine fissure. Signals travel from the retina along the optic nerve, partly cross at the optic chiasm, relay in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus and arrive in V1. Because of this crossing, each occipital lobe sees the opposite half of the visual field from both eyes. A stroke in the posterior cerebral artery, which feeds the occipital lobe, therefore often leaves a person blind in the same half of the field in both eyes (contralateral homonymous hemianopia), or with smaller blind islands (scotomas), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Damage to both occipital lobes can produce cortical blindness. Beyond V1, visual information flows along two streams: a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe and a dorsal 'where' stream into the parietal lobe (Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin, 1982). Suppose Meera's grandmother can read the right half of a newspaper line but keeps missing the words on the left; a right occipital stroke would explain it. Option a is right because partial loss of sight with intact eyes means the visual cortex is damaged, and the visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: parietal damage can make a person ignore the left side of space (hemispatial neglect), which looks like blindness but is a failure of attention, not of seeing.", "traps": {"b": "The parietal lobe handles touch and body sense (somatosensory cortex) and spatial attention; its damage causes sensory loss or hemispatial neglect rather than true partial blindness.", "c": "The temporal lobes handle hearing, language comprehension (Wernicke's area) and memory; a deep temporal lesion can cut a small upper quarter of vision through the optic radiation, but the classic site for stroke blindness is the occipital lobe.", "d": "The reticular formation is a brainstem network that controls arousal, sleep and alertness; damage there causes drowsiness or coma, not loss of part of the visual field."}}, {"q": "The technique that uses a magnetic field to develop an image of the brain is called the:", "concept": "The answer is c: the technique that uses a magnetic field to build an image of the brain is magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). The person lies inside a powerful magnet that lines up the hydrogen nuclei (protons) in the body's water and fat. Pulses of radio waves knock them out of line; as they relax back they give off signals, and because grey matter, white matter and fluid relax at different rates, a computer turns the signals into detailed slices in any plane. Paul Lauterbur (1973) showed how to make such images, and he shared the 2003 Nobel Prize with Peter Mansfield. MRI uses no X-rays or radioactivity and has the best spatial resolution of the common scans, so it is the standard way to see brain structure, such as tumours, multiple sclerosis plaques or a shrunken hippocampus. Functional MRI (fMRI) tracks changes in blood oxygen (the BOLD signal) to show which areas are active during a task. Compare its look-alikes: computed tomography (CT), introduced by Godfrey Hounsfield in the early 1970s, builds slices from X-rays, and positron emission tomography (PET) follows a radioactive tracer. If Riya's neurologist wants a detailed view of a small lesion, she orders an MRI; if Riya has just hit her head and may be bleeding, a quick CT comes first. Option c is right because MRI is the only option that images the brain with a magnetic field. Exam trap: the EEG and the MRI are both 'brain tests', but the EEG records electrical activity through scalp electrodes and makes no picture of structure.", "traps": {"a": "'Clinical scan' is not the name of any imaging technique; it is a vague everyday phrase used as a distractor.", "b": "The ablation technique removes or destroys a piece of brain tissue to see which behaviour is lost, as Pierre Flourens (1820s) did with animals; it is a lesion method, not an imaging method, and uses no magnetic field.", "d": "The EEG (electroencephalogram), first recorded in humans by Hans Berger (1929), measures the brain's electrical activity through scalp electrodes; it gives waves over time, not a magnetic image of structure."}}, {"q": "Which of these is associated with conduct disorder?", "concept": "The answer is d: conduct disorder is associated with all three outcomes: criminal behaviour leading to imprisonment, antisocial personality disorder and psychosis. Conduct disorder (CD) is a childhood or adolescent pattern of repeatedly violating the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate rules. DSM-5 groups its 15 signs under four headings (aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious violations of rules such as truancy or running away) and requires at least 3 in the past year. Its long-term outcomes are well documented. First, CD is the gateway to antisocial personality disorder (ASPD): an adult cannot receive an ASPD diagnosis unless there is evidence of conduct disorder with onset before age 15. Lee Robins (1966), following child guidance clinic children into adulthood, found that serious childhood antisocial behaviour was the strongest predictor of adult antisocial personality. Second, Terrie Moffitt (1993) separated common, short-lived adolescence-limited offending from a life-course-persistent pattern that starts early with conduct problems and continues into adult crime, violence and imprisonment. Third, cohort studies such as the Dunedin study in New Zealand found that children with conduct problems were more likely to develop psychotic and schizophreniform disorders as adults. Imagine Arjun, who at 9 bullies classmates, hurts stray dogs and steals; without help, each of these three paths becomes more likely for him. This paper asks this same question twice, as Q16 and Q31, and the answer is the same both times. Option d is right because imprisonment, ASPD and later psychosis are each established outcomes of conduct disorder. Exam trap: students pick antisocial personality disorder alone because it is the best-known link, but the other two are also documented, so 'All of these' is the complete answer.", "traps": {"a": "Criminal behaviour leading to imprisonment is a true outcome of conduct disorder, especially in Terrie Moffitt's (1993) life-course-persistent group, but it is only one of three correct statements.", "b": "Antisocial personality disorder is a true outcome, since DSM-5 requires conduct disorder before age 15 for an adult ASPD diagnosis, but choosing it alone leaves out the other two documented links.", "c": "Psychosis is a true but less obvious outcome: cohort studies such as the Dunedin study link childhood conduct problems with adult schizophreniform disorder, yet it is only part of the full answer."}}, {"q": "Where is Broca's area located?", "concept": "The answer is a: Broca's area lies in the frontal lobe, in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45) in most people, just in front of the part of the motor cortex that controls the face, tongue and lips. Paul Broca (1861) described a patient, Louis Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan' because it was nearly the only syllable he could say. Leborgne understood speech but could not produce it, and at autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left frontal lobe. This was the first strong evidence that a mental function sits in one region of the cortex and that language depends mainly on the left hemisphere. Damage causes Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech such as 'Walk... dog... park', with comprehension fairly well preserved. If Kabir has a left frontal stroke, he may understand his doctor's questions yet struggle to answer them. Option a is right because Broca's area is a speech production area in the frontal lobe. Exam trap: Wernicke's area (Carl Wernicke, 1874), the comprehension area, lies in the temporal lobe, and examiners swap the two names and their lobes.", "traps": {"b": "The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex and processes vision, not speech production.", "c": "The parietal lobe handles touch, body sense and spatial attention; its angular gyrus helps with reading and writing, but Broca's area is not there.", "d": "The temporal lobe holds Wernicke's area (Carl Wernicke, 1874), which supports language comprehension; it is the classic swap for Broca's frontal speech area."}}, {"q": "Afferent and efferent nerves are found in which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is c: afferent and efferent nerves are the two parts of the somatic nervous system. The nervous system is first split into the central nervous system (CNS: brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (PNS: all the nerves outside them). The PNS has two divisions. The somatic (voluntary) nervous system deals with the outside world. Its afferent (sensory) nerves carry signals from the skin, eyes, ears and muscles into the CNS, and its efferent (motor) nerves carry commands out to the skeletal muscles. The autonomic nervous system runs the internal organs and is itself split into sympathetic and parasympathetic branches. A simple memory aid: afferent arrives, efferent exits. When Riya feels a mosquito on her arm (an afferent signal in) and slaps it (an efferent command out to her arm muscles), she is using the somatic system. The 31 pairs of spinal nerves are mixed: sensory fibres enter through the dorsal root and motor fibres leave through the ventral root, the Bell-Magendie law (Charles Bell, 1811; Francois Magendie, 1822). Option c is right because the textbook diagram of the nervous system lists afferent and efferent nerves as the two branches of the somatic division. Exam trap: the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems are branches of the autonomic system, offered to tempt students who remember that the autonomic system also has inward and outward fibres. Key note: strictly the whole peripheral nervous system carries both kinds of fibre (the autonomic system has visceral afferents too), while inside the CNS bundles of axons are called tracts, not nerves. The somatic system is the textbook answer and stands.", "traps": {"a": "The sympathetic nervous system is the fight-or-flight branch of the autonomic system; it is described mainly by its outflow to the organs, not as the division the textbook labels afferent and efferent.", "b": "The parasympathetic nervous system is the rest-and-digest branch of the autonomic system; like the sympathetic branch it is a subdivision of the ANS, not the division that the textbook splits into afferent and efferent nerves.", "d": "The central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) receives afferent input and sends efferent output, but its own bundles of axons are called tracts; the afferent and efferent nerves themselves lie in the peripheral, somatic division."}}, {"q": "We usually avoid remembering something that is associated with fear or unpleasantness. This avoidance is termed as:", "concept": "The answer is b: the habit of avoiding memories that are linked with fear or unpleasantness is called repression. Sigmund Freud (1915), in his paper 'Repression', made it the cornerstone of psychoanalysis: the ego unconsciously and automatically blocks threatening memories, wishes and feelings from awareness, so the person is protected from anxiety. Because it happens outside awareness, the person does not know they are doing it; they simply cannot recall. Freud saw repression as the primary defence, with other defences acting as back-ups when it fails, and the loss of access it produces is often called motivated forgetting. The repressed material does not vanish: it can return in disguised form through dreams, slips of the tongue and neurotic symptoms. For example, Meera cannot remember anything about the day her father was rushed to hospital, though she clearly remembers the days before and after; she is not choosing to forget. Later laboratory work has shown that people can push unwanted memories out of mind, though whether true repression of trauma happens remains debated. Option b is right because the stem describes forgetting driven by the unpleasantness of the memory, which is the textbook definition of repression. Exam trap: suppression and repression differ in awareness; suppression is the conscious decision not to think about something, while repression is unconscious. Key note: suppression is the conscious, deliberate pushing away of a thought; the stem's habitual avoidance of remembering painful things is what textbooks call repression (motivated forgetting, Sigmund Freud), so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Suppression is the conscious, deliberate decision to put a thought out of mind, such as choosing not to worry about an exam until it is over; George Vaillant (1977) ranks it as a mature defence, while the stem describes automatic avoidance.", "c": "Retrieval failure is forgetting because the right cue is missing, as in the tip-of-the-tongue state or the encoding specificity principle of Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson (1973); it is not driven by the memory being unpleasant.", "d": "Forgetting is the general loss of stored information through decay, interference or retrieval failure; it is the broad category, while the stem names a specific, emotion-driven kind."}}, {"q": "______ glands secrete directly into the bloodstream.", "concept": "The answer is b: endocrine glands secrete their products, called hormones, directly into the bloodstream. 'Endocrine' means 'secreting within'. Endocrine glands have no ducts (they are ductless glands); their hormones pass into the capillaries around them and travel in the blood to target cells anywhere in the body that carry the right receptors. Ernest Starling (1905) coined the word 'hormone', from a Greek word meaning 'to set in motion'. The main endocrine glands are the pituitary (often called the master gland, itself controlled by the hypothalamus), the thyroid, the parathyroids, the adrenals, the pancreas (its islets of Langerhans release insulin and glucagon), the pineal gland (melatonin) and the gonads (testes and ovaries). Exocrine glands, by contrast, release their secretions through ducts onto a surface or into a cavity: sweat, tear, salivary and digestive glands. The pancreas is both: its islets are endocrine and its digestive juice is exocrine. When Kabir is frightened before a viva, his adrenal glands pour adrenaline into his blood and within seconds his heart beats faster; here the endocrine system works alongside the sympathetic nervous system. Hormones act more slowly than nerve signals but their effects last longer. Option b is right because only endocrine glands release their secretions straight into the blood. Exam trap: 'duct glands' sounds like a separate category, but it is simply another name for exocrine glands, which by definition do not secrete into the bloodstream.", "traps": {"a": "Exocrine glands, such as sweat, tear and salivary glands, release their secretions through ducts onto a body surface or into a cavity, not into the blood.", "c": "Duct glands is another name for exocrine glands; the duct carries the secretion to a surface, which is exactly why they do not empty into the bloodstream.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because endocrine glands, option b, do secrete directly into the bloodstream."}}, {"q": "Which part of the brain is responsible for transferring short term memory to long term memory?", "concept": "The answer is b: the hippocampus is the brain structure that transfers (consolidates) new information from short-term into long-term memory. The hippocampus is a curved, seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system. It is not the permanent store; instead it binds the pieces of a new experience together and, over days to years, helps the cortex lay down a lasting copy, a process called consolidation. The classic evidence is the patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). In 1953 the surgeon William Scoville removed most of both medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampi, to control H.M.'s epilepsy, and William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported the result. H.M. kept his intelligence, his short-term memory span and most of his older memories, but he could no longer form new long-term memories for facts and events (anterograde amnesia). He could still learn new motor skills such as mirror drawing without remembering the practice, showing that procedural memory relies on other structures. The hippocampus also builds maps of space; London taxi drivers who learned the city's streets were found to have a larger posterior hippocampus. If Riya's grandmother has early Alzheimer's disease, which strikes the hippocampus first, she can chat normally but forgets the conversation within minutes. Option b is right because consolidation from short-term to long-term memory depends on the hippocampus. Exam trap: the amygdala sits right next to the hippocampus and also affects memory, but its job is to add emotional strength (especially fear) to memories, not to transfer ordinary facts into long-term storage.", "traps": {"a": "The cerebellum supports motor coordination, the timing of movements and procedural skill learning such as eyeblink conditioning, not the consolidation of facts and events into long-term memory.", "c": "The amygdala adds emotional intensity, especially fear, to memories and drives fear conditioning; it changes how strongly an event is stored but is not the main transfer station from short-term to long-term memory.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because the hippocampus, option b, is the textbook structure for consolidating short-term into long-term memory."}}, {"q": "The term used to refer to the first two weeks of life is:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Elizabeth Hurlock's lifespan scheme, the first two weeks of life are called infancy. Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), whose Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach is the standard text in Indian courses, divides life into ten periods: prenatal (conception to birth), infancy (birth to the end of the second week), babyhood (2 weeks to 2 years), early childhood (2 to 6 years), late childhood (6 to 10 or 12), puberty, adolescence, early adulthood, middle age and old age. Her infancy is short because it is the period of adjustment to life outside the womb: the baby must start breathing, feeding, controlling body temperature and eliminating on its own. She splits it into two parts: the partunate period, from birth until the umbilical cord is cut (about 15 to 30 minutes), and the neonate period, from then to the end of the second week. During these days the newborn often loses a little weight and then regains it; once the adjustments are made, babyhood begins. In everyday English 'infancy' means roughly the first year or two, which Hurlock calls babyhood, so the word is easy to misread. If baby Arjun is ten days old, Hurlock would place him in infancy (the neonate part); at three months he is in babyhood. Option a is right because Hurlock's own label for birth to two weeks is infancy. Exam trap: everyday usage makes students think infancy covers the first year, and 'neonatal' sounds like the medical word for newborns, so both lead away from Hurlock's term. Key note: in medicine the neonatal period is the first 28 days, and Hurlock's neonate period sits inside infancy, so b is the near miss; Hurlock's term for the whole first two weeks is infancy, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The neonatal stage is the medical name for the first 28 days, and in Elizabeth Hurlock's scheme the neonate period is only the second part of infancy (from cutting the cord to the end of week 2), so it is a near miss rather than her label for the whole two weeks.", "c": "Early childhood in Elizabeth Hurlock's scheme runs from 2 to 6 years, the preschool years, long after the first two weeks.", "d": "The preoperational stage is the second stage of cognitive development of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), about 2 to 7 years, marked by symbolic thought and egocentrism, not a period of the newborn."}}, {"q": "The act in which people help someone without any personal interest or motive is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: helping someone without any personal interest or motive falls under prosocial behaviour. Prosocial behaviour is any voluntary action that benefits another person, such as sharing, comforting, donating, volunteering or rescuing. Within it, social psychologists pick out altruism: helping done purely for the other person's welfare, with no expectation of reward and often at a cost to oneself. Whether true altruism exists is a long debate. C. Daniel Batson (1981) argued in his empathy-altruism hypothesis that empathy produces a genuinely selfless wish to help: in his studies, students who felt high empathy for a suffering young woman, 'Elaine', offered to take her electric shocks even when they could easily leave. Robert Cialdini (1973) replied with the negative-state relief model, which says we help mainly to ease our own distress. Evolutionary accounts add kin selection (William Hamilton, 1964) and reciprocal altruism (Robert Trivers, 1971). Picture Meera stopping on a busy Kolkata road to help an elderly stranger who has fallen, then leaving without giving her name; she gains nothing and loses time, so her act is prosocial and, more precisely, altruistic. Option c is right because helping with no personal gain is the defining form of prosocial behaviour, and it is the only option that names helping at all. Exam trap: 'social obligation' sounds like helping, but helping because a norm demands it is a motive, which the stem rules out. Key note: strictly, helping with no expected reward is altruism, a subset of prosocial behaviour; since altruism is not offered, the umbrella term prosocial behaviour is the best fit, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Nurturance is the caring, protective tendency to look after the young or helpless; Henry Murray (1938) listed it as a psychological need (n Nurturance), so it is a motive, which the stem excludes.", "b": "Social obligation is helping because a norm requires it, such as the norm of reciprocity of Alvin Gouldner (1960) or the social responsibility norm; it is helping with a motive, not without one.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because prosocial behaviour, option c, is the umbrella term that includes selfless helping (altruism)."}}, {"q": "Short term memory holds information for ______ seconds.", "concept": "The answer is a: short-term memory (STM) holds information for about 30 seconds or less unless it is rehearsed. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information passes from sensory memory (under a second for visual traces) into STM, and from there into long-term memory (LTM), which is effectively unlimited and can last a lifetime. STM is small as well as brief. George Miller (1956) put its capacity at 'seven, plus or minus two' chunks, and Nelson Cowan (2001) argued it is nearer four when chunking and rehearsal are blocked. Its duration was measured by John Brown (1958) and by Lloyd Peterson and Margaret Peterson (1959) using the Brown-Peterson technique: participants heard a consonant trigram such as KBF, then counted backwards in threes to stop rehearsal. Recall was about 80 per cent after 3 seconds but fell to about 10 per cent by 18 seconds, so textbooks give STM a duration of about 15 to 30 seconds. Geoffrey Keppel and Benton Underwood (1962) later showed that much of this loss comes from interference by earlier trigrams rather than pure decay. Rehearsal can keep an item alive as long as you like: if Kabir repeats an OTP to himself, it lasts until he types it in, but if someone interrupts him he loses it within seconds. Option a is right because, without rehearsal, STM holds information for roughly 15 to 30 seconds, which is 30 seconds or less. Exam trap: 'seven plus or minus two' is the capacity of STM, not its duration in seconds, and examiners mix up the two numbers.", "traps": {"b": "50 seconds is longer than unrehearsed short-term memory lasts; the data of Lloyd Peterson and Margaret Peterson (1959) show recall near 10 per cent by 18 seconds.", "c": "More than 50 seconds describes information kept alive by rehearsal or transferred to long-term memory, not the natural span of short-term memory.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because the textbook duration of about 15 to 30 seconds fits option a."}}, {"q": "Delusion is a disorder of:", "concept": "The answer is b: a delusion is a disorder of thought, specifically of the content of thought. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, gave the classic marks of a delusion: it is held with extraordinary conviction, it cannot be changed by contrary evidence or argument, and its content is false or impossible. Modern definitions add that it is out of keeping with the person's cultural and educational background, so a belief shared by a whole community, such as faith in a deity, is not a delusion. Psychopathology splits thought disorders into two kinds. Disorders of thought content concern what a person thinks: delusions, overvalued ideas and obsessions. Disorders of thought form concern how thoughts are linked: loosening of associations, flight of ideas, tangentiality, circumstantiality and thought blocking. Common delusional themes are persecutory, grandiose, referential, jealous, nihilistic and somatic. Delusions are a core symptom of schizophrenia and delusional disorder, and they also occur in psychotic depression and mania. For example, Kabir insists that his neighbours are spying on him through cameras hidden in his ceiling fan; when his family opens the fan and shows him it is empty, he says they have been bribed. That fixed false belief is a disorder of thought content. Option b is right because a delusion is a false belief, and beliefs are products of thinking, not of sensing or feeling. Exam trap: hallucinations and delusions are often listed together as psychotic symptoms, but a hallucination (a perception without a stimulus) is a disorder of perception, while a delusion is a disorder of thought.", "traps": {"a": "Disorders of perception are hallucinations (perception without a stimulus) and illusions (misperception of a real stimulus); a delusion is a false belief, not a false perception.", "c": "Disorders of personality are enduring, inflexible patterns of inner experience and behaviour (the personality disorders); a delusion is a symptom of disordered thought, usually in psychosis, not a personality pattern.", "d": "Disorders of affect concern mood and emotional expression, such as depression, elation, or blunted and incongruent affect; delusions may be coloured by mood but are disorders of thought."}}, {"q": "The term free association was coined by:", "concept": "The answer is c: free association, as a technique and a term in psychotherapy, comes from Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). Working on hysteria with Josef Breuer (their Studies on Hysteria appeared in 1895), Freud first used hypnosis, then a 'pressure technique', pressing the patient's forehead and insisting that a memory would come. Many patients could not be hypnotised, so during the 1890s he replaced both with free association: the patient lies on a couch, the analyst sits out of sight, and the patient follows one 'fundamental rule', to say whatever comes to mind, however trivial, embarrassing or illogical, without censoring it. Freud believed in psychic determinism, so no idea was random; each link in the chain led back towards a buried conflict. When the chain stalls, the patient is showing resistance, and the feelings the patient comes to direct at the analyst show transference; both are worked through in therapy. Free association, with dream analysis (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), became the central method of psychoanalysis. For example, Riya begins by talking about missing a train, drifts to her fear of being left behind, and then reaches a forgotten memory of being lost at a mela as a small child. Option c is right because Freud created and named free association as the core method of psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) is tempting because he used the word association test, in which a person answers a stimulus word with the first word that comes to mind, but that is a structured test, not free association. Key note: Francis Galton (1879) first ran word-association experiments on himself, but free association as a therapy method (say whatever comes to mind) was Sigmund Freud's, developed in the 1890s in place of hypnosis; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) founded individual psychology, known for the inferiority complex, striving for superiority, birth order and style of life; he did not create free association.", "b": "Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994; the option spells it Erickson) is known for the eight psychosocial stages of Childhood and Society (1950), not free association; the hypnotherapist Milton Erickson (1901 to 1980) is also unrelated.", "d": "Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) developed the word association test and the idea of complexes, a structured cousin of free association; the free association method itself is Sigmund Freud's."}}, {"q": "The term IQ was introduced by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the term intelligence quotient (IQ) was introduced by the German psychologist William Stern (1912). Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test in Paris, and their 1908 revision expressed a child's result as a mental age (MA): the age level of the items the child could pass. Mental age alone is hard to compare across ages, because being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. Stern's solution was to divide mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a quotient, the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916) then multiplied the ratio by 100 to remove the decimals in his Stanford-Binet, giving IQ = MA/CA x 100, and made the abbreviation IQ popular. So if Arjun is 8 years old with a mental age of 10, his IQ is 10/8 x 100 = 125. The ratio IQ later ran into trouble with adults, because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), which compares a person with others of the same age. Option c is right because Stern coined both the quotient and its name. Exam trap: Terman is the most common wrong answer because the Stanford-Binet made IQ famous, but he popularised the term rather than introducing it. Key note: William Stern (1912) coined the intelligence quotient (Intelligenzquotient, mental age divided by chronological age); Lewis Terman (1916, Stanford-Binet) multiplied it by 100 and made the abbreviation IQ popular, so Terman is the trap; Stern stands.", "traps": {"a": "Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied William Stern's ratio by 100 in the Stanford-Binet and popularised the abbreviation IQ, but he did not introduce the term.", "b": "Gordon Allport (1937) is a trait theorist known for cardinal, central and secondary traits and for his definition of personality; he had nothing to do with IQ.", "d": "John B. Watson (1913) founded behaviourism with his manifesto 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It' and ran the Little Albert study (1920); he did not coin IQ."}}, {"q": "To compute IQ which formula is used?", "concept": "The answer is a: the classic formula for IQ is mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100 (MA/CA x 100). This is the ratio IQ. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test, and their 1908 revision expressed results as a mental age (MA): the age level at which a child performs. William Stern (1912) divided MA by chronological age (CA) to form the intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied it by 100 in the Stanford-Binet to clear the decimals. When MA equals CA, the IQ is exactly 100, which is average. A child ahead of her age scores above 100 and a child behind scores below. Worked example: Meera is 10 years old and passes test items up to the 12-year level, so her IQ is 12/10 x 100 = 120. Her classmate Kabir, also 10, has a mental age of 8, so his IQ is 8/10 x 100 = 80. The ratio IQ fails for adults: mental age stops climbing in the mid-teens while chronological age keeps rising, so a 40-year-old would seem to lose intelligence every year. David Wechsler (1939) therefore introduced the deviation IQ, which places a person's score on a normal curve for their own age group, with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Option a is right because it is the only option that divides mental age by chronological age and then multiplies by 100. Exam trap: the inverted ratio, CA/MA x 100, is the usual slip; remember that a mental age below chronological age must give an IQ below 100, so MA goes on top.", "traps": {"b": "CA/MA x 100 inverts the ratio: a 10-year-old with a mental age of 8 would wrongly score 125 instead of 80.", "c": "CA/MA minus 100 both inverts the ratio and subtracts instead of multiplying, giving meaningless numbers near minus 99.", "d": "MA/CA plus 100 keeps the right ratio but adds 100 instead of multiplying, so every score would sit just above 100 whatever the child's ability."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a type of thinking?", "concept": "The answer is c: 'positive thinking' is not one of the types of thinking described in psychology; it is a popular self-help idea. Thinking is the mental manipulation of representations (images, concepts, symbols and language) to understand, solve problems, reason and decide. Standard educational psychology texts, especially those used in India, classify thinking by its level and purpose. Perceptual or concrete thinking works with objects and events present to the senses, like a young child counting real mangoes. Conceptual or abstract thinking uses concepts and symbols that go beyond what is in front of us, such as 'justice' or 'x + 3 = 7'. Problem-solving or reflective thinking is goal-directed: it defines a problem, gathers facts, tries solutions and checks them, in the steps John Dewey (1910) set out in How We Think. Other listed types include creative or divergent thinking (Joy Paul Guilford, 1950), critical thinking and associative or non-directed thinking such as daydreaming. 'Positive thinking' comes instead from Norman Vincent Peale's (1952) bestseller The Power of Positive Thinking; it describes an optimistic attitude, not a cognitive process with its own structure. Psychology studies optimism under other names, such as the learned optimism of Martin Seligman (1990). For example, when Arjun works out why his scooter will not start by checking the fuel and then the spark plug, he is using problem-solving thinking; telling himself 'it will all be fine' is an attitude, not a type of thought. Option c is right because positive thinking is a self-help outlook rather than a recognised category of thinking. Exam trap: the word 'thinking' appears in all four options, so students assume each is a textbook category; look for the one that describes a mood or outlook rather than a mental process.", "traps": {"a": "Perceptual (concrete) thinking, the most basic level, works with objects and events directly present to the senses; it is a standard category in textbook lists of types of thinking.", "b": "Conceptual (abstract) thinking uses concepts and symbols beyond the here and now, as in algebra or moral ideas; it is a recognised type of thinking.", "d": "Problem-solving (reflective) thinking is goal-directed reasoning through steps such as those of John Dewey (1910); it is one of the core recognised types of thinking."}}, {"q": "According to Thorndike all of the following are law of learning except:", "concept": "The answer is d: there is no 'law of memory' among Edward Thorndike's laws of learning. Edward Thorndike (1898) put hungry cats in puzzle boxes with food outside. At first a cat scratched and pushed at random; by accident it worked the latch that opened the door, and over many trials its escape time fell gradually, not suddenly. Thorndike called this trial-and-error learning and explained it through connectionism: learning is the forming of bonds between a stimulus and a response. He proposed three primary laws. The law of readiness says that when a person is ready to act, acting is satisfying and being prevented is annoying. The law of exercise says that bonds are strengthened by use (law of use) and weakened by disuse (law of disuse), so practice matters. The law of effect, set out in Animal Intelligence (1911), says that responses followed by satisfaction are stamped in and those followed by discomfort are stamped out; it became the root of the operant conditioning of Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1938). Thorndike also listed subsidiary laws such as multiple response, attitude (set), associative shifting and response by analogy. Around 1930 he weakened the law of exercise and the punishment half of the law of effect, after finding that practice without feedback, and punishment, did little. For example, Riya learns sums faster when she is eager to start (readiness), practises daily (exercise) and is praised for correct answers (effect). Option d is right because readiness, exercise and effect are Thorndike's three laws, and memory is not among them. Exam trap: the law of disuse sounds like forgetting, which tempts students to accept a 'law of memory', but disuse is part of the law of exercise.", "traps": {"a": "The law of readiness is one of Edward Thorndike's three primary laws: using a bond is satisfying when the learner is ready, and being blocked is frustrating.", "b": "The law of exercise (or practice) is a genuine law of Edward Thorndike: use strengthens a stimulus-response bond and disuse weakens it.", "c": "The law of effect (1911) is Edward Thorndike's best-known law: satisfying consequences strengthen a response and annoying ones weaken it, the forerunner of operant reinforcement."}}, {"q": "The MMPI is used to measure:", "concept": "The answer is c: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) measures personality and psychological disorders (psychopathology). Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built it at the University of Minnesota Hospitals to aid psychiatric diagnosis. They used empirical criterion keying: hundreds of true or false statements were given to patient groups with known diagnoses and to about 700 'Minnesota normals', and an item was kept for a scale only if it separated the groups, whatever its content seemed to be about. The result was 10 clinical scales: hypochondriasis, depression, hysteria, psychopathic deviate, masculinity-femininity, paranoia, psychasthenia, schizophrenia, hypomania and social introversion. Validity scales guard against distorted answering: L (lie, faking good), F (infrequency, faking bad or random answering) and K (defensiveness), plus the 'cannot say' count. Scores are read as a profile, often by the two highest scales (two-point codes). The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 items; shorter versions, the MMPI-2-RF (2008) and the MMPI-3 (2020), followed, and the MMPI-A serves adolescents. It is the most widely used and researched objective personality test in clinical work. For example, if Kabir's MMPI-2 shows high depression and psychasthenia scores with acceptable L, F and K, the clinician may suspect depression with anxiety and plan a fuller interview. Option c is right because the MMPI was designed to assess both personality characteristics and clinical disorders. Exam trap: the MMPI is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test, so 'unconscious drives' is wrong; and it was not built on the Big Five, which belongs to the NEO inventories.", "traps": {"a": "Unconscious drives are the target of projective techniques such as the Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) and the TAT (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), not of the MMPI's true or false items.", "b": "The Big Five traits are measured by the NEO inventories of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (NEO PI-R, 1992); the MMPI's scales came from clinical criterion groups, not the five-factor model.", "d": "Leadership potential is judged with work-oriented tools such as assessment centres or the California Psychological Inventory (Harrison Gough, 1957); the MMPI is a clinical instrument."}}, {"q": "According to Eysenck extraverts seek to ________ their arousal while introverts seek to _________ their arousal.", "concept": "The answer is c: according to Hans Eysenck, extraverts seek to increase their arousal while introverts seek to decrease it. In The Biological Basis of Personality, Hans Eysenck (1967) linked his extraversion-introversion dimension to the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS), the brainstem network that sets how alert and aroused the cerebral cortex is. He proposed that introverts have a higher resting level of cortical arousal. People feel and perform best at a moderate, optimal level of arousal, the inverted U of the Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson, 1908). Introverts already sit near or above that level, so extra stimulation overloads them and they avoid noise, crowds and risk. Extraverts start below it, so they are chronically under-aroused and seek stimulation, such as parties, music and novelty, to bring their arousal up. The evidence fits: when a few drops of lemon juice are put on the tongue, introverts salivate more than extraverts, and introverts are more sensitive to faint stimuli, tolerate pain less and condition faster. Later research supported these differences more firmly than the exact brain mechanism. Picture two hostel roommates: Kabir studies with loud music and friends chatting around him, while Meera goes to the quiet library corner. On Eysenck's theory Kabir is raising his low arousal and Meera is protecting her already high arousal. Option c is right because it matches the direction of the theory: under-aroused extraverts increase stimulation and over-aroused introverts reduce it. Exam trap: option a reverses the theory, and the reversal is tempting because introverts look calm; remember that their quiet behaviour is a way of lowering arousal that is already high.", "traps": {"a": "Decrease, increase reverses the theory of Hans Eysenck (1967): it is introverts, with high resting cortical arousal, who seek to lower stimulation, and extraverts who seek to raise it.", "b": "Hide, reveal confuses arousal with self-disclosure; the theory of Hans Eysenck (1967) is about levels of cortical arousal, not about concealing feelings.", "d": "Reveal, hide also turns arousal into a question of openness; the theory concerns seeking or avoiding stimulation, not revealing or hiding anything."}}, {"q": "Projective tests claim to reveal information about:", "concept": "The answer is c: projective tests claim to reveal unconscious processes: hidden needs, conflicts, fears and wishes that a person cannot or will not report directly. They rest on the projective hypothesis named by Lawrence Frank (1939): when people respond to an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they must impose their own meaning on it, and in doing so they project their inner world onto it. The idea grows from the concept of projection of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), the defence of attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others, and from the psychoanalytic belief that much of personality is unconscious. The best-known projective tests are the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921; ten cards), the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), in which people tell stories about ambiguous pictures, sentence completion tests and drawing tests such as Draw-A-Person. Because the task is open-ended and its purpose disguised, faking is harder than on a questionnaire, though scoring is less reliable and validity is often criticised. For example, shown the TAT card of a boy looking at a violin, Arjun tells a story about a child forced to practise by a strict father who keeps failing; a clinician might take this as a clue to Arjun's own feelings about parental pressure. Option c is right because uncovering unconscious material through ambiguous stimuli is the stated aim of projective testing. Exam trap: projective tests are about personality and the unconscious, not abilities; career aptitude and intellectual attainment are measured by structured aptitude and achievement tests with right and wrong answers.", "traps": {"a": "Career aptitude is measured by structured aptitude batteries such as the Differential Aptitude Tests (1947), which predict future performance in specific areas; projective tests do not aim at it.", "b": "Intellectual attainment is measured by achievement and intelligence tests with right and wrong answers, such as the scales of David Wechsler (1939); projective tests have no correct answers.", "d": "Parenting style is assessed by observation, interviews and questionnaires built on the authoritative, authoritarian and permissive styles of Diana Baumrind (1966), not by projective tests."}}, {"q": "The relative apparent motion of objects in the visual field as the viewer moves the head is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the relative apparent motion of objects in the visual field as the viewer moves the head is called motion parallax. When you move, objects at different distances seem to move at different speeds and in different directions across your view. Near objects sweep past quickly in the direction opposite to your movement, while far objects move slowly and seem to travel with you. The brain reads these speed differences as distance: the faster something slides across the retina, the nearer it is. James J. Gibson (1950), in The Perception of the Visual World, widened the idea into optic flow, the whole pattern of motion that streams across the eyes as we move, which guides walking, driving and landing an aircraft. Motion parallax is a monocular depth cue, since it works with one eye, and a kinetic cue, since it needs movement. It sits beside the other monocular cues (linear perspective, relative size, interposition, texture gradient, aerial perspective and accommodation) and the two binocular cues (retinal disparity and convergence). The classic example: Arjun looks out of a train window and sees the electric poles beside the track flash backwards, while distant hills and the moon seem to glide along with the train. Option c is right because motion parallax is the name for the depth information carried by the different apparent motions of near and far objects when the head moves. Exam trap: accommodation is also a monocular depth cue, so it sounds plausible, but it comes from the lens changing shape to focus, not from movement.", "traps": {"a": "The image-retina system is the term of Richard Gregory (1966, Eye and Brain) for one way we detect movement, when an image runs across a still retina; it explains seeing motion, not the depth cue created by moving the head.", "b": "Accommodation is the change in the lens's shape, made by the ciliary muscles, to focus on near or far objects; it is a monocular depth cue that works without any head movement and only at short distances.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because motion parallax, option c, is the exact name for this cue."}}, {"q": "Clinical psychology's major contribution to World War 1 was:", "concept": "The answer is a: clinical psychology's major contribution to World War I was the creation of tests to measure the intelligence of recruits. When the United States entered the war in 1917, the army needed to sort well over a million men quickly. Robert Yerkes (1917), then president of the American Psychological Association, chaired a committee of psychologists that built two group intelligence tests. The Army Alpha was a written test for literate, English-speaking recruits, with items on arithmetic, analogies, number series and general information. The Army Beta was a non-verbal, pictorial test for men who could not read or did not speak English, using mazes, picture completion and cube counting. About 1.75 million recruits were tested, and the scores were used to screen out men judged unfit and to pick candidates for officer training. In the same year Robert Woodworth (1917) wrote the Personal Data Sheet to screen soldiers likely to break down under fire, the forerunner of personality inventories. The war showed that tests could be given to huge groups at once, which set off a boom in group testing in schools and industry in the 1920s, and for decades testing remained the clinical psychologist's main job. Imagine Arjun, a farm worker who never went to school: he would have taken the Beta, not the Alpha. Option a is right because the army intelligence tests were psychology's signature contribution to the First World War. Exam trap: shell shock sounds like the obvious clinical task, but during the war it was treated mainly by army doctors and psychiatrists. Key note: shell shock treatment was mostly psychiatry's work; clinical psychology's big move into therapy came after World War II, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Treating shell shock was mainly the work of army doctors and psychiatrists in World War I; clinical psychology's large move into psychotherapy came after World War II, when the Veterans Administration trained psychologists as therapists from 1946.", "c": "Interrogation techniques were not a contribution of clinical psychology in World War I; psychological work on interrogation came much later, in the Second World War and Cold War era, and remains ethically controversial.", "d": "Selecting and training pilots became a major task of aviation psychology in World War II (the US Army Air Forces devised the stanine score there), not clinical psychology's main contribution to World War I."}}, {"q": "Behaviour modification is an example of which of the following treatment methods?", "concept": "The answer is a: behaviour modification is a treatment that helps the individual unlearn maladaptive behaviours and emotions and learn adaptive ones in their place. Behaviour modification (used almost interchangeably with behaviour therapy) applies experimentally established principles of learning to clinical problems. Its core assumption is that problem behaviour is learned by the same laws as normal behaviour, so it can be unlearned. The behaviour itself is the target, not a hidden conflict behind it, and what keeps the problem going now matters more than how it began. Hans Eysenck (1952) argued that a treatment built on learning theory should replace talk therapy, and Joseph Wolpe (1958) and Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1953) supplied the classical and operant tools. The name behaviour therapy first appeared in a 1953 report by Ogden Lindsley, Skinner and Harry Solomon. Classical methods (systematic desensitisation, flooding) extinguish or countercondition learned fears; operant methods (token economies, shaping, extinction, time-out) change behaviour through its consequences. Example: Kabir panics in lifts after getting stuck once. His therapist does not ask about his childhood; she teaches him relaxation and builds a graded hierarchy of lift rides, so the conditioned fear is unlearned. Option a is right because unlearning maladaptive behaviour and emotion is the defining aim of the behavioural approach. Exam trap: b describes psychoanalysis, which digs into childhood. d sounds behavioural because therapists and trained parents do change other people's behaviour, but the stem asks what the treatment does for the individual in treatment.", "traps": {"b": "Exploring childhood experiences is the method of psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy (Sigmund Freud), which seeks insight into unconscious conflicts; behaviour modification deliberately sets the distant past aside and works on present behaviour.", "c": "Analysing other people's behaviour is what an observer, researcher or attribution theorist does; it is not a treatment goal for a client, and behaviour modification works on the client's own behaviour.", "d": "Modifying others' behaviour describes the change agent (a therapist, teacher or parent in parent training), not what the treatment does for the person in therapy, who learns to change his or her own responses."}}, {"q": "The stage of psycho-social developments in which basic trust vs. mistrust develops:", "concept": "The answer is b: Erik Erikson's first psychosocial crisis, basic trust versus mistrust, belongs to the first year of life (birth to about 1 year). Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages across the whole lifespan. Each stage is a crisis between two poles, and a good resolution leaves a virtue. In stage 1, infancy (Sigmund Freud's oral stage), the helpless baby learns whether the world is dependable. If feeding, comfort and contact come reliably and warmly, the infant develops basic trust and the virtue of hope; if care is cold, rejecting or unpredictable, mistrust sets in. The key relationship is the maternal person. Erikson held that a little mistrust is healthy, but trust must outweigh it. Example: baby Meera cries and her mother comes, feeds and soothes her, day after day. Meera learns that people can be counted on, a template for her later relationships. The next stages are autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3 years, will), initiative versus guilt (3 to 6, purpose) and industry versus inferiority (6 to 11 or puberty, competence). Option b is right because trust versus mistrust is resolved in the first year, before the toddler's push for autonomy. Exam trap: some texts stretch stage 1 to 18 months, which tempts students towards 1 to 3 years; but 1 to 3 is autonomy versus shame and doubt, the toilet-training stage. Every option here is a real Erikson age band, so match the crisis to its band.", "traps": {"a": "1 to 3 years is Erikson's second stage, autonomy versus shame and doubt (Freud's anal stage), whose virtue is will; trust has already been settled by then.", "c": "3 to 6 years is Erikson's third stage, initiative versus guilt (Freud's phallic stage), whose virtue is purpose.", "d": "6 to 11 years is Erikson's fourth stage, industry versus inferiority (Freud's latency period), whose virtue is competence."}}, {"q": "At which stage the child begins to utter words to ask for something rather than just reaching out to get it:", "concept": "The answer is a: using words to ask for something, instead of reaching out to grab it, marks Jean Piaget's preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years). Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) described four stages of cognitive development. In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) the infant knows the world through action: looking, grasping, sucking and reaching. Near its end the child gains mental representation, and the preoperational stage opens with the symbolic (semiotic) function: the ability to let one thing stand for another. Words, mental images, deferred imitation and pretend play all use it. The child no longer has to act on an object to deal with it; a word can now do the work. Thought is still preoperational, though: it is egocentric, centred on one feature at a time and not reversible, so conservation fails. Example: at 10 months Arjun stretches and whines towards the biscuit tin; at two and a half he says 'Biscuit, please' from across the room. That shift from action to symbol is the change of stage. Option a is right because the stem contrasts symbolic requesting with physical reaching, the hallmark of preoperational thought. Exam trap: first words arrive around the first birthday, so 'sensory motor' looks right, but the stem describes words replacing actions, not the first word itself. Key note: first words appear around 12 months, inside Jean Piaget's sensorimotor stage, so d is the near miss; but using words as symbols in place of acting on objects (the symbolic or semiotic function) is the hallmark of the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years), which is what the stem describes, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) brings logical, reversible operations on concrete objects, such as conservation and classification; children use words long before it.", "c": "The formal operational stage (about 11 years onwards) brings abstract, hypothetico-deductive reasoning; it has nothing to do with first using words to request things.", "d": "The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) is the stage of knowing through action, the reaching the stem contrasts with; first words appear late in it, but replacing action with symbols defines the next stage."}}, {"q": "According to Piaget the basic unit with which the cognitive structure is built up is:", "concept": "The answer is a: for Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), the schema (or scheme) is the basic unit from which cognitive structure is built. A schema is an organised pattern of action or thought for dealing with a class of experiences. A newborn's sucking and grasping are schemas; so are a ten-year-old's adding and classifying. Cognitive structure is simply the whole set of a child's schemas and the way they are organised. Schemas change through two functional invariants. Organisation combines simple schemas into larger systems, as looking and grasping merge into reaching for what one sees. Adaptation fits schemas to the world through two processes: assimilation takes new experience into an existing schema, and accommodation changes the schema when the experience will not fit. When schemas handle events smoothly the child is in equilibrium; a misfit causes disequilibrium, and the self-regulating drive back to balance, equilibration, moves the child from stage to stage. Example: two-year-old Riya's 'doggie' schema means any four-legged animal, so she calls a cow 'doggie' (assimilation). When it moos and her father says 'cow', she splits the schema in two (accommodation). Option a is right because assimilation, accommodation and equilibrium all act on schemas; the schema is the unit they work with. Exam trap: examiners list the processes beside the unit. Assimilation and accommodation are things done to schemas, and equilibrium is a state of balance among them, so none of them is the building block itself.", "traps": {"b": "Accommodation is Piaget's process of changing an existing schema to fit new experience; it reshapes the unit but is not the unit.", "c": "Equilibrium is the balanced state when schemas fit experience, and equilibration is the drive to restore it; it is the motor of development, not its building block.", "d": "Assimilation is Piaget's process of fitting new experience into an existing schema without changing it; it uses the unit rather than being it."}}, {"q": "One of the following part of the brain controls impulsive behaviour:", "concept": "The answer is b: the prefrontal cortex, the front part of the frontal lobe, is the brain region that controls impulsive behaviour. The prefrontal cortex runs the executive functions: planning, judgement, working memory, set-shifting and, crucially, response inhibition, stopping an urge before it becomes an act. The classic evidence is Phineas Gage. In 1848 a tamping iron passed through his frontal lobe in a railway blast; his speech, memory and intelligence survived, but the reliable foreman became impulsive, profane and poor in judgement. His physician John Martyn Harlow (1868) reported friends saying he was 'no longer Gage', and Hanna Damasio and colleagues (1994) placed the damage in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex. Orbitofrontal and ventromedial damage gives a disinhibited, socially inappropriate picture; dorsolateral damage gives a dysexecutive syndrome with perseveration. The prefrontal cortex is also the last region to mature, into the mid-20s, which helps explain adolescent risk taking, and weak prefrontal control features in ADHD and antisocial behaviour. Example: Arjun, 17, wants to overtake on a blind bend; his prefrontal 'brake' weighs the danger against the thrill and stops him. Option b is right because impulse control is a prefrontal executive function, shown by lesion, imaging and developmental evidence. Exam trap: 'cerebrum' is tempting because the prefrontal cortex sits inside it, but the cerebrum is the whole forebrain mass with sensory, motor and language areas too; the specific region is the answer.", "traps": {"a": "The cerebrum is the whole of the two cerebral hemispheres, including sensory, motor, visual and language areas; it contains the prefrontal cortex but is far too broad to name the impulse-control region.", "c": "The cerebellum (hindbrain) coordinates balance, posture, timing and motor learning; damage causes ataxia and intention tremor, not disinhibition.", "d": "The left hemisphere is dominant for language in most people (Broca's and Wernicke's areas); impulse control depends on prefrontal cortex in both hemispheres, not on one side."}}, {"q": "The theoretical position that says the whole is more than the sum of the parts is:", "concept": "The answer is b: Gestalt psychology holds that the whole is more than (strictly, different from) the sum of its parts. Gestalt is German for an organised whole or form. The school began when Max Wertheimer (1912) reported the phi phenomenon: two lines flashed about 60 milliseconds apart look like one line moving. The motion lies in neither stimulus, so no adding up of sensations can produce it. With Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) and Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941), Wertheimer attacked structuralism, which broke consciousness into elements, and behaviourism, which broke behaviour into reflexes. Gestalt psychologists studied wholes and relations instead: figure and ground, the laws of grouping (proximity, similarity, closure, continuity, Prägnanz), Kohler's insight in chimpanzees and the field theory of Kurt Lewin (1890 to 1947). Koffka preferred 'different from' to 'more than', because the whole has its own properties, not just extra ones. Example: Meera hears a song played in a different key. Every note has changed, yet she recognises the melody at once, because the tune lives in the relations between notes, not in the notes themselves. Option b is right because the whole-over-parts slogan is the founding claim of Gestalt psychology. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt's (1832 to 1920) creative synthesis and John Stuart Mill's (1806 to 1873) mental chemistry also say a whole gains new properties, so setters sometimes bait with structuralism or associationism; but the slogan and the theoretical position belong to Gestalt.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive psychology (the cognitive revolution from 1956; Ulric Neisser's Cognitive Psychology, 1967) studies the mind as an information processor, often breaking tasks into stages, which is close to the opposite of a holistic slogan.", "c": "Behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913) is elementistic: it builds behaviour out of stimulus-response links and reflexes, the very analysis Gestalt rejected.", "d": "Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) explains behaviour through unconscious conflict among id, ego and superego; it has no whole-and-parts principle of perception."}}, {"q": "______ is a negative emotional response or dislike towards members of a group.", "concept": "The answer is a: prejudice is a negative emotional response, an attitude of dislike, towards people because they belong to a particular group. Social psychologists split intergroup bias into three parts that match the ABC model of attitudes. Prejudice is the affective part (A): a feeling of dislike or hostility. Gordon Allport (1954), in The Nature of Prejudice, defined it as \"an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalization\". A stereotype is the cognitive part (C): a belief about the traits typical of a group, which the journalist Walter Lippmann (1922) called \"pictures in our heads\". Discrimination is the behavioural part (B): unfair negative action towards a group's members, such as refusing them a flat or a job. The three often travel together but can separate: a person can hold a stereotype without hostility, or discriminate because of a rule without personal dislike. Allport also ranked prejudice in action on a five-step scale, from antilocution (hostile talk) through avoidance, discrimination and physical attack to extermination. Example: Kabir believes people from another state are lazy (stereotype), feels irritated whenever one joins his office (prejudice), and never shortlists their applications (discrimination). Option a is right because the stem asks for an emotional response, and emotion is the affective component, which is prejudice. Exam trap: setters swap the three terms. Remember: stereotype is what you think, prejudice is what you feel, discrimination is what you do.", "traps": {"b": "A stereotype is the cognitive component: a generalised belief about a group's traits (Walter Lippmann's 'pictures in our heads'); it is a thought, not an emotional response.", "c": "Discrimination is the behavioural component: unfair action against group members; the stem describes a feeling, not an act.", "d": "'Differential attitude' is not a standard social psychology term for intergroup bias; it is a filler distractor built on the word attitude."}}, {"q": "______ technique involves presenting target people with small requests and then following up with larger requests, the one that is desired all along.", "concept": "The answer is b: making a small request first and then following it with the larger request wanted all along is the foot-in-the-door technique. (The printed paper garbles this option as 'Put in the door'; it means foot in the door.) Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966) showed it with Californian homeowners. Asked cold to put a large, ugly 'Drive Carefully' sign on their lawns, about 17% agreed. But about 76% agreed if, two weeks earlier, they had accepted a small 'Be a safe driver' sign. Two explanations are offered: consistency (having said yes once, refusing now feels inconsistent) and self-perception (watching yourself agree, you infer that you are the kind of person who supports such causes). The first request must be small enough that nearly everyone accepts it. Its relatives work differently. Door-in-the-face (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) reverses the order. Low-ball (Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins agreement to a good deal and then worsens the terms. That's-not-all (Jerry Burger, 1986) sweetens the offer before the person has answered. Example: a volunteer asks Riya to sign a petition for a cleaner lake; a week later he asks her to give up a Sunday to clear rubbish from it, and she agrees. Option b is right because small-then-large is the defining sequence of the foot-in-the-door. Exam trap: door-in-the-face is the mirror image (large, refused, then small), and examiners swap the two; fix the order of the requests before you answer.", "traps": {"a": "Low-ball (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) wins a commitment to an attractive deal and then raises its cost; there is no small-then-large pair of requests.", "c": "Door-in-the-face (Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) uses the reverse order: a huge request that is refused, then the smaller real one, working through reciprocal concessions.", "d": "That's-not-all (Jerry Burger, 1986) sweetens a single offer with extras or a discount before the person replies; it has one request, not an escalation."}}, {"q": "The Father of Experimental Psychology is:", "concept": "The answer is c: Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt (1832 to 1920) is called the father of experimental psychology. Wundt, a physician who had worked as assistant to Hermann von Helmholtz (1821 to 1894), announced psychology as a new, independent science in Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874), where 'physiological' meant using the experimental methods of physiology. In 1879 his students began original research in his laboratory at the University of Leipzig, the date textbooks give for psychology's birth as a science. He added a journal, Philosophische Studien (1881), and trained a generation of students who carried the laboratory method to Europe and America. His method was controlled introspection: trained observers gave short reports on carefully timed and repeated stimuli. He studied reaction times, sensations, feelings and attention, and called his system voluntarism because it stressed will and attention. Example: in Wundt's laboratory a student such as Arjun would press a key when a light flashed, and the difference between simple and choice reaction times would be measured in milliseconds. Option c is right because Wundt gave experimental psychology its first formal research laboratory, journal and students. Exam trap: Gustav Theodor Fechner (1801 to 1887) and William James (1842 to 1910) are each 'first' at something nearby, Fechner for psychophysics and James for a small Harvard demonstration laboratory (1875) and American psychology. Key note: some historians (for example Edwin Boring, 1886 to 1968) credit Gustav Fechner's Elements of Psychophysics (1860) with founding experimental psychology, but Wundt is the standard answer and stands.", "traps": {"a": "William James (1842 to 1910) is the father of American psychology and functionalism (Principles of Psychology, 1890); his 1875 Harvard laboratory was for teaching demonstrations, not formal research.", "b": "Gustav Theodor Fechner founded psychophysics with Elements of Psychophysics (1860) and Fechner's law (S = k log I); he is a precursor, sometimes argued for, but not the standard father of experimental psychology.", "d": "Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) founded psychoanalysis, a clinical method built on case studies and free association, not laboratory experiment."}}, {"q": "Hermann Ebbinghaus, had done the pioneering experiments on:", "concept": "The answer is d: Hermann Ebbinghaus (1850 to 1909) did the pioneering experiments on memory, reported in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory, 1885). Ebbinghaus showed that a higher mental process could be measured experimentally, something Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) had doubted. Acting as his own sole participant, he invented the nonsense syllable (consonant-vowel-consonant strings such as DAX or BUP) to avoid the meanings and associations of real words. He learned lists until he could recite them twice without error and then relearned them after delays. Relearning was quicker even when nothing could be recalled, and he measured this with the savings method: original trials minus relearning trials, divided by original trials, times 100. His forgetting curve drops steeply, losing more than half within an hour and about two thirds within a day, then levels off without reaching zero. He also found the spacing effect (spread-out practice beats massed practice) and noticed that items at the start and end of a list are learned more easily. Example: Meera learns a list of nonsense syllables in 20 readings; a week later she recalls none, but relearns the list in 12 readings, a 40% saving. Option d is right because nonsense syllables, savings and the forgetting curve are all memory research, and the serial position finding is one result within it. Exam trap: the serial position effect is a real Ebbinghaus observation, so it baits the half-informed; but it is one part of his memory work, and memory is the full answer.", "traps": {"a": "Perception was the territory of Gestalt psychology (Max Wertheimer, 1912) and others; the Ebbinghaus illusion of size contrast carries his name, but his pioneering experimental programme was on memory.", "b": "Psychophysics was founded by Gustav Theodor Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860); Ebbinghaus borrowed Fechner's quantitative spirit for memory but did not pioneer psychophysics.", "c": "The serial position effect (better recall of first and last items) was noticed by Ebbinghaus but studied systematically later (for example by Bennet Murdock, 1962); it is one finding inside his memory work, not the field he pioneered."}}, {"q": "Among the following pioneering psychologists who is not associated with Behaviourism?", "concept": "The answer is a: Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021) is not associated with behaviourism; he founded cognitive therapy. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913), held that psychology should study only observable behaviour and its environmental causes, leaving out mind and consciousness. The neobehaviourists who followed refined it. Edwin Ray Guthrie (1886 to 1959) explained learning by contiguity alone: a stimulus that accompanied a movement will tend to be followed by that movement again, at full strength after one trial. Clark Leonard Hull (1884 to 1952) built a hypothetico-deductive system of drive reduction in which reaction potential equals habit strength times drive (Principles of Behavior, 1943). Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist, studied operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement. Beck, a psychiatrist first trained in psychoanalysis, moved in a different direction. In the 1960s he argued that depression grows from negative automatic thoughts and schemas, summed up in the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), and he built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). His therapy targets thoughts, which strict behaviourists refused to treat as causes. Example: Riya fails a test and thinks 'I am useless and always will be'. A Beck therapist tests that thought against the evidence, while a Skinnerian would look at how studying is reinforced. Option a is right because Beck's theory is cognitive, from the school that rose against behaviourism. Exam trap: cognitive behaviour therapy uses behavioural techniques, so Beck's name sits near behaviour therapy in textbooks, but he is a cognitive pioneer, not a behaviourist.", "traps": {"b": "Edwin Ray Guthrie was a neobehaviourist known for contiguity theory and one-trial learning (The Psychology of Learning, 1935), so he belongs to behaviourism.", "c": "Burrhus Frederic Skinner was the leading radical behaviourist: operant conditioning, the Skinner box and schedules of reinforcement.", "d": "Clark Leonard Hull was a neobehaviourist whose drive-reduction theory (Principles of Behavior, 1943) defined reaction potential as habit strength times drive."}}, {"q": "Who is the most valuable contributor in the theory of insight learning?", "concept": "The answer is d: Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) is the main contributor to the theory of insight learning. Kohler, one of the three founders of Gestalt psychology, was stranded at the anthropoid research station on Tenerife from 1913 to 1920 and studied chimpanzees there (The Mentality of Apes, 1917 in German, 1925 in English). His best-known chimpanzee, Sultan, could not reach a banana outside the cage with either of two short sticks. After a pause he fitted one stick into the other and at once raked in the fruit. Other apes stacked boxes to reach fruit hung from the roof. Kohler called this insight: a sudden reorganisation of the perceptual field in which the animal sees how the parts of the problem relate to the goal. Insight learning is abrupt rather than gradual, smooth once found, well remembered and readily transferred to new problems, and the moment of understanding is the 'aha' experience. Example: Arjun stares at a puzzle for ten minutes, then suddenly sees that turning one piece sideways solves it, and next time he repeats the solution at once. Option d is right because Kohler's ape studies are the founding evidence and theory of insight learning. Exam trap: Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941) was Kohler's Gestalt colleague, so the shared school makes c tempting, and Edward Thorndike (1898) is offered because his cats in puzzle boxes are the classic contrast: their learning was gradual trial and error, the opposite of insight.", "traps": {"a": "Edward Thorndike (1898) studied cats in puzzle boxes and proposed trial-and-error learning and the law of effect, the gradual learning that Kohler set insight against.", "b": "Edward Chace Tolman (1886 to 1959) is the purposive behaviourist of latent learning and cognitive maps in rats (1930, 1948), a cognitive learning theory but not insight learning.", "c": "Kurt Koffka was a Gestalt founder who wrote Growth of the Mind (1921) and Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935) and spread the idea of insight, but the ape experiments and the theory are Kohler's."}}, {"q": "Transference Focused Therapy is a structured psychodynamic treatment designed by:", "concept": "The answer is b: transference-focused psychotherapy (TFP) was designed by Otto F. Kernberg (born 1928) for borderline personality disorder. Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, described borderline personality organisation: identity diffusion, primitive defences (especially splitting and projective identification) and largely intact reality testing. Because good and bad images of self and others never merged in early development, the person sees others as all good or all bad and swings between idealising and devaluing them. TFP, set out in a manual by John Clarkin, Frank Yeomans and Kernberg (1999), uses this pattern as its material. It begins with a treatment contract that sets limits on self-harm and acting out. Sessions are usually twice a week, and the therapist watches how the split images of self and others appear in the relationship with the therapist (the transference), then clarifies, confronts and interprets them so the patient can join them into a whole picture. A randomised trial by Clarkin and colleagues (2007) found improvement in suicidality, anger and impulsivity. Example: Meera praises her therapist as perfect one week and calls him cruel the next when he moves a session; in TFP the therapist explores that switch as it happens in the room. Option b is right because TFP is Kernberg's object-relations treatment built on his model of borderline organisation. Exam trap: Marsha Linehan (1993) also designed a structured treatment for borderline personality disorder, dialectical behaviour therapy, so a is the near miss; but DBT is cognitive-behavioural, not psychodynamic.", "traps": {"a": "Marsha Linehan created dialectical behaviour therapy (manual 1993) for borderline personality disorder, a cognitive-behavioural treatment balancing acceptance and change skills, not a psychodynamic, transference-based one.", "c": "Heinz Kohut (1913 to 1981) founded self psychology (The Analysis of the Self, 1971), treating narcissistic disorders through mirroring and idealising selfobject transferences; TFP is not his.", "d": "Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970) founded Gestalt therapy, a here-and-now experiential approach (empty chair, awareness), not a structured psychodynamic treatment."}}, {"q": "The concept of Transitional Object was introduced by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the transitional object was introduced by Donald W. Winnicott (1896 to 1971), the British paediatrician and psychoanalyst, in his paper 'Transitional Objects and Transitional Phenomena' (read 1951, published 1953). A transitional object is the infant's first 'not-me' possession: a blanket, soft toy or corner of cloth that the baby clings to, especially at bedtime or when the mother is away. Winnicott said it is neither wholly inside (part of the baby) nor wholly outside (part of the world); it lives in an intermediate area of experience between fantasy and reality. It helps the child move from fusion with the mother to awareness that she is separate, soothing anxiety while the child learns to bear her absence. Parents sense its value and do not wash or replace it, because changing it breaks the continuity it offers; in time it simply loses importance. Winnicott linked this intermediate space to adult play, art, culture and religion. The idea sits inside his wider object-relations theory: the good-enough mother, the holding environment, and the true self versus the false self. Example: three-year-old Kabir cannot sleep without his worn blue blanket when his mother travels; it stands in for her comfort until he can hold her in mind. Option a is right because both the term and the theory are Winnicott's. Exam trap: John Bowlby (1907 to 1990) also wrote about separation and comfort, so attachment theory tempts, but Bowlby's concepts are the attachment bond, the secure base and internal working models.", "traps": {"b": "John Bowlby founded attachment theory: the attachment bond, the secure base, internal working models and protest, despair and detachment after separation; he did not coin the transitional object.", "c": "Harry Stack Sullivan (1892 to 1949) built interpersonal theory: the self-system, personifications, dynamisms and good-me, bad-me and not-me; the transitional object is not his.", "d": "Ronald Fairbairn (1889 to 1964) was an object-relations theorist who held that libido is object-seeking, not pleasure-seeking, and wrote on schizoid states; the transitional object is Winnicott's."}}, {"q": "\"Dereflection\" is a component of:", "concept": "The answer is c: dereflection is a technique of logotherapy, the meaning-centred therapy of Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997). Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi concentration camps, described his approach in Man's Search for Meaning (1946 in German). Logotherapy (from logos, meaning) holds that the primary human motive is the will to meaning, and that a frustrated search for meaning produces an existential vacuum and noogenic neurosis. Frankl gave it two signature techniques. Dereflection counters hyper-reflection, excessive attention to oneself or one's symptom, which often makes the symptom worse, as in insomnia or sexual dysfunction. The client is helped to turn attention away from the self and towards a meaningful task or other people, so the problem fades for lack of feeding. Paradoxical intention, the second technique, asks the client to wish for or do the very thing he fears, which breaks anticipatory anxiety. Socratic dialogue then helps the client find meaning in work, love or suffering. Example: Arjun lies awake checking whether sleep is coming. His therapist uses dereflection, asking him to spend that time planning the music class he teaches, and sleep arrives once he stops watching for it. Option c is right because dereflection is Frankl's own named logotherapy technique. Exam trap: logotherapy is one of the existential therapies, so a is the near miss; but when the specific school is offered, a named technique goes to its own school, not to the broad family.", "traps": {"a": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, 1909 to 1994; Irvin Yalom, 1980) is the broad family that works with death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; logotherapy belongs to it, but dereflection is Frankl's own technique, so the specific school is the better answer.", "b": "Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) relies on the core conditions of empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence, with no directive techniques such as dereflection.", "d": "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls) heightens present awareness through the empty chair, dialogue and exaggeration; it turns attention towards experience, not away from it."}}, {"q": "The concept of \"birth trauma\" was first mentioned by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the concept of birth trauma belongs to Otto Rank (1884 to 1939), who set it out in The Trauma of Birth (1924). Rank was one of the closest early followers of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) and for years secretary of the Vienna Psychoanalytic Society. He argued that birth is the first and model anxiety of life: the baby is torn from the blissful unity of the womb and separated from the mother. All later anxiety, he said, repeats this primal separation, and much of life is a struggle between the wish to return to that union and the fear of losing oneself in it. Neurosis was an unresolved birth trauma, and therapy should help the patient relive and master the separation. Rank later developed will therapy, a short, time-limited approach that stressed the patient's creative will; it became the basis of the functional school of social case work. Example: on Rank's view, Riya's panic every time she leaves home for a new hostel would echo the first separation of all, at birth. Option d is right because the named concept, and the book that made it famous, are Rank's. Exam trap: Freud had already called birth the prototype of anxiety, so b looks safe, but the stem names 'birth trauma', which is Rank's theory. Key note: Sigmund Freud had already called birth the first experience and prototype of anxiety (a 1909 footnote to The Interpretation of Dreams) and later rejected Rank's theory (Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety, 1926), so b is arguable for 'first mentioned'; the concept 'birth trauma' is Rank's, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Otto Fenichel (1897 to 1946) wrote the encyclopedic The Psychoanalytic Theory of Neurosis (1945), a synthesis of Freudian theory; birth trauma is not his concept.", "b": "Sigmund Freud called birth the prototype of anxiety, but as a model, not the cause of all neurosis, and he rejected Rank's birth trauma theory in Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety (1926).", "c": "Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961) founded analytical psychology: the collective unconscious, archetypes, introversion and extraversion; he did not propose birth trauma."}}, {"q": "The best known work of Erich Fromm is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the best-known work of Erich Fromm (1900 to 1980) is Escape from Freedom (1941), published in Britain as The Fear of Freedom. Fromm, a German psychoanalyst also trained in sociology, called his approach humanistic psychoanalysis and studied how whole societies shape character. Writing as fascism spread across Europe, he argued that modern people have won freedom from the old ties of church, clan and guild, but at the price of isolation and anxiety. Many flee this burden through three mechanisms of escape: authoritarianism (submitting to a strong leader or dominating others), destructiveness (attacking the world that threatens them) and automaton conformity (becoming exactly what society expects). The healthy answer is positive freedom, reached through spontaneous love and productive work. His later books include Man for Himself (1947), The Sane Society (1955), which set out the existential needs of relatedness, transcendence, rootedness, identity and a frame of orientation, and The Art of Loving (1956). Example: Kabir, newly living alone in a big city and anxious about his choices, joins a group whose leader tells him exactly what to think and how to live; Fromm would call this escape through authoritarianism. Option b is right because Escape from Freedom is Fromm's landmark book. Exam trap: all four titles are real books about Freud, psychoanalysis or madness, so you must match title to author; none of the other three is by Fromm.", "traps": {"a": "Freud and Beyond (1995) is a history of post-Freudian psychoanalysis by Stephen Mitchell and Margaret Black, not a book by Fromm.", "c": "Freud Along the Ganges (2005) is a collection on psychoanalysis and Indian culture edited by Salman Akhtar, not Fromm's.", "d": "Madness and Civilization (1961) is Michel Foucault's history of how Western society confined and defined the insane, not Fromm's."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a part of Gerstmann syndrome?", "concept": "The answer is a: prosopagnosia is not part of Gerstmann syndrome. Josef Gerstmann (1924) first reported finger agnosia and over the next few years linked three more signs to it, giving a tetrad that follows damage to the dominant (usually left) parietal lobe, especially the angular gyrus. The four signs are finger agnosia (inability to recognise, name or point to one's own or the examiner's fingers), agraphia (acquired loss of writing), acalculia or dyscalculia (acquired loss of calculation) and left-right disorientation. Gerstmann suggested a common disorder of the body schema behind them. The angular gyrus sits where the parietal, temporal and occipital lobes meet and supports reading, writing, number and spatial processing, so damage there disturbs all four. The pure tetrad is rare and often comes with mild aphasia. Prosopagnosia, face blindness, comes instead from damage to the fusiform face area on the underside of the temporal and occipital lobes, usually on the right or on both sides. Example: after a left parietal stroke, Meera cannot write her name, cannot work out change at the shop, muddles 'show me your left hand' and cannot say which finger the doctor is touching. Yet she recognises her son's face instantly. Option a is right because face recognition is a ventral temporo-occipital function and is not one of Gerstmann's four signs. Exam trap: dyscalculia and agraphia sound like separate learning disorders, but here they are acquired signs; remember the tetrad as fingers, writing, sums and left-right.", "traps": {"b": "Left-right disorientation, confusing the left and right sides of one's own or another person's body, is one of Gerstmann's four signs.", "c": "Agraphia, an acquired loss of writing after dominant angular gyrus damage, is one of Gerstmann's four signs.", "d": "Dyscalculia (acalculia), an acquired loss of calculation, is one of Gerstmann's four signs."}}, {"q": "Value Scales are associated with the personality psychologist:", "concept": "The answer is a: value scales are associated with Gordon Allport (1897 to 1967), through the Allport-Vernon-Lindzey Study of Values. Gordon Allport and Philip Vernon (1931) built the Study of Values; Gardner Lindzey joined for the revisions of 1951 and 1960. It measures the relative strength of six basic values taken from Types of Men by Eduard Spranger (1914 in German; 1928 in English): theoretical (the discovery of truth), economic (the useful and practical), aesthetic (form and harmony), social (love of people), political (power) and religious (unity with the whole of experience). Items are forced choices, so the scores are ipsative: a high score on one value must be balanced by lower scores on others. The profile therefore shows what matters most to the person compared with her own other values, not compared with other people. It was widely used in counselling, vocational guidance and research. Allport, the founder of trait psychology in America, held that a mature personality has a unifying philosophy of life, and values reveal it. Example: on the Study of Values, Riya scores highest on theoretical and lowest on economic, which fits her choice of research over a better-paid corporate job. Option a is right because the classic value scale carries Allport's name. Exam trap: the 16PF of Raymond Cattell (1949) and the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire of Hans Eysenck (1975) are trait inventories, not value scales, and Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994) built no test.", "traps": {"b": "Raymond Cattell is linked with the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire and source traits found by factor analysis, not with value scales.", "c": "Hans Eysenck is linked with extraversion, neuroticism and psychoticism and the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire, not with value scales.", "d": "Erik Erikson proposed the eight psychosocial stages and identity theory; he built no value scale."}}, {"q": "What is the resolution point of Erik Erikson's fourth stage of psychosocial development?", "concept": "The answer is b: the fourth stage of Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994), industry versus inferiority, resolves in the virtue of competence. In Childhood and Society (1950) Erikson gave each of his eight psychosocial stages a crisis between two poles, and in Insight and Responsibility (1964) he named the virtue, or basic strength, that a good resolution leaves. Stage 4 covers the school years, about 6 to 11 or puberty, matching the latency period of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939). School brings tools, tasks and classmates to compare oneself with. A child who finishes tasks, learns skills and earns recognition develops industry, a sense of being able to make things work; repeated failure, harsh comparison or being made to feel behind brings inferiority. Competence is the free use of skill and intelligence in completing tasks, unimpaired by a sense of inferiority. Teachers and peers become key relationships as the child's world widens beyond the family. The virtues to memorise, in order: hope, will, purpose, competence, fidelity, love, care, wisdom. Example: nine-year-old Kabir struggles with long division, but his teacher breaks it into steps and praises each success; by the end of term he volunteers to solve problems at the board, the sense of competence Erikson described. Option b is right because competence is the virtue of the fourth stage. Exam trap: the other three options are also Erikson virtues, from stage 2 (will), stage 3 (purpose) and stage 5 (fidelity), so count the stages carefully.", "traps": {"a": "Fidelity is the virtue of Erikson's fifth stage, identity versus role confusion (adolescence): loyalty to freely chosen values and people.", "c": "Will is the virtue of Erikson's second stage, autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 1 to 3 years).", "d": "Purpose is the virtue of Erikson's third stage, initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6 years)."}}, {"q": "\"the range of psychological functions between, on the one hand, those functions which the child is able to master without assistance, and on the other hand, those functions which the child can manage if given assistance\", is the concept of _____________ in cognitive development.", "concept": "The answer is a: the range between what a child can master alone and what the child can manage with help is the zone of proximal development (ZPD) of Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934). Vygotsky, the Soviet psychologist, developed the idea in the early 1930s; it reached most English readers in the collection Mind in Society (1978). He described two children who both solve tasks set for eight-year-olds when working alone. Given hints, one solves tasks set for twelve-year-olds, the other only those for nine-year-olds. Their actual developmental level is the same, but their zones differ, so a single test score misses what each is ready to learn. The ZPD holds functions that are still ripening, 'buds' rather than 'fruits' of development. Good teaching aims inside it: what a child does with an adult or a more capable peer today, she can do alone tomorrow. Support fitted to the learner and withdrawn as skill grows was later named scaffolding by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976). Example: Meera cannot finish a 20-piece jigsaw alone, but succeeds when her father suggests starting with the edge pieces; that jigsaw lies in her ZPD. Option a is right because the stem quotes the classic definition of the ZPD. Exam trap: central neoformation and central line of development are genuine Vygotsky terms from his theory of age periods, so the author fits all three; but they name the main new structure of each age and the processes tied to it, not the gap between solo and assisted performance.", "traps": {"b": "Central neoformation is Vygotsky's term, from his theory of age periods, for the main new psychological formation that emerges in a given age; it is not the gap between unassisted and assisted performance.", "c": "Conservation is Jean Piaget's term for understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in shape or arrangement, gained in the concrete operational stage.", "d": "Central line of development is also Vygotsky's term: the developmental processes most directly tied to an age's central neoformation, as against peripheral lines; it does not describe the solo versus assisted gap."}}, {"q": "A salesperson knocks on your door and requests you to buy the latest technology refrigerator of his \"internationally famed\" organization. When you turn him down, he requests you to buy a portable ice box. You find yourself obliged to comply. He has used, to near success:", "concept": "The answer is b: asking first for an expensive refrigerator, which you refuse, and then for a cheap ice box, which you feel obliged to buy, is the door-in-the-face technique. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1975) demonstrated it with students. Asked to counsel juvenile delinquents two hours a week for two years, every student refused. Asked next to take the delinquents on a single two-hour trip to the zoo, about 50% agreed, against under 17% of students asked only the small favour. The large request is meant to be refused; it makes the second, real request look like a concession. Because of the reciprocity norm (we repay favours and concessions), the target feels pressure to concede in turn, and by contrast the second request also seems smaller than it is. Guilt about the first refusal may add to the pull. The technique works best when the same person makes both requests, one soon after the other, and the first is not absurdly large. In the stem, the salesman's real goal was always the ice box. By retreating from the refrigerator he seemed to give something up, and you felt you owed him a yes. Option b is right because the order is a large refused request followed by the smaller target request. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door uses the reverse order (small then large), so check which request came first. Key note: the door-in-the-face works through the reciprocity norm (a concession is returned with a concession), so d names the mechanism, not the technique; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Foot-in-the-door (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966) puts the small request first and the large one second; here the large request came first and was refused.", "c": "Low-ball (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) gets agreement to an attractive deal and then worsens its terms; the salesman never changed the terms of a deal you had accepted.", "d": "Reciprocity is Cialdini's principle (we repay favours and concessions) that explains why the door-in-the-face works; it names the mechanism, not the technique the salesman used."}}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to monitor and correct learning, is metacognition, literally thinking about thinking. John Flavell (1979) made the term central to developmental psychology. Metacognition has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what you know about your own mind, about tasks and about strategies: for instance, that a long list needs more study than a short one. Metacognitive regulation is the active control of thinking: planning how to tackle a task, monitoring whether you understand as you go, and evaluating and correcting when you do not. Its memory branch is metamemory (Flavell, 1971), which includes the feeling of knowing and judgements of how well something has been learned. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts school success, so study-skills training, self-questioning and reciprocal teaching all aim to strengthen it. Example: while reading a chapter on neurons, Meera notices she cannot explain the last paragraph in her own words, so she rereads it and draws a diagram. That check-and-fix loop is metacognition. Option c is right because the stem describes awareness of one's own thinking that makes learning possible through self-correction. Exam trap: algorithms and analogies are problem-solving tools, things you think with, while metacognition is thinking about how you are thinking; 'constructive process' sounds learning-related but describes how memory and knowledge are built, not awareness of that building.", "traps": {"a": "An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution if followed exactly (unlike a heuristic); it is a problem-solving method, not awareness of one's own thinking.", "b": "Analogies solve a new problem by mapping it onto a familiar one with the same structure; this is a problem-solving strategy, not self-monitoring of thought.", "d": "Constructive process refers to how memory and knowledge are actively built and rebuilt (Frederic Bartlett, 1932, and constructivist learning theory); it is not awareness of one's own thought."}}, {"q": "How many subjects would be required in a 3 × 3 × 2 factorial design with ten subjects per cell?", "concept": "The answer is d: a 3 × 3 × 2 factorial design has 18 cells, and ten subjects per cell gives 180 subjects. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at once, combining every level of each factor with every level of the others. The notation tells you everything. The number of numerals is the number of factors, and each numeral is that factor's number of levels, so 3 × 3 × 2 means three factors: two with three levels and one with two. Each combination of levels is a cell (a treatment condition), and the number of cells is the product: 3 × 3 × 2 = 18. In a between-subjects design each participant serves in only one cell, so the total sample is the number of cells times the number of participants per cell: 18 × 10 = 180. A three-factor design yields three main effects, three two-way interactions and one three-way interaction, tested with factorial analysis of variance. Example: Kabir tests therapy type (CBT, interpersonal therapy, waiting list), dose (low, medium, high) and sex (male, female) on depression scores. With ten clients in each of the 18 combinations, he needs 180 clients. Option d is right because subjects equal cells multiplied by subjects per cell. Exam trap: each distractor stops a step early. 10 is one cell's sample, 18 is the number of cells, and 90 multiplies only two factors (3 × 3 = 9, times 10). Also do not add the numerals (3 + 3 + 2 = 8) or count the factors (3) instead of multiplying the levels.", "traps": {"a": "10 is the number of subjects in a single cell; it ignores the other 17 cells altogether.", "b": "18 is the number of cells (3 × 3 × 2), not the number of subjects; it forgets to multiply by ten per cell.", "c": "90 multiplies only two of the factors (3 × 3 = 9 cells, times 10), dropping the third factor with its two levels."}}, {"q": "Which of the following clinical procedure/s is/are not based on classical conditioning? 1. Flooding, 2. Transference, 3. Client centered therapy, 4. Systematic desensitization", "concept": "The answer is b: transference (2) and client-centred therapy (3) are not based on classical conditioning, while flooding (1) and systematic desensitisation (4) are. Classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), explains how a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned one comes to trigger a conditioned response such as fear. If fears are learned this way, the same laws can unlearn them. Systematic desensitisation, from Joseph Wolpe (Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, 1958), uses counterconditioning: the relaxed client works up an anxiety hierarchy so that relaxation, a response incompatible with anxiety, replaces fear at each step (reciprocal inhibition). Flooding, and its imaginal form implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967), uses extinction: the client stays with the feared stimulus at full intensity, with escape blocked, until the conditioned fear dies away because no harm follows. Transference, by contrast, is a psychoanalytic concept from Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939): the patient redirects feelings about early figures onto the analyst, and interpreting this brings insight. Client-centred therapy, from Carl Rogers (1951), relies on empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence to free the client's actualising tendency. Example: Riya's fear of dogs could be treated by relaxed, graded steps towards a dog (desensitisation) or by staying beside a calm dog until her panic subsides (flooding); both are conditioning methods. Option b is right because only items 2 and 3 lack a conditioning basis. Exam trap: in 'not based on' items students pick the conditioning therapies by reflex; read the negative and choose the non-behavioural pair.", "traps": {"a": "'1 only' names flooding, which is itself a classical conditioning therapy working by extinction of conditioned fear, so it cannot be the procedure that is not conditioning-based.", "c": "'1, 2 and 3' correctly includes transference and client-centred therapy but wrongly adds flooding, which rests on extinction of a classically conditioned response.", "d": "'1, 3 and 4' includes flooding and systematic desensitisation, the two conditioning therapies in the list, and leaves out transference, which is psychoanalytic."}}, {"q": "The aim of the ______ was to regulate training policies and programs in the field of disability rehabilitation and standardize training courses for professionals working with people with disabilities.", "concept": "The answer is b: the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) Act 1992 was passed to regulate and standardise the training of professionals who work with people with disabilities. The Rehabilitation Council of India began in 1986 as a registered society, and the RCI Act 1992 made it a statutory body (in force from 22 June 1993); the Act was amended in 2000. Its jobs are to regulate and monitor training policies and programmes in disability rehabilitation, to set minimum standards and standardise syllabi, to recognise institutions and qualifications, and to keep the Central Rehabilitation Register of qualified professionals. Practising without registration is an offence under the Act. The RCI sits under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, not the Ministry of Health. Clinical psychologists, rehabilitation psychologists, special educators, audiologists and speech therapists all come under it, which is why an Indian clinical psychologist must hold an RCI-recognised qualification and be on the RCI register. For example, when Meera finishes her clinical psychology training and wants to practise, she applies for RCI registration; the Mental Healthcare Act then counts her as a mental health professional because of that qualification. Option b is right because the stem lists exactly the RCI Act's purposes: regulating training policies and programmes and standardising courses for rehabilitation professionals. Exam trap: the other three Acts are rights or care laws, not training laws. Examiners rely on the shared word 'disability' in the PwD and RPwD Acts to pull candidates away from the RCI Act, which is about the professionals, not the people they serve.", "traps": {"a": "The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016 is a rights law: it lists 21 disabilities, sets the 40 percent benchmark disability and guarantees reservations and access. It does not regulate professional training.", "c": "The Persons with Disabilities Act 1995 was India's first comprehensive disability rights law (7 disabilities), covering education, employment and non-discrimination, not the training of rehabilitation professionals.", "d": "The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 is a rights-based mental health care law (right to access care, advance directives, decriminalising attempted suicide). It defines a clinical psychologist by an RCI-recognised qualification but does not regulate disability training."}}, {"q": "The first Disability Act came into effect in India in the year:", "concept": "The answer is c: India's first disability Act, the Persons with Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act, dates from 1995. Before it, India had no comprehensive law on the rights of disabled people; services were left to welfare schemes and charities. The 1995 Act recognised 7 disabilities: blindness, low vision, leprosy-cured, hearing impairment, locomotor disability, mental retardation and mental illness. It promised free education up to 18 years, a 3 percent reservation in identified government posts, barrier-free access and protection from discrimination, and it set up Chief Commissioners and State Commissioners to enforce these rights. It was replaced by the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016 (in force 2017), which widened the list to 21 disabilities, including autism spectrum disorder and specific learning disabilities, and raised the job reservation to 4 percent. A useful timeline: RCI Act 1992 (professionals), PwD Act 1995 (rights), National Trust Act 1999 (autism, cerebral palsy, intellectual and multiple disabilities), RPwD Act 2016 (rights, widened). Suppose Kabir, who has low vision, applied for a reserved government post in 2005; his claim would have rested on the 1995 Act. Option c is right because 1995 is the year of India's first comprehensive disability law. Exam trap: 2016 is the famous recent Act, so candidates who know only the RPwD Act pick d; it is the replacement, not the first. Key note: the 1995 Act was passed in December 1995 and came into force on 7 February 1996; the RCI Act 1992 came earlier but regulates professionals, not disability rights, and is not an option; 1995 stands.", "traps": {"a": "No Indian disability Act dates from 1968; that is the year the Indian Association of Clinical Psychologists was founded, a date from the same chapter of Indian history that candidates mix up.", "b": "1990 is the year of the Americans with Disabilities Act in the United States, not an Indian law; India had no disability Act until 1995.", "d": "2016 is the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, which replaced the 1995 Act and expanded it to 21 disabilities, so it is the second comprehensive Act, not the first."}}, {"q": "\"Constructive alternativism\" is a core assumption of whose personality theory?", "concept": "The answer is b: constructive alternativism is the core philosophical assumption of George Kelly's psychology of personal constructs. George Kelly (1955), in The Psychology of Personal Constructs, pictured every person as a scientist who builds theories about the world, tests them through prediction and revises them when they fail. The theories are personal constructs: bipolar dimensions such as friendly versus hostile, or clever versus dull, through which a person construes events. Constructive alternativism is the assumption that any event can always be construed in more than one way, so no one is a prisoner of their past or of a single interpretation; people can always re-construe their lives. Kelly built the rest of the theory on this base: a fundamental postulate ('a person's processes are psychologically channelised by the ways in which he anticipates events') and eleven corollaries. He measured constructs with the Role Construct Repertory Test (the Rep Test) and treated clients with fixed-role therapy, in which a client lives out a new character sketch for a few weeks to try a different way of construing. Take Arjun, who construes his failed exam as proof that he is stupid. Kelly would say this is only one construction; Arjun could re-construe it as evidence that he studied the wrong way, and that alternative opens new action. Option b is right because constructive alternativism is Kelly's own term and the foundation of his cognitive theory of personality. Exam trap: 'constructive' and 'alternative' sound like the growth language of humanistic psychology, so candidates drift to d; but Carl Rogers (1951) speaks of the actualising tendency and conditions of worth, not of constructs.", "traps": {"a": "B. F. Skinner (1953) held radical behaviourism: behaviour is shaped by its consequences, and inner interpretations are not causes, which is the opposite of Kelly's view.", "c": "Kurt Goldstein (1939, The Organism) gave a holistic, organismic theory and coined self-actualisation as the single master motive; constructs play no part in it.", "d": "Carl Rogers (1951, 1959) built self theory around the actualising tendency, the self-concept, conditions of worth and incongruence; his core assumption is growth, not alternative construals."}}, {"q": "'Moses and Monotheism' is authored by:", "concept": "The answer is b: Moses and Monotheism (1939) is Sigmund Freud's last book, published in the year he died. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) began it in Vienna and finished it in London after fleeing the Nazis in 1938. In it he argued that Moses was an Egyptian nobleman who passed on the one-god religion of the Pharaoh Akhenaten to the Hebrews, and that the Hebrews later killed him. The guilt over this killing was repressed and returned, like a neurotic symptom, as the religion's lasting sense of guilt. The book applies psychoanalysis to religion and history, and it belongs to a group of Freud's cultural works: Totem and Taboo (1913, the primal horde and the killing of the father), The Future of an Illusion (1927, religion as wish fulfilment) and Civilization and Its Discontents (1930, instinct against civilisation). A handy chain: Totem and Taboo starts the father-killing story, and Moses and Monotheism ends it. If Riya is asked which Freud book calls religion an illusion, the answer is The Future of an Illusion, not Moses and Monotheism, even though both deal with religion. Option b is right because Freud is the author, and the book's theme, repressed guilt returning in religious form, is classic Freudian reasoning. Exam trap: Carl Jung (1938) also wrote widely about religion, so a religion-themed title pulls candidates towards c, and Viktor Frankl's (1946) spiritual themes make d sound plausible; neither wrote this book.", "traps": {"a": "Heinz Hartmann (1939) wrote Ego Psychology and the Problem of Adaptation, the founding text of ego psychology (the conflict-free ego sphere). It appeared the same year as Moses and Monotheism, but it is not about religion.", "c": "Carl Jung wrote on religion in Psychology and Religion (1938) and Answer to Job (1952), seeing religion as a healthy expression of archetypes, not as returning guilt; Moses and Monotheism is not his.", "d": "Viktor Frankl wrote Man's Search for Meaning (1946) and founded logotherapy; his spiritual themes concern the will to meaning, not the history of Moses."}}, {"q": "The contention that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience, attests to which of the following hypotheses:", "concept": "The answer is d: the idea that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience is the facial feedback hypothesis. The hypothesis says that the face does not only show an emotion; signals from the facial muscles and skin feed back to the brain and shape, or even start, the feeling. Charles Darwin (1872) suggested that freely expressing an emotion intensifies it, William James (1884) held that feelings follow bodily changes, and Silvan Tomkins (1962) made the face the seat of affect, which is the modern root of the hypothesis. The classic test is the pen study of Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper (1988): people who held a pen in their teeth (forcing a smile without knowing it) rated cartoons funnier than people who held it in their lips (which blocks a smile). A large multi-lab replication in 2016 did not find the effect, and later meta-analyses suggest it is real but small. So if Kabir forces a broad smile before an interview, the hypothesis predicts that he will feel a little calmer and happier. Option d is right because the stem describes influence running from face to feeling, which is the defining claim of facial feedback. Exam trap: the universality hypothesis also concerns faces, so it is the near miss; but universality is about whether expressions are produced and recognised the same way across cultures, not about the face changing the feeling. Options b and c are about the autonomic nervous system, not the face.", "traps": {"a": "The universality hypothesis, from Charles Darwin (1872) and tested by Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1971) with the Fore of Papua New Guinea, holds that basic expressions are recognised across cultures; it concerns recognition, not the face changing the feeling.", "b": "The emotional specificity hypothesis, implied by the James-Lange theory, holds that each emotion has its own pattern of autonomic activity (heart rate, skin temperature); it is about internal arousal, not facial expression.", "c": "The emotional generality hypothesis, from Walter Cannon (1927) and assumed by Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962), holds that all emotions share the same general autonomic arousal; it too concerns the autonomic nervous system, not the face."}}, {"q": "Replacing the fragmented self with the cohesive self is the primary developmental and therapeutic goal of:", "concept": "The answer is b: replacing a fragmented self with a cohesive self is the central developmental and therapeutic goal of Heinz Kohut's self psychology. Heinz Kohut (1971), in The Analysis of the Self and later The Restoration of the Self (1977), argued that narcissism has its own healthy line of development, not only a pathological one. The child's self grows through selfobjects: people experienced as part of the self because they perform functions the child cannot yet perform alone. Three selfobject needs must be met: mirroring (the parent's delighted response that confirms the child's 'Look at me!'), idealising (merging with a calm, strong parent) and twinship (feeling essentially alike with others). When parents respond well but imperfectly, each bearable failure lets the child take over the soothing function, which Kohut called transmuting internalisation, and this builds a cohesive self. When mirroring and idealising fail chronically, the self stays fragmented and enfeebled, and narcissistic problems follow. In therapy the analyst offers empathic attunement; the client forms selfobject transferences (mirror, idealising, twinship), and the therapist's small, understood empathic failures repeat the healthy process, so the self becomes cohesive. Imagine Kabir, who feels he falls apart whenever his boss ignores him; a Kohutian therapist would read this as a fragile, fragmenting self and work through empathy to strengthen its cohesion. Option b is right because 'fragmented self' and 'cohesive self' are Kohut's signature terms. Exam trap: Otto Kernberg (1975) also wrote on narcissism and borderline states, so a is the near miss; but Kernberg's goal is integrating split good and bad images of self and others, not building self cohesion through selfobjects.", "traps": {"a": "Otto Kernberg (1975) described borderline personality organisation and pathological narcissism built on splitting; his transference-focused psychotherapy aims to integrate split good and bad images through confrontation and interpretation, not Kohut's empathic building of a cohesive self.", "c": "David Malan, at the Tavistock Clinic, developed brief dynamic psychotherapy around the triangle of conflict (defence, anxiety, hidden feeling) and the triangle of person (others, therapist, parents); his focus is one circumscribed conflict, not self cohesion.", "d": "Mardi Horowitz described stress response syndromes (alternating intrusion and avoidance after trauma) and co-developed the Impact of Event Scale (1979); his work concerns processing traumatic events, not the fragmented self."}}, {"q": "Which of the following names is not associated with Existential Therapy?", "concept": "The answer is b: Sandor Ferenczi was a psychoanalyst, not an existential therapist. Sandor Ferenczi (1873 to 1933) was a Hungarian member of Sigmund Freud's (1856 to 1939) inner circle who experimented with the technique of analysis: an active technique, a warmer relaxation technique, even mutual analysis, and a 1932 paper on the 'confusion of tongues' between adults and children that took childhood sexual trauma seriously. He is a forerunner of relational psychoanalysis, not of existentialism. Existential therapy starts from philosophy, above all Martin Heidegger's Being and Time (1927), and treats a person as being-in-the-world facing the givens that Irvin Yalom (1980) named as death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Its names include Karl Jaspers (1913), the German psychiatrist and philosopher whose General Psychopathology brought phenomenology into psychiatry; Ludwig Binswanger (1881 to 1966) and Medard Boss (1963), who built Daseinsanalysis; Rollo May (1958), who brought existential ideas to America; Viktor Frankl (1946), the founder of logotherapy; and R. D. Laing (1960) in Britain. Suppose Meera is anxious after a friend's death; an existential therapist would help her face mortality and choose how to live, not interpret her childhood drives. Option b is right because Ferenczi's work sits wholly inside psychoanalysis, while the other three are standard names in the existential tradition. Exam trap: Ferenczi stressed warmth and the real relationship, which sounds humanistic, and Laing is often filed only under anti-psychiatry; but Laing's The Divided Self was openly existential and phenomenological, so he belongs here, and Ferenczi does not.", "traps": {"a": "Rollo May (The Meaning of Anxiety, 1950; Existence, co-edited 1958) is the leading American existential psychologist, so he clearly belongs to existential therapy.", "c": "Karl Jaspers (General Psychopathology, 1913) was a psychiatrist and existentialist philosopher who brought phenomenology into psychiatry; he is a founder of the existential tradition (the paper spells his name 'Carl').", "d": "R. D. Laing (The Divided Self, 1960) gave an explicitly existential and phenomenological account of schizophrenia and ontological insecurity, so he belongs to British existential psychiatry as well as anti-psychiatry."}}, {"q": "A more preferred activity can be used to reinforce a less preferred activity. This is referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is b: using a more preferred activity to reinforce a less preferred one is the Premack principle. David Premack (1959) argued that reinforcement is relative: any behaviour that an individual does more often when free to choose (a high-probability behaviour) can reinforce one done less often (a low-probability behaviour), if access to the first depends on doing the second. So no activity is a reinforcer in itself; it depends on which is more probable for that individual at that time. In his rat work, for a thirsty rat drinking reinforced running, but for a rat deprived of exercise running reinforced drinking. Parents know the rule as 'Grandma's rule': first eat your vegetables, then you get dessert. Behaviour therapists use it in classrooms, wards and token systems, where a favourite activity is earned by completing a disliked task. For example, Riya's mother tells her she can watch her favourite show only after finishing her mathematics homework; the preferred show strengthens the less preferred homework. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly Premack's rule of high-probability behaviour reinforcing low-probability behaviour. Exam trap: the direction matters, since a less preferred activity cannot reinforce a more preferred one. DRO also uses reinforcement, so candidates who see the word 'reinforce' pick d, but DRO rewards the absence of a problem behaviour, not access to a preferred activity.", "traps": {"a": "Trace conditioning is a classical conditioning arrangement (Ivan Pavlov) in which the conditioned stimulus ends before the unconditioned stimulus begins, leaving a gap; it concerns the timing of stimuli, not one activity rewarding another.", "c": "Stimulus generalisation is responding to stimuli similar to the conditioned stimulus, as when Little Albert (John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner, 1920) came to fear other furry things; no activity is used as a reinforcer.", "d": "Differential reinforcement of other behaviour (DRO) reinforces intervals in which a problem behaviour does not occur, in order to reduce it; it does not use a preferred activity to build a less preferred one."}}, {"q": "\"Moving towards people, moving away from people, moving against people\" are constituents of whose classification of pattern of needs:", "concept": "The answer is c: moving towards, against and away from people are Karen Horney's three neurotic trends. Karen Horney (1937), in The Neurotic Personality of Our Time, argued that personality is shaped mainly by social relationships, not sexual instincts. A child whose parents show indifference (no genuine warmth, broken promises, favouritism, ridicule) feels basic hostility, represses it out of helplessness or fear, and is left with basic anxiety: a pervasive feeling of being lonely and helpless in a hostile world. To cope, the child adopts strategies that harden into ten neurotic needs, such as needs for affection, a dominant partner, power, prestige, self-sufficiency and perfection. In Our Inner Conflicts (1945) she grouped these needs into three trends. The compliant type moves towards people, seeking love and approval; the aggressive type moves against people, seeking power and admiration; the detached type moves away from people, seeking independence and distance. A healthy person uses all three flexibly; a neurotic person is locked into one, and the clash among them is the core of neurosis. For example, Arjun agrees with everyone and panics if a friend seems annoyed (towards), while his brother Kabir turns every conversation into a contest (against). Option c is right because the stem quotes Horney's own triad. Exam trap: Erich Fromm (1941) also listed social strategies (mechanisms of escape and character orientations), and both were neo-Freudians of the cultural school, so d is the near miss; but the towards, against and away wording is Horney's alone.", "traps": {"a": "John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950, Personality and Psychotherapy) translated Freud into learning theory (drive, cue, response, reward, approach-avoidance conflict); they did not classify needs as movements towards, against or away from people.", "b": "Melanie Klein's object relations theory describes the paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions, splitting and projective identification; it concerns the infant's inner objects, not adult neurotic trends.", "d": "Erich Fromm (1941) described mechanisms of escape (authoritarianism, destructiveness, automaton conformity) and character orientations (receptive, exploitative, hoarding, marketing, productive), close cousins of Horney's trends but with different names."}}, {"q": "In as much as the first three stages of Kohlberg's theory of moral development represent a shift from unquestioning obedience to a relativistic outlook and a concern towards good motives, they echo whose theory?", "concept": "The answer is b: the first three of Kohlberg's stages echo Jean Piaget's theory of moral development. Jean Piaget (1932), in The Moral Judgment of the Child, watched Swiss children play marbles and told them paired stories (John breaks fifteen cups by accident; Henry breaks one cup while sneaking jam). He described heteronomous morality, or moral realism, from about 5 to 10 years: rules come from adults and cannot change, acts are judged by their consequences, and punishment is expected to follow wrongdoing automatically (immanent justice). Then comes autonomous morality: rules are agreements that can be changed, and acts are judged by intentions. Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), working from Piaget, used dilemmas such as Heinz stealing a drug to save his wife and found three levels with six stages. Stage 1 (punishment and obedience) is unquestioning obedience to avoid punishment, close to heteronomy. Stage 2 (instrumental exchange) is a relativistic outlook: right is whatever serves each person's interest, as in 'you scratch my back and I'll scratch yours'. Stage 3 (good boy, good girl) judges by good motives and approval, close to Piaget's autonomous concern with intentions. So the march from obedience to relativism to good motives is a finer-grained version of Piaget's shift. For example, at 6 Meera says John is naughtier because more cups broke; at 10 she says Henry is naughtier because he meant to misbehave. Option b is right because Kohlberg openly built his stages on Piaget's two moralities. Exam trap: Lev Vygotsky (1934) is the other big developmental name and sits beside Piaget in every syllabus, but he wrote no stage theory of moral reasoning.", "traps": {"a": "Lev Vygotsky (1978, Mind in Society) gave a sociocultural theory of cognitive development (zone of proximal development, inner speech, cultural tools); he proposed no stages of moral judgement.", "c": "Harry Harlow (1958) showed that infant rhesus monkeys prefer a soft cloth mother to a wire mother that feeds them (contact comfort); this is attachment research, not moral reasoning.", "d": "John Bowlby (1969) built attachment theory (secure base, internal working models, maternal deprivation); it explains emotional bonds, not the move from obedience to good motives."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not considered a part of the reticular activating system?", "concept": "The answer is c: the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus is not part of the reticular activating system; it is a specific sensory relay for vision. The reticular formation is a net-like core of about 100 small nuclei running through the brainstem from the medulla to the midbrain. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system (RAS), keeps the cortex awake and alert. Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949) showed this by electrically stimulating the reticular formation of sleeping cats: the cats woke, and their EEG switched from slow synchronised waves to the fast desynchronised pattern of alertness. Damage to the system causes coma. The RAS reaches the cortex by two routes: a dorsal route through the intralaminar (non-specific) thalamic nuclei, which project diffusely to wide areas of cortex, and a ventral route through the hypothalamus and basal forebrain. Its components include the mesencephalic (midbrain) reticular nuclei, the locus coeruleus (noradrenaline), the raphe nuclei (serotonin) and cholinergic nuclei at the junction of pons and midbrain. The lateral geniculate nucleus is different in kind: a specific thalamic nucleus carrying a point-to-point map of the retina to the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe. If Arjun dozes in a lecture and a loud bang wakes him, his RAS raises arousal; his lateral geniculate nucleus only passes on what his eyes see. Option c is right because the lateral geniculate is a specific visual relay, not a diffuse arousal nucleus. Exam trap: two thalamic options appear, and candidates reason that the thalamus is only a relay; but the intralaminar nuclei are the non-specific nuclei that belong to the RAS, while the geniculate nuclei are specific sensory relays.", "traps": {"a": "The locus coeruleus, in the pons, is the brain's main source of noradrenaline and a core arousal nucleus of the RAS, active in vigilance and nearly silent in REM sleep.", "b": "The intralaminar nuclei are the non-specific thalamic nuclei that relay reticular activation diffusely to the cortex, so they are part of the RAS's thalamic route.", "d": "The mesencephalic (midbrain) reticular nuclei are the upper end of the reticular formation, the region Moruzzi and Magoun stimulated to wake sleeping cats, so they are central to the RAS."}}, {"q": "A Census would entail which sampling technique?", "concept": "The answer is d: a census involves no sampling technique at all, because every member of the population is counted. A population is the whole group a researcher wants to describe (all households in India, all students in a college). A census is complete enumeration: data are collected from every element of that population. A sample is a smaller subset chosen by some rule, and a sampling technique is that rule. Probability methods give each element a known chance of selection: simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling. Non-probability methods do not: convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling. Because a census studies everyone, it has no sampling error; its figures are population parameters, not estimates. It can still suffer non-sampling error, such as households missed by enumerators (coverage error), people who refuse (non-response) and badly worded questions (measurement error). Censuses are slow and costly, which is why India's national Census is held only once every ten years while sample surveys fill the gaps. If Kabir, a college counsellor, wants the stress level of all 300 students, he can give the questionnaire to all 300 (a census) or pick 60 by lottery (simple random sampling). Option d is right because counting every unit leaves nothing to sample. Exam trap: quota and stratified samples are often matched to census proportions, so candidates link the census to b or c. Key note: a census is complete enumeration, every member of the population is counted, so no sampling technique is involved; a census is a kind of survey, which is why a is tempting, but a survey is a method of data collection, not a sampling technique, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "A survey is a method of collecting data by questionnaire or interview; a census is a complete survey and a sample survey a partial one, but 'survey' names the data-collection method, not a way of selecting units.", "b": "Quota sampling is a non-probability method that fills set quotas (for example by age and sex, often matched to census proportions) with whoever is available; a census selects no one because it includes everyone.", "c": "Simple random sampling gives every member an equal chance of being chosen for a subset, as in a lottery; a census needs no selection because every member is included."}}, {"q": "As per the NIMH classification of Intelligence quotient, a person with an IQ of 48 would fall in the category of:", "concept": "The answer is b: an IQ of 48 falls in the moderate range of intellectual disability on the NIMH classification. The National Institute for the Mentally Handicapped (NIMH, Secunderabad), now the National Institute for the Empowerment of Persons with Intellectual Disabilities (NIEPID), uses the World Health Organization's ICD-10 bands, which Indian disability certification also followed. On a test with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, the bands are: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34 and profound below 20. Borderline intellectual functioning, 70 to 84 (some sources 71 to 84), is below average but is not intellectual disability. DSM-IV-TR used overlapping bands (mild from 50 or 55 to about 70, moderate from 35 or 40 to 50 or 55), and DSM-5 (2013) now grades severity by adaptive functioning, not IQ, because support needs depend on daily skills. The old educational labels follow the same bands: mild was 'educable' (academic skills to about sixth grade), moderate 'trainable' (self-care and simple supervised work, academics to about second grade), and severe and profound needed extensive support. So if Meera, aged 14, scores 48 on a standardised test and has matching adaptive deficits, she is in the moderate band: she can learn self-care and simple routines but needs supervised support. Option b is right because 48 lies inside 35 to 49. Exam trap: 48 is only two points below 50, so candidates who misremember the bands, or who use the DSM-IV-TR overlap, choose mild; on the ICD-10 and NIMH bands mild starts at 50.", "traps": {"a": "Borderline intellectual functioning (IQ about 70 to 84) is below average but is not intellectual disability; 48 is far below it.", "c": "Severe intellectual disability is IQ 20 to 34 on the ICD-10 and NIMH bands, with very limited speech and lifelong close support; 48 is above this range.", "d": "Mild intellectual disability is IQ 50 to 69 (the old 'educable' group); 48 sits just below its lower limit, which is the trap."}}, {"q": "Who among the following is credited with the development of Logotherapy?", "concept": "The answer is c: logotherapy was developed by Viktor Frankl. Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997) was a Viennese psychiatrist and neurologist whose logotherapy (from the Greek logos, 'meaning') is often called the third Viennese school of psychotherapy, after Sigmund Freud's (1900) psychoanalysis with its will to pleasure and Alfred Adler's (1912) individual psychology with its will to power. Frankl held that the primary human motive is the will to meaning. When it is frustrated, a person falls into an existential vacuum (boredom, emptiness) or a noogenic neurosis, a neurosis arising from the spiritual dimension rather than from conflict between drives. His Man's Search for Meaning (1946) drew on his survival of Nazi concentration camps, where he saw that prisoners who kept a purpose were more likely to endure. Meaning can be found in three ways: by creating a work or doing a deed, by experiencing something or loving someone, and by the attitude taken to unavoidable suffering. Two logotherapy techniques are well known: paradoxical intention (deliberately wishing for the feared symptom, such as trying to stay awake to beat insomnia) and dereflection (turning attention away from oneself towards a task or another person). If Arjun, a retired teacher, feels his life is pointless, a logotherapist would help him find meaning in mentoring others. Option c is right because Frankl coined the term and founded logotherapy. Exam trap: Rollo May (1950) is also an existential therapist, so d is the near miss; but May wrote on anxiety, will and love and founded no school called logotherapy.", "traps": {"a": "Fritz Perls (Gestalt Therapy, 1951, with Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman) founded Gestalt therapy: here-and-now awareness, the empty chair and unfinished business, not the search for meaning.", "b": "Eric Berne (Games People Play, 1964) founded transactional analysis: Parent, Adult and Child ego states, transactions, games and life scripts.", "d": "Rollo May (The Meaning of Anxiety, 1950; Love and Will, 1969) brought existential psychology to America and separated normal from neurotic anxiety, but he did not found logotherapy."}}, {"q": "Who founded the first psychology laboratory?", "concept": "The answer is b: among the options given, William James is credited with the first psychology laboratory, the small demonstration laboratory he set up at Harvard in 1875. William James (1842 to 1910) was the leading American pioneer of psychology and the father of functionalism; his Principles of Psychology (1890) defined the field in the United States. In 1875 he was given a room at Harvard with apparatus for showing students experiments on sensation and physiology. It was used for teaching, not for a programme of original research. The usual answer to 'who founded the first psychology laboratory' is Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who opened the first formal research laboratory at the University of Leipzig, trained students such as Edward Titchener (1898) and James McKeen Cattell (1890), and studied sensation and reaction time with controlled introspection. In America, G. Stanley Hall (1883) opened the first research laboratory at Johns Hopkins University. So the sequence to remember is: James's Harvard demonstration room (1875), Wundt's Leipzig research laboratory (1879), Hall's Johns Hopkins laboratory (1883) and, in India, Narendra Nath Sengupta's laboratory at Calcutta University (1915). If Riya sees Wundt among the options she should choose him; here he is missing, so James wins as the only laboratory founder offered. Option b is right because James's 1875 room is the earliest psychology laboratory among the four names. Exam trap: Ivan Pavlov (1897) ran a famous laboratory, so a tempts; but it was a physiology laboratory studying digestion and conditioning, and it came later. Key note: the standard answer, Wilhelm Wundt (Leipzig, 1879, the first formal research laboratory), is not among the options. William James set up a small demonstration laboratory at Harvard in 1875, which is why he is the answer here; b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936) ran a physiology laboratory in St Petersburg from the 1890s, studying digestion and then classical conditioning; it was not a psychology laboratory and came after 1875.", "c": "Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis in his consulting room from clinical cases and free association; he never founded an experimental laboratory.", "d": "Carl Jung used word-association tests clinically at the Burghölzli hospital in Zurich, but he did not found the first psychology laboratory."}}, {"q": "The structuralist primarily used the method of research called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the structuralists relied mainly on introspection, the trained, systematic observation of one's own conscious experience. Structuralism was the school of Edward Titchener (1898, when he named it), an Englishman trained by Wilhelm Wundt (1879) at Leipzig who taught at Cornell University. Its aim was to break consciousness into its basic elements, as a chemist breaks a compound into elements: sensations, images and feelings, each described by attributes such as quality, intensity, duration and clearness. The method was introspection by highly trained observers, who reported the raw elements of an experience under controlled conditions. They had to avoid the stimulus error: naming the object (an apple) instead of describing the sensations (red, round, smooth). Wundt had used a stricter, more limited experimental self-observation of immediate experience, and his own system is better called voluntarism; Titchener stretched introspection much further. Introspection declined because trained observers in different laboratories could not agree and no one could check their reports, as the imageless thought controversy with the Würzburg school showed. The functionalists, William James (1890) and John Dewey (1896), asked what consciousness is for, and John B. Watson (1913) rejected introspection altogether. For example, a trained observer like Meera, tasting lemonade, would report 'sour, cold, moderately intense, fading' rather than 'it is lemonade'. Option c is right because introspection was the defining method of structuralism. Exam trap: free association also looks inward, so a tempts; but free association is Sigmund Freud's clinical method of the 1890s for reaching the unconscious, while introspection describes conscious experience.", "traps": {"a": "Free association is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic method of saying whatever comes to mind to reach unconscious material; the structuralists studied only conscious experience in the laboratory.", "b": "Surveys collect self-reports from many people by questionnaire or interview; they belong to later social and applied research, not to the laboratory analysis of mental elements.", "d": "Observation of behaviour is the method of behaviourists and naturalistic researchers; the structuralists wanted the inner elements of consciousness, which only the experiencer can report."}}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses, caused by long-term abuse of alcohol, often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "concept": "The answer is d: Korsakoff's syndrome, a result of long-term alcohol misuse, often brings profound retrograde as well as anterograde amnesia. Sergei Korsakoff (1887), a Russian neuropsychiatrist, described patients, mostly heavy drinkers, who could not lay down new memories and had lost large stretches of their past. The cause is deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1): alcohol supplies calories without vitamins and impairs thiamine absorption, and neurons need thiamine to use glucose. It usually follows Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke (1881), an acute emergency with confusion, ataxia and ophthalmoplegia (paralysed eye movements); untreated survivors settle into Korsakoff's amnesia, and the continuum is called Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial (mediodorsal) thalamus, so it is a diencephalic amnesia. The picture is severe anterograde amnesia, temporally graded retrograde amnesia (older memories are better kept than recent ones, and the loss may reach back decades), apathy, poor insight and confabulation: plausible but false stories that fill memory gaps, told sincerely. Short-term memory span and procedural learning are relatively spared, and early thiamine injections can halt the progression. For example, Arjun, aged 55 with twenty years of heavy drinking, cannot recall this morning's visitors, believes it is still years earlier and confidently describes a trip he never took. Option d is right because Korsakoff's syndrome is the alcohol-linked amnesic disorder with profound retrograde loss. Exam trap: Alzheimer's disease also causes severe amnesia, so a tempts; but it is not caused by alcohol, and it begins with loss of recent episodic memory before spreading to language and other cognition.", "traps": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease (Alois Alzheimer, 1906) is a neurodegenerative dementia with amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles that starts with loss of recent memory; it is not caused by alcohol.", "b": "Parkinson's disease is loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra, causing resting tremor, rigidity and slowed movement, with impaired procedural learning; it is not caused by alcohol and does not bring profound retrograde amnesia.", "c": "Huntington's disease is an inherited (autosomal dominant, CAG repeat) degeneration of the caudate and putamen, causing chorea, psychiatric change and dementia; alcohol plays no part."}}, {"q": "Who introduced Behaviourism?", "concept": "The answer is a: John B. Watson introduced behaviourism. John B. Watson (1913), in his paper 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', often called the behaviourist manifesto, declared that psychology should be a purely objective, experimental branch of natural science whose goal is the prediction and control of behaviour. He rejected introspection and consciousness as unscientific, because no one else can check them, and studied only observable stimuli and responses. With Rosalie Rayner (1920) he conditioned fear in an 11-month-old boy, Little Albert, by pairing a white rat with a loud noise, and the fear generalised to other furry objects. He also boasted that, given a dozen healthy infants, he could train any one of them to become any type of specialist regardless of talent or ancestry, a statement of extreme environmentalism. Behaviourism built on the conditioning work of Ivan Pavlov (1897) and Edward Thorndike (1898), and it was carried forward by the neobehaviourists Clark Hull (1943), Edward Tolman (1932) and B. F. Skinner (1938), whose radical behaviourism studied operant conditioning. For example, if Riya develops a fear of dogs after one bit her, a Watsonian explanation is classical conditioning, with no reference to her inner thoughts. Option a is right because Watson founded and named behaviourism. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (1879) is offered because he founded experimental psychology, and candidates equate 'founder of the laboratory' with 'founder of every school'; but behaviourism began as a revolt against the introspective psychology of Wundt and Edward Titchener (1898).", "traps": {"b": "Wilhelm Wundt (the paper writes 'William') founded the first psychology research laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 and used introspection, the very method Watson attacked.", "c": "Lev Vygotsky built the sociocultural theory of development (zone of proximal development, inner speech); he worked on mind and culture, not stimulus-response behaviourism.", "d": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon (1905), built the first practical intelligence test; he founded mental testing, not behaviourism."}}, {"q": "Which of the following cells produce myelin in the CNS?", "concept": "The answer is d: oligodendrocytes produce myelin in the central nervous system (CNS). Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped around axons in many layers. It is interrupted at gaps called nodes of Ranvier, so the action potential jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction), which makes conduction much faster and saves energy. Myelin is made not by neurons but by glial cells. In the CNS (brain and spinal cord), each oligodendrocyte sends out several arms and wraps segments of many different axons. In the peripheral nervous system (PNS), each Schwann cell wraps a single segment of one axon, and Schwann cells also guide the regrowth of cut peripheral nerves, one reason PNS damage can recover while CNS damage usually does not. The other glia have different jobs: astrocytes (the largest, star-shaped) help form the blood-brain barrier, buffer potassium and recycle glutamate; microglia (the smallest) are the brain's immune cells, clearing debris and pruning synapses; ependymal cells line the ventricles and help make cerebrospinal fluid. Disease follows the same split: multiple sclerosis destroys CNS myelin made by oligodendrocytes, while Guillain-Barre syndrome attacks PNS myelin made by Schwann cells. So when Meera's neurologist explains her multiple sclerosis, it is oligodendrocyte myelin that is being lost. Option d is right because oligodendrocytes are the myelin-forming cells of the CNS. Exam trap: Schwann cells also make myelin, so a is the near miss; the CNS versus PNS split is exactly what the stem tests, and swapping the two is the classic error.", "traps": {"a": "Schwann cells make myelin in the peripheral nervous system, one cell per axon segment; they are the PNS counterpart, not the CNS myelin makers.", "b": "Astrocytes are star-shaped CNS glia that help form the blood-brain barrier, buffer potassium and recycle glutamate; they do not make myelin.", "c": "Microglia are the CNS's resident immune cells (macrophages) that clear debris and prune synapses; they do not make myelin."}}, {"q": "As per the Temperament and Character Inventory an individual who scores high on Novelty seeking but low on both Harm avoidance and Reward dependence is likely to have:", "concept": "The answer is c: high novelty seeking with low harm avoidance and low reward dependence is the profile C. Robert Cloninger linked to antisocial personality. C. Robert Cloninger (1987) proposed a tridimensional model in which three heritable temperament dimensions each depend mainly on one transmitter system: novelty seeking (exploratory excitement, impulsiveness, quick temper; dopamine), harm avoidance (worry, fear of uncertainty, shyness, fatigability; serotonin) and reward dependence (sentimentality, attachment, dependence on approval; noradrenaline). He later added persistence and three character dimensions (self-directedness, cooperativeness, self-transcendence) in the Temperament and Character Inventory (1993). Crossing high and low on the three temperaments gives eight combinations, which he matched to personality types. Antisocial: high novelty seeking, low harm avoidance, low reward dependence. Histrionic: high novelty seeking, low harm avoidance, high reward dependence. Passive-aggressive: high on all three. Obsessional: low novelty seeking, high harm avoidance, low reward dependence. The logic is easy to see: the antisocial person chases thrills (high novelty seeking), feels little fear of punishment (low harm avoidance) and does not care about others' approval (low reward dependence). So if Kabir constantly seeks excitement, shows no anxiety about consequences and is indifferent to praise or disapproval, the model predicts antisocial traits. Option c is right because the stem gives the antisocial combination exactly. Exam trap: histrionic people are also impulsive thrill-seekers with little anxiety, so a is the near miss; the deciding dimension is reward dependence, which is high in histrionic personality (craving attention and approval) and low in antisocial personality.", "traps": {"a": "Histrionic personality in Cloninger's model is high novelty seeking and low harm avoidance but high reward dependence; the need for approval separates it from the antisocial profile.", "b": "Obsessional personality is nearly the reverse profile: low novelty seeking, high harm avoidance and low reward dependence (cautious, anxious, emotionally reserved).", "d": "Passive-aggressive personality is high on all three dimensions (novelty seeking, harm avoidance and reward dependence), an unstable mix of impulsiveness, worry and dependence."}}, {"q": "An oil painting itself can include all the following cues to depth perception except:", "concept": "The answer is b: an oil painting can contain every pictorial depth cue, but it cannot contain retinal disparity, which needs two eyes looking at a real three-dimensional scene. The brain builds depth from cues, and only two are binocular, needing both eyes. In retinal (binocular) disparity, the eyes, about 6 centimetres apart, receive slightly different images of a near object, and the nearer the object, the bigger the difference; the brain fuses the two images into the solid depth called stereopsis. In convergence, the eyes turn inward to fixate near objects. All the other cues are monocular, and most are pictorial cues, the tools painters have used since the Renaissance: linear perspective (parallel lines such as rail tracks converge towards the horizon), texture gradient (pebbles look smaller and denser with distance), relative size (of two similar objects, the smaller image seems farther), interposition (a partly hidden object is behind), shading, height in the visual field and aerial perspective (distant hills look hazy and bluish). A flat canvas sends both eyes essentially the same image, so it carries no disparity; that is why a painting looks flatter than a real scene, and why the stereoscope of Charles Wheatstone (1838) shows each eye a slightly different picture to create disparity artificially. If Riya paints a road shrinking towards a mountain, she can use perspective, texture and size, but her painting cannot give her viewer's two eyes different views. Option b is right because retinal disparity comes from the viewer's two eyes, not from anything that can be painted. Exam trap: linear perspective, texture gradient and relative size sound technical, but they are exactly the pictorial cues a painter draws; the one binocular cue is the exception.", "traps": {"a": "Linear perspective, parallel lines converging with distance, is a monocular pictorial cue that Renaissance painters used deliberately, so a painting can include it.", "c": "Texture gradient, surface texture growing finer and denser with distance (James J. Gibson, 1950), is a monocular pictorial cue that can be painted onto a canvas.", "d": "Relative image size, the smaller of two similar objects seeming farther away, is a monocular pictorial cue that any painter can use."}}, {"q": "Which of the following sequence is correct?", "concept": "The answer is c: Sigmund Freud's psychosexual stages run oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud (1905), in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, argued that the libido, the energy of the sexual instinct, centres on a different erogenous zone at each stage of childhood, and that too much or too little gratification at a stage leaves a fixation that shapes adult personality. The oral stage (birth to about 1 year or 18 months) centres on the mouth, sucking and biting; fixation is linked to dependence, smoking or sarcasm. The anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) centres on bowel control and toilet training; fixation gives the anal-retentive (orderly, stingy, stubborn) or anal-expulsive (messy, careless) character. The phallic stage (about 3 to 5 or 6 years) centres on the genitals and the Oedipus complex, resolved by identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego. The latency stage (about 6 to puberty) is a quiet time when sexual energy is diverted into school, friends and skills; Freud called it a period rather than a true stage. The genital stage (puberty onward) brings mature sexual interest in others. A memory aid: 'Old Age Pensioners Love Grapes'. For example, a 4-year-old like Kabir who announces he will marry his mother is in the phallic stage; at 9, absorbed in cricket and friends, he is in latency. Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps Freud's order. Exam trap: option a puts latency before phallic, because candidates remember latency as early and quiet; but latency follows the Oedipal crisis of the phallic stage.", "traps": {"a": "Oral, anal, latency, phallic, genital wrongly puts latency before the phallic stage; latency comes after the Oedipus complex is resolved, at about 6 years.", "b": "Anal, oral, phallic, latency, genital reverses the first two; the mouth is the first erogenous zone in infancy, and toilet training comes later.", "d": "Oral, phallic, anal, genital, latency scrambles the order and even puts latency after adult genital sexuality, which contradicts Freud's sequence."}}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is Wernicke area located in?", "concept": "The answer is c: Wernicke's area lies in the temporal lobe, in the posterior part of the left superior temporal gyrus, next to the primary auditory cortex. Carl Wernicke (1874), a German neurologist, described patients with damage here who spoke fluently, with normal rhythm and grammar, but whose speech made little sense (word salad and made-up words called neologisms) and who could not understand what was said to them. This is Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia. Its partner is Broca's area, in the left inferior frontal gyrus of the frontal lobe, described by Paul Broca (1861) from his patient 'Tan': damage there gives Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia, with slow, effortful, telegraphic speech but fairly good comprehension. The two areas are joined by the arcuate fasciculus, whose damage causes conduction aphasia (poor repetition), a core idea of Norman Geschwind's (1965) Wernicke-Geschwind model. So if Meera, after a stroke, chatters fluently but cannot follow instructions, suspect her left temporal lobe. Option c is right because Wernicke's area is temporal cortex. Exam trap: the area borders the parietal lobe, and the nearby angular gyrus (reading and writing) is parietal, so b tempts; and Broca's frontal location is often swapped in for a.", "traps": {"a": "The frontal lobe holds Broca's area (left inferior frontal gyrus, Paul Broca, 1861), the site of non-fluent expressive aphasia, not Wernicke's area.", "b": "The parietal lobe borders Wernicke's area and contains the angular gyrus (reading and writing) and the somatosensory cortex, but Wernicke's area itself is temporal.", "d": "The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex (V1) and visual association areas; it handles sight, not language comprehension."}}, {"q": "What is the full form of EEG?", "concept": "The answer is a: EEG stands for electroencephalography, the method of recording the brain's electrical activity through electrodes on the scalp. The word breaks into electro (electrical), encephalo (brain, from the Greek for 'in the head') and graphy (recording). Hans Berger (1929), a German psychiatrist, published the first human recordings, which he had begun in 1924, and described the alpha rhythm of relaxed wakefulness. The EEG picks up the summed activity of many cortical neurons firing together. It has excellent temporal resolution (milliseconds) but poor spatial resolution, and it is the standard test for epilepsy and for sleep staging, where beta, alpha, theta and delta waves mark different states. Look-alike terms follow the same pattern: ECG (heart), EMG (muscles) and EOG (eye movements). Option a is right because electroencephalography is the full form of EEG as a technique. Exam trap: b and c are invented look-alikes that echo angiography (imaging blood vessels) and ergography (recording muscular work), and the real near miss is d. Key note: EEG is used for both electroencephalography (the method of recording the brain's electrical activity through scalp electrodes, Hans Berger, 1924/1929) and electroencephalogram (the recording itself), so d is also a correct expansion; as the name of the technique, a stands.", "traps": {"b": "'Electroengiography' is not a real term; it looks like angiography, the X-ray imaging of blood vessels with contrast dye, which records no electrical activity.", "c": "'Electroergography' is not a real term; it echoes the ergograph, Angelo Mosso's instrument for recording muscular work and fatigue, while the true electrical record of muscle is electromyography (EMG).", "d": "Electroencephalogram is the trace produced by electroencephalography, so it is a genuine expansion of EEG, but it names the record rather than the technique."}}, {"q": "According to Piaget, children are able to grasp the concept of object permanence in the:", "concept": "The answer is c: in Jean Piaget's theory, object permanence is achieved during the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years). Object permanence is the understanding that objects go on existing when they can no longer be seen, heard or touched. Jean Piaget (1937), in The Construction of Reality in the Child, drew on observations of his own three children and traced the concept across the six sensorimotor substages. Before about 4 months a toy covered by a cloth seems to cease to exist, and the infant looks away. From about 4 to 8 months the infant retrieves a partly hidden toy. From about 8 to 12 months the infant searches for a fully hidden toy but makes the A-not-B error: after finding it several times under cloth A, the infant keeps searching at A even after watching it hidden under cloth B. From 12 to 18 months the infant follows visible moves but fails invisible ones, and by 18 to 24 months, with mental representation, object permanence is complete. Later research suggests Piaget underestimated infants: Renee Baillargeon (1987), using the violation-of-expectation (drawbridge) method, found that infants of 3.5 to 4.5 months look longer at events in which a hidden box seems to vanish. For example, when 9-month-old Arjun watches his rattle go under a pillow, he lifts the pillow to find it, something a 3-month-old would not do. Option c is right because object permanence is the landmark achievement of the sensorimotor stage. Exam trap: the preoperational stage follows next and is defined by symbols, so b tempts candidates who link mental representation with preoperational thought; but permanence is complete before the preoperational stage begins.", "traps": {"a": "The formal operational stage (about 11 or 12 onward) brings abstract, hypothetical and hypothetico-deductive reasoning; object permanence was mastered about a decade earlier.", "b": "The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) is marked by symbolic thought, egocentrism and failure of conservation; it begins after object permanence is complete.", "d": "The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) brings conservation, reversibility, seriation and classification, logical operations far beyond object permanence."}}]});
